// The Coach. Delivery course.
window.CURRICULUM=[{"strand": "Voice", "intro": "This strand trains the instrument beneath everything else: breath, articulation, tempo, silence, intonation and size. It is taught as a voice coach would teach a peer who already performs, mechanism and physiology first, then drilled repetition, then the real room. The aim is a voice that stays low, unhurried, exact and audible in a committee, at a lectern or on a ceremonial stage, when the stakes and the nerves are highest.", "units": [{"id": "voice-1", "title": "Breath and the Thought Group", "goal": "Take a low, silent breath at a planned boundary and carry a whole thought group on it, with the operative final clause as fully voiced as the first.", "teach": {"body": "Speech breathing is a different act from tidal breathing, and the difference is the management of recoil. After a large inhalation the lungs and chest wall want to spring back, generating more pressure beneath the vocal folds than conversational phonation requires. The inspiratory muscles therefore keep a braking hold on the early part of the exhalation, the checking action, and as lung volume falls below its resting level the abdominal wall takes over the work of expulsion. Singers know the coordination as appoggio. Hixon's kinematic studies of the chest wall established that ordinary speech is conducted largely in the middle range of lung volume rather than at its extremes. The practical consequence is that the most efficient breath for a long sentence is not the largest one. A maximal breath has to be held back; a residual one has to be squeezed out. Both are audible.\n\nThe inhalation itself should be quick, low and silent: lateral and posterior expansion at the lower ribs, the abdominal wall releasing forward, shoulders and clavicles uninvolved. An audible intake usually means a partly closed glottis or a constricted pharynx, and from the back of a lecture theatre it sounds like effort.\n\nThe phrase is the unit of planning. Linguists describe connected speech as delivered in intonation units, or thought groups, each typically carrying one new idea under a single contour. Elite speakers make the breath and the intonation unit coincide, so that the inhalation is absorbed into a boundary the listener already expects. A breath taken inside a noun phrase, between \"curriculum\" and \"framework\", is the one breath the room notices. It also discloses that the sentence was not planned, which in a syllabus committee is a small concession of authority.\n\nThe onset matters as much as the intake. A hard glottal attack on a vowel-initial word (\"Item four\", \"Our position\") gives a clipped, pressed start; a breathy onset sounds tentative. Aim for a balanced onset, airflow and fold closure beginning together, with no catch and no leak. Breathe, release the hold, and let the first word ride the start of the exhalation rather than arriving after a locked pause.\n\nPressure exposes all of this. Anxiety raises the breath into the upper chest, shortens the phrase and increases the rate, while a mind working ahead keeps adding clauses to a sentence already in flight. The result is the characteristic double failure of speakers who know too much: a long periodic sentence begun on half a breath, the final clause, usually the operative one, delivered on residual air, pressed and creaky, then a top-up gasp before a qualification that should never have been attached. On the podium the microphone amplifies the gasp; in committee the operative clause simply goes unheard.\n\nThe discipline is editorial as much as physical. Place the main verb early. Mark every breath in a prepared text before you speak it. In unscripted speech, treat the end of each clause as a refill point and use it, even when you do not yet need air. When a sentence threatens to outrun the breath, end it and begin another. A full stop costs nothing; a gasp costs the sentence, and the full stop is the sound of someone who finished thinking before he spoke.", "model": "Chair, members of the panel, | thank you. || I'll speak to the *assessment outline* only, | since the rationale was settled at our last meeting. || The working party has made *one* substantive change. | The externally set task now sits in *Semester Two*, | where the moderation data say it *belongs*. || Everything else in the outline | is *unchanged*. || I'll take questions on that change, | and then I'll ask the panel | to *endorse* the outline | for *consultation*.", "mistake": "Chair, members of the panel, thank you, I'll speak to the assessment outline only, since the rationale was settled last time, and the working party has made one substantive change, which is that the externally set task now sits in Semester Two, where the moderation data (breath) suggest it belongs, and everything else is unchanged, so I'll take questions and then ask the panel to endorse it for consultation.", "remember": ["Breathe low and silent before the first word.", "Breathe at thought-group boundaries, never inside a phrase.", "Size the breath to the phrase, not to capacity.", "Begin on a balanced onset: no catch, no leak.", "End the sentence before it outruns the breath."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-1-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "Low breath, checked release", "how": "Follow each instruction on the pattern shown. The in-breath is silent and lateral at the lower ribs; the out-breath is even to the last second, with no abdominal squeeze at the finish.", "items": ["Hands at the lower ribs, thumbs toward the spine. Breathe in silently until the ribs widen sideways and into your thumbs. Release on a sustained \"sss\" with the ribs held open, so the checking action, not collapse, governs the flow.", "Same breath, a third smaller. Release on \"fff\". Notice that the smaller breath needs almost no braking: this is the volume for a committee sentence.", "Same breath. Release on a hummed \"mmm\" at your habitual speaking pitch, intensity level from onset to offset, with no swell at the start and no fade at the end.", "Same breath. Release on \"vvv\" and stop at the first sign of abdominal squeeze. That point marks the end of your usable phrase; note how early it arrives.", "Same breath. Speak \"eight, eleven, eighteen, eighty\" at boardroom level, each vowel on a balanced onset: no glottal catch, no breathy leak.", "Same breath. Speak \"I move that the report be received\" on the first third of the exhalation, then let the ribs settle and the next breath fall in by recoil alone."], "pattern": [3, 1, 8, 2], "checks": ["Did the shoulders and clavicles stay completely still on every in-breath?", "Was every in-breath inaudible, including the last?", "Did intensity hold level to the final second, with no squeeze or fade?", "On the vowel-initial numbers, was each onset free of a click or a puff of air?", "Did the next breath arrive by recoil rather than being taken?"]}, {"id": "voice-1-d2", "type": "read", "title": "One thought group, one breath", "how": "Read each line aloud at boardroom level. Breathe only at the bars, and give the final stressed word the same tone and intensity as the first.", "items": ["The working party has reviewed | the draft assessment outline, | and recommends | that the *weighting* of the practical component | remain at *fifty per cent*.", "Before we turn to the substantive item, | I should note | that two members have declared | a *conflict of interest*, | and will leave the room | for the *vote*.", "Distinguished guests, | colleagues, | and families of our graduating students, | it is a privilege | to welcome you | to *this* evening.", "The question for this committee | is not whether the course is *rigorous*, | but whether it is *assessable* | at the standard the syllabus *claims*.", "The association finished the financial year | with a modest *surplus*, | which the board proposes | to hold in *reserve* | against the cost of the next *festival*.", "If the panel is minded to fund the project, | we would ask | that the grant be released | in *two* tranches, | the second | on delivery of the *interim report*."], "checks": ["Did every breath fall on a marked bar, and none between them?", "Was the final stressed word as full in tone as the first?", "Was every breath silent, including the ones taken quickly?", "Did any phrase end on pressed or creaky air?", "Did the first word after each breath begin without a glottal catch?"]}, {"id": "voice-1-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Lengthen the thought group", "how": "Say each line three times: first breathing at every bar, then at every second bar, then on a single breath taken low before the first word. The last word must keep full tone on all three.", "items": ["The syllabus, | the assessment outline, | the grade descriptions | and the support materials | will be released *together*.", "I thank the board | for its patience, | the auditor | for her diligence, | and the members | for their *trust*.", "The panel considered | artistic merit, | community reach, | financial viability | and the capacity of the applicant | to *deliver*.", "We have consulted | the teachers, | the universities, | the conservatorium | and the students | who will sit the *examination*.", "The motion before the council | is to adopt the revised fee schedule, | to note the treasurer's advice | and to refer the question of hardship | to the *finance committee*."], "checks": ["On the single-breath version, did the final word keep full tone with no creak?", "Did you take the single breath before the first word, not on it?", "Did the single-breath version stay at the same tempo, rather than rushing to finish?", "Could you still hear the thought-group boundaries in the version where you did not breathe at them?"]}, {"id": "voice-1-d4", "type": "recall", "title": "Phrase it live", "how": "Read the anchors, then speak the talk in order with the anchors hidden. Let each anchor become one or two thought groups, refill at every clause end, and end each sentence before it needs a second breath.", "items": ["Opening a SCSA review meeting / purpose of today / three questions for the panel / what we will not decide / how the record will be kept", "Opening a conference paper / the problem in one sentence / why it matters now / the evidence you will show / what you will ask of the room", "Welcome at a graduation ceremony / acknowledge Country and guests / what this cohort achieved / what the evening holds / a word to families", "Report to the festival board / the headline result / one material risk / one decision needed / the timeline", "A case to the principal / the request / the cost / the consequence of not acting / the date you need an answer"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did every breath fall at a clause or sentence boundary?", "Were there any audible gasps or mid-phrase top-ups?", "Did any sentence run on with \"and\" or \"which\" to postpone a breath?", "Did the final sentence finish with full voice and a clean stop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Take a ceremonial or conference opening and mark every breath with a bar, then edit the text until no thought group needs more than a comfortable breath. Deliver it to the back of the largest room available, breathing only at your marks, then deliver it again from memory and record both. Compare the recordings for any breath that moved, and for the tone of the final clause.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["In the first delivery, did every breath fall on a mark?", "From memory, did the breaths still fall at thought-group boundaries?", "Did any edit you made shorten a sentence rather than stretch a breath?", "Did the final clause carry as fully as the opening in both recordings?"]}}, {"id": "voice-2", "title": "Articulation and the Ending", "goal": "Finish every word that carries information, above all phrase endings, figures, names and contrasts, so that it survives distance, reverberation and amplification without sounding over-articulated.", "teach": {"body": "Intelligibility at distance is carried by consonants. Vowels hold most of the acoustic energy and comparatively little of the contrast that separates one word from its neighbours; consonants are brief, weak, and concentrated in the higher frequencies, which are the first to suffer from distance, reverberation, an indifferent public address system and an audience of a certain age. In a reverberant hall the decaying energy of each stressed vowel spills into the following segment and obscures it, which acousticians call overlap masking. What goes missing is precisely what carries the information: the final stop of \"forecast\", the nasal at the end of \"ten\", the difference between \"fifteen\" and \"fifty\".\n\nConnected speech compounds the loss. In Australian English, word-final /t/ is commonly glottalised or replaced by a glottal stop before a consonant or a pause; final stops are often unreleased; and the ordinary processes of assimilation and elision turn \"that particular\" into \"tha' p'ticular\", \"next decision\" into \"nex decision\" and \"seven per cent\" into \"sevem per cent\". None of this is careless. It is efficient articulation for a listener a metre away who shares your context. A panel of twelve around a boardroom table, a conference audience listening through loudspeakers or a ceremony in a sports hall does not share it, and a listener cannot ask you to repeat a figure.\n\nThe research on clear speech, the register speakers adopt spontaneously for listeners who are struggling, describes the remedy: a slightly slower rate, more and longer pauses, fuller release of word-final stops, less vowel reduction, and greater intensity in consonants relative to vowels. Note what is absent from that list: loudness for its own sake, and the uniform over-articulation that makes a speaker sound like an elocution lesson. Among articulate people over-precision reads as performance. The skill is selective precision.\n\nSpend it in three places. First, the end of the intonation unit, where the nucleus usually falls and where the voice is most inclined to fade. Second, anything a listener must record or act on: figures, dates, clause numbers, proper names, titles of works, the wording of a motion. Third, contrast, where meaning hangs on a consonant or a stress pattern: \"can\" against \"can't\", \"affect\" against \"effect\", \"seventy\" against \"seventeen\", \"the amended motion\" against \"the motion\".\n\nMechanically, give the vowels jaw space and let the tongue tip do the consonant work; a fixed jaw forces the tongue to compensate and blurs both. Release final plosives audibly into the following silence, a small aspiration rather than an explosion. Keep alveolar contacts exact, since /t d n s z l/ carry a disproportionate share of English contrasts. A cut-off places the beat as precisely as an attack, and the final consonant is the cut-off of a word.\n\nUnder pressure articulation fails predictably. Rate rises and the tongue undershoots its targets, so clusters collapse. Effort migrates to volume, which spreads the vowels and smears the edges further. Fatigue late in a long meeting produces lip and tongue laxity just as the decisions are taken. And a speaker reading a prepared text articulates the prose he rehearsed and drops the figures he did not. The correction is the same in every case: slow by a fraction, finish the word, and let the pause after it carry the information home.", "model": "The revised weighting | is *thirty* per cent for the written examination, | and *seventy* for the practical. || That is *seventy*, | not seventeen. || Submissions close on *Friday* the *eighth* of *August*, | at *five* p.m., | and they go to the *Executive Officer*, | not to the *chair*. || Clause *four point two* | is *unchanged*. || Clause *four point three* | is *deleted*.", "mistake": "The revised weighting's thirty per cent for the written exam and seventy for the practical, submissions close Friday the eigh' of August at five p.m. and go to the Executive Officer rather than the chair, an' clause four point two's unchanged and four point three's deleted.", "remember": ["Finish the last word of every thought group.", "Land every figure, date, name and clause number.", "Release final stops into the silence, without explosion.", "Be selectively precise; uniform precision sounds performed.", "Slow by a fraction before you add volume."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-2-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Release the ending", "how": "Say each item three times: conversationally, then releasing every final consonant into silence, then at conference-hall level keeping that release. Vowel length must not change between versions; only the edges sharpen.", "items": ["That's not what the report says.", "Seventeen, not seventy.", "Thirteen per cent, not thirty.", "The amended motion.", "We can't support it.", "Clause eight, subclause two.", "Next financial year.", "The draft was circulated last week."], "checks": ["Was each final /t/ or /d/ an audible release rather than a glottal stop?", "Did the hall version keep the endings exactly as clean as the quiet one?", "Did the vowels stay the same length while the consonants sharpened?", "In \"can't\", was the /t/ audible enough to rule out \"can\"?"]}, {"id": "voice-2-d2", "type": "read", "title": "Figures, names, clauses", "how": "Read each line at boardroom level. Every stressed word must finish cleanly, because the listener has to record it or act on it.", "items": ["The meeting is adjourned | to *Tuesday* the *twenty-first*, | at *four-thirty*, | in the *Council Chamber*.", "The grant sought is *forty-eight thousand* dollars, | over *two* years, | not *eighty-four*.", "I'd like to acknowledge | Professor *Margaret Hart*, | Dean of the *Faculty of Arts*.", "Item *nine*, | the *auditor's* report, | appears at page *thirty-six* of the papers.", "The working party recommends | *option B*, | at an annual cost of *six point two* per cent | of the operating *budget*.", "Our next speaker is | Associate Professor *Daniel Okafor*, | whose paper is titled | *Assessment Without Examinations*.", "Nominations close at *noon* on the *thirty-first*, | and must be *seconded* in *writing*."], "checks": ["Would a minute-taker at the far end have recorded every figure correctly?", "Did every proper name finish, including its last consonant?", "Did \"-teen\" and \"-ty\" differ in both stress and final consonant?", "Did the final word of each line release rather than fade?"]}, {"id": "voice-2-d3", "type": "swap", "title": "Glottal to clean", "how": "Say the weak version as written, the way it emerges at speed, then the strong version with the endings finished. Keep the strong version at your normal rate: the change is in the edges, not the tempo.", "items": ["Tha' wasn' the board's in'ention => That was *not* the board's *intention*.", "We'll circula' the draf' nex week => We'll circulate the *draft* | *next* week.", "The fundin' was cu' by a third => The *funding* was *cut* | by a *third*.", "I can' accep' tha' amendmen' => I *can't* accept that *amendment*.", "The panel didn' fin' the evidence persuasive => The panel did *not* find the evidence *persuasive*.", "Tha' concludes the formal par' of the evenin' => That concludes the *formal* part of the *evening*."], "checks": ["Could you name exactly which consonants the weak version lost?", "Did the strong version stay at speaking rate, without sounding staged?", "Did the final word of each strong line release cleanly into silence?", "Did the strong version avoid over-articulating unstressed words?"]}, {"id": "voice-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Clean over noise", "how": "Imagine the ambient noise of a conference foyer or a hall settling after applause. Give the announcement once, at a slightly slower rate, with every figure, name and final word finished, then stop.", "items": ["Announce to two hundred delegates a change of room, time and session number for the afternoon keynote.", "Tell a board the three figures that matter in this year's accounts.", "Tell a SCSA panel which clause you propose to amend, and how it will read.", "Announce the recipient, the title and the donor of a named award at a ceremony.", "Tell a funding panel the amount sought, the period and the principal deliverable.", "Tell a committee the date, time and place of the reconvened meeting, and the single item on it."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 10, "structure": "ONE", "checks": ["Did every figure and proper name finish cleanly?", "Did you say it once, without restating any detail?", "Did the last word of each sentence carry rather than fade?", "Did you stop as soon as the information was complete?"]}], "apply": {"source": "direction", "task": "Take a one-line procedural announcement for a large room and deliver it once over imagined foyer noise, finishing every figure, name and final consonant at a slightly slower rate, then stop. Record it, and transcribe it from a single playback at normal speed: any word you have to replay is a consonant you lost.", "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did you say it once, without repeating yourself?", "Did the transcription from a single playback match your text exactly?", "Were figures and names finished without the surrounding words sounding over-precise?", "Did the final word release into silence rather than drop?"]}}, {"id": "voice-3", "title": "Tempo and the Clock", "goal": "Set a deliberate tempo for each room, mark the operative sentence by slowing and framing it, and fit what you say to the time allotted without accelerating to finish.", "teach": {"body": "Rate has two components that behave differently. Articulation rate is the speed of the syllables themselves; speaking rate is the overall figure once pauses are included. Listeners attribute calm and authority far more to the distribution of silence than to syllable speed. Speakers told to slow down tend to stretch the syllables, which produces a drawl that a senior room hears as condescension or fatigue. The expert lever is pause time: articulation at a natural, even slightly brisk speed, with silence placed at the boundaries of thought groups and lengthened at the boundaries of ideas.\n\nPauses do cognitive work for both parties. Goldman-Eisler's studies of spontaneous speech showed that hesitation pauses are where planning happens, and that they tend to fall before words of high information and low predictability. A speaker who allows himself those pauses, rather than covering them with sound or rushing through them, gives the room the processing time he is taking himself. In an academic committee, where members are checking what you say against a paper in front of them, that time is not a courtesy. It is a condition of being understood.\n\nTempo should be chosen for the room the way a tune is counted off, not discovered once under way. As working ranges rather than research norms: a committee table carries comfortably somewhere around 140 to 165 words per minute; a conference room on amplification, a ceremony or a large hall wants something nearer 120 to 145, because reverberation lengthens every syllable's tail and distance strips away the visual cues of the face; a formal citation or a eulogy can go slower still. These are starting tempi. The meter will show you where you are.\n\nThe deeper skill is variation. A change in rate signals importance more reliably than any absolute rate. The advocate's technique for the sentence the bench must remember is to pause before it, take it at a markedly slower tempo with its stressed syllables given full length, pause after it, and then return to the prevailing tempo. Musicians call it rubato: time borrowed and repaid. A recommendation, a motion, a figure or the name of an honouree delivered at the speed of the preamble will be heard as preamble.\n\nUnder pressure tempo drifts in one direction. Adrenaline accelerates the internal clock, so a rushed delivery feels normal from the inside, exactly as an ensemble rushes in performance without perceiving it. A fast associative mind compounds this: the next thought arrives before the present one is complete, and speech accelerates to catch it. The audible signs are compressed pauses, thought groups run together, and a final clause thrown away. The remedy is an external reference, which is what the meter is for, and the habit of completing the sentence before admitting the next idea.\n\nThe clock is arithmetic, and seniority shows in respecting it. At 140 words per minute, a three-minute conference introduction is a little over four hundred words, names included. A ten-minute agenda slot is rarely ten minutes of speaking: allow perhaps a third for presentation and leave the remainder to the committee. Write to the time, cut before you stand, and never recover a deficit by accelerating through the conclusion, which is the part the room came for.", "model": "Item four is the *festival venue* for next year. || The board has two options before it. | The *first* is to remain at the current venue, | at a cost increase of *eleven* per cent. | The *second* is to move to the university concert hall, | which is cheaper, | but available on only *one* of our two weekends. || || The executive *recommends* | that we *remain*. || || I'll set out the reasons briefly, | and then open it to the board.", "mistake": "Item four is next year's festival venue, and just for context, the current venue has been with us for some years and the relationship's been good, though costs have moved, so the board has two options: either we stay at an eleven per cent increase or we move to the university concert hall, which is cheaper but only available on one of our two weekends, and the executive recommends that we remain, and I'll go through the reasons and then open it up.", "remember": ["Slow with silence, not with stretched syllables.", "Count off the room's tempo before the first word.", "Take the operative sentence slower, framed by silence.", "Budget roughly 140 words to the minute.", "Never recover time by rushing the conclusion."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-3-d1", "type": "pace", "title": "Committee table tempo", "how": "Read each item as if at a committee or board table, keeping the meter inside the range. Get there by placing pauses at boundaries, not by stretching words.", "items": ["Thank you, Chair. I'll be brief. The working party met three times this term and has reached agreement on all but one of the matters referred to it. That matter is the weighting of the externally set task, and I'll come to it last.", "The papers for this meeting include the draft outline, the consultation summary and a note from the moderation team. Members will have seen that the consultation was broadly supportive, with reservations concentrated in two areas.", "I propose that we take items five and six together, since they turn on the same question. If there is no objection, I'll ask the Executive Officer to summarise both.", "The financial position is sound. Income was slightly ahead of budget, expenditure slightly behind, and the reserve now sits at a level the auditor describes as prudent. There is one matter for decision.", "Before we close, I'd like to record the committee's thanks to the outgoing deputy chair, whose judgement over six years has spared this body more than one avoidable mistake."], "targets": {"wpm": [140, 165], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did the meter stay in range from the first sentence to the last?", "Did you reach the range by pausing rather than by stretching words?", "Were pauses at full stops clearly longer than pauses at commas?", "Did the final sentence of each item hold the tempo of the first?"]}, {"id": "voice-3-d2", "type": "pace", "title": "Ceremony and hall tempo", "how": "Read each item as if to a full hall or an amplified conference room, aimed at the back wall, inside the slower range. Keep the syllables natural and let the boundaries take the extra time.", "items": ["Principal, members of the school board, distinguished guests and families. Good evening, and welcome to this ceremony.", "It is my privilege to open the third session of this conference, on assessment in the performing arts. Our first speaker needs no introduction in this room, which is fortunate, because she has asked me to keep it short.", "This award recognises sustained contribution to music education in Western Australia. Its recipient this year has taught, conducted, examined and argued for the discipline in this state for more than thirty years.", "We gather this afternoon to remember a colleague whose influence on this community will outlast every one of us in the room.", "The festival this year brought more than forty ensembles to Perth across three days. None of it happens without the volunteers, and I would ask you to thank them now."], "targets": {"wpm": [120, 145], "fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the meter stay inside the hall range throughout?", "Did it sound unhurried rather than deliberately slowed?", "Were the syllables at conversational speed, with the time taken in the pauses?", "Did the final sentence of each item end on a clean stop rather than trail?"]}, {"id": "voice-3-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Slow the operative sentence", "how": "Say each item twice. First at one even tempo throughout; then pause at the double bar, take the final sentence markedly slower with full-length stressed syllables, pause after it, and imagine resuming at the original tempo.", "items": ["We have reviewed the evidence from the trial schools, the moderation data and the teacher survey. || We do not recommend *proceeding* | in *twenty twenty-eight*.", "The panel considered fourteen applications against the published criteria. || *Three* | have been *funded*.", "I have listened carefully to every view expressed this evening. || My advice to the council | is that we *defer* the decision | to the *March* meeting.", "The association has had a demanding year, and the accounts reflect it. || We have finished the year | in *surplus*.", "She has examined, conducted, mentored and argued for this discipline for three decades. || Please welcome | Professor *Anne Rowe*.", "The draft has been through four rounds of consultation. || This is the *final* opportunity | to *amend* it."], "checks": ["On the second version, was the operative sentence audibly slower than the preamble?", "Did you hold silence both before and after it?", "Did the slowness come from longer stressed syllables and pauses, not a drawl on every word?", "Did the preamble keep its original tempo rather than slowing in anticipation?"]}, {"id": "voice-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Speak to the clock", "how": "Speak for exactly the time given at committee tempo, using the structure shown, and slow and frame the now-what sentence. Finish on time: not early by rushing, not late by trailing.", "items": ["The outcome of the syllabus consultation, for a SCSA reference group.", "Why the festival's insurance costs have risen, for the association board.", "A proposed change to the moderation process, for a university academic committee.", "The case for a dedicated performance space, for the principal and the business manager.", "The findings of a program review, as the opening of a conference session.", "A shortfall in the year's budget, for the school board."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 15, "structure": "WSN", "targets": {"wpm": [135, 160], "fillers": 2, "pauses": 5}, "checks": ["Did you finish within three seconds of the time?", "Was the now-what sentence noticeably slower, with silence either side?", "Did the meter stay in range in the final fifteen seconds?", "Did you stop after the now-what rather than add a further sentence?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Take a ceremonial or conference passage and, before speaking, identify its single operative sentence and check its length against the clock at hall tempo. Deliver it inside the time, slowing and framing that sentence only. Then deliver it a second time with exactly ten seconds removed, achieved by cutting text rather than by speaking faster.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["Did the meter stay in the hall range on both deliveries?", "Was the operative sentence clearly slower, with silence either side, both times?", "Did the second delivery save its ten seconds by cutting rather than accelerating?", "Did the conclusion hold its tempo in both?"]}}, {"id": "voice-4", "title": "Silence and the Held Beat", "goal": "Hold the full stop, place an anticipatory pause before the word that matters, and hold a question to the floor in silence until the room answers.", "teach": {"body": "A pause is a rest with a value, and speakers who command senior rooms treat it as notated. It does four distinct jobs. The boundary pause marks the end of a thought group or sentence and gives the listener time to integrate what has just been said: roughly a beat at a full stop, longer at the end of a stage of an argument. The anticipatory pause, placed immediately before a key word, creates expectation and confers emphasis without any change of volume: \"The panel's recommendation is | not to fund.\" The consolidating pause, after a decision, a figure or a name, lets it land before anything competes with it. And the waiting pause, before a speech begins or after a question to the floor, transfers the obligation to speak to the room.\n\nConversation analysis explains why the last is so effective. In the account of turn-taking set out by Sacks, Schegloff and Jefferson, turns change hands at transition-relevance places, points at which a turn could be complete, and silence at such a point puts pressure on others to take the turn. A chair who asks \"Is there any further discussion?\" and then holds silence is using that pressure deliberately. One who fills the gap with \"or, if not, we can move on\" has released it, and has usually signalled the answer he would prefer.\n\nFilled pauses are the counterpart. \"Um\" and \"uh\" are not random noise: Clark and Fox Tree argued that speakers use them as signals, \"uh\" announcing a minor delay and \"um\" a longer one, and that among their functions is holding the floor while planning. In a room of articulate people that signal is costly. It advertises that planning is still under way, and in an adversarial room it invites interruption at the moment you are most exposed. The connective filler, the \"and\" or \"so\" that welds sentences together, does the same work less visibly: it avoids the full stop because the full stop feels like an invitation to be interrupted.\n\nSilence holds the floor perfectly well on three conditions. The body stays still. The gaze stays on the room, or on the person addressed, rather than dropping to the papers or lifting to the ceiling, both of which read as retrieval. And the pitch before the pause makes clear whether you have finished: a level or slightly rising tone on the last word signals continuation, so the room waits; a fall signals completion, so the room may take the turn. Accomplished chairs exploit that distinction constantly, holding the floor through a long pause with a level tone and surrendering it with a fall.\n\nThe perceptual difficulty is that silence is far longer from the inside than from the outside, and anxiety lengthens it further. A two-second pause that feels perilous at the lectern barely registers with a conference audience. The calibration must be external: count beats, and trust the count over the sensation.\n\nUnder pressure pauses fail in characteristic ways. They migrate from before key words to before trivial ones, which is planning rather than emphasis. They shorten at sentence ends and collapse into connectives. They are ended prematurely by a filler, a self-correction or a rephrasing of the question just asked. And they are spent looking for approval. The face in a held silence should be neutral and attentive: the face you give a section settling before a downbeat.", "model": "The final award this evening | is the one this association guards most *closely*. || It is given not for a single performance, | but for a *body of work*. || This year | it goes to a teacher who has built three ensembles from *nothing*, | sent her students to every conservatorium in the *country*, | and never once asked for *credit*. || || The award for *distinguished service* to jazz education || goes to || *Joan Pemberton*.", "mistake": "The final award this evening is the one the association guards most closely. It's given not for a single performance but for a body of work, and this year it goes to, ah, a teacher who's built three ensembles from nothing, and I should say she'd want me to mention the staff who helped, and who's sent her students to just about every conservatorium in the country, so the award for distinguished service to jazz education goes to Joan Pemberton.", "remember": ["Hold the full stop for a beat, and stay still.", "Pause before the word that must be heard.", "After a question to the floor, count five silently.", "Keep your gaze on the room through every silence.", "Replace each filler with a closed mouth and a breath."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-4-d1", "type": "hold", "title": "Hold the full stop", "how": "Say each line with a clean fall, then hold silence for the count shown, body still and eyes on an imagined committee. Take a quiet breath during the hold.", "items": ["The meeting is now open.", "That concludes the treasurer's report.", "The committee does not support the amendment.", "The motion is carried.", "That is the end of my submission.", "I declare this conference open."], "hold": 2, "checks": ["Did you hold the full count without any movement of the head or hands?", "Did any sound, breath noise or filler leak out at the end of the hold?", "Did your eyes stay on the imagined room rather than drop?", "Did each line end on a clean fall before the silence?"]}, {"id": "voice-4-d2", "type": "read", "title": "The anticipatory pause", "how": "Read each line, placing a clear pause at the bar immediately before the stressed word, with a level tone going into it. The pause must sound chosen, not searched for.", "items": ["The panel's recommendation | is *not* to fund.", "What this syllabus lacks | is *coherence*.", "The recipient of this year's medal | is *Dr Ruth Adeyemi*.", "The question is not whether we can afford it. | It is whether we can afford | *not* to.", "After two years of consultation, | the authority has decided | to *withdraw* the course.", "The single change that would make the difference | is *time*."], "checks": ["Was there an unmistakable silence before each stressed word?", "Did the pitch stay level going into the pause, so it sounded unfinished?", "Did the stressed word arrive firmly, without a rush after the silence?", "Did your gaze hold steady through each pause?"]}, {"id": "voice-4-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Hold the floor open", "how": "Ask each question as a chair to the floor, ending on a fall, then hold silence for the full count. Do not answer it, rephrase it, soften it or move on.", "items": ["Is there any further discussion on the motion?", "Does any member wish to speak against?", "Are there questions for the presenter?", "Is the board ready to vote?", "Professor Hart, would you like to respond?", "Is there anything the panel needs from me before it deliberates?"], "hold": 5, "checks": ["Did you hold the full five seconds without speaking?", "Did you stay still with a neutral, attentive face?", "Did you resist rephrasing, softening or answering your own question?", "Did each question end on a fall, as an invitation from the chair rather than a plea?"]}, {"id": "voice-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Silence instead of fillers", "how": "Speak on the topic using the structure shown. Whenever you feel a filler or a connective \"and\" coming, close your mouth, take a breath and pause instead; let the meter count pauses, not ums.", "items": ["Why ensemble performance belongs in the senior secondary curriculum.", "What an examining panel should listen for in a performance assessment.", "Why adjudication at a schools' festival should be formative rather than ranked.", "What makes a committee decision defensible.", "The case for releasing exemplars with a new syllabus.", "Why an arts funding panel should weight community reach."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 0, "pauses": 6}, "checks": ["Did the filler count stay at zero?", "Did you pause at least six times, including once before your key word?", "Did each pause stay still and silent, with eyes up?", "Did you end on the restated point, then stop without a connective?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "A conference plenary has run late and the room is restless and talking when you rise to introduce the next speaker. Say aloud how you will take the room without asking for it, then do it: walk on, stand, wait in stillness, and give your opening sentence only when the room is quiet, with an anticipatory pause before the speaker's name.", "seconds": 45, "checks": ["Did you avoid \"shh\", \"okay\", \"right\" or any request for quiet?", "Did you stay still and neutral for the whole wait?", "Did your first sentence begin only after the room had settled?", "Was there an audible anticipatory pause before the speaker's name?"]}}, {"id": "voice-5", "title": "Intonation and the Downward Close", "goal": "End statements on a clean fall, use the fall-rise and pitch reset deliberately, and keep rising tones for genuine questions and unfinished lists.", "teach": {"body": "English intonation is organised in intonation units, each with a nucleus, or tonic syllable: the most prominent syllable, usually on the last lexical word carrying new information, where the main pitch movement occurs. The tone chosen on that nucleus carries meaning the words do not. In the British descriptive tradition the inventory is usually given as fall, rise, fall-rise, rise-fall and level. A fall signals completion and assertion: this is so, and this is finished. A rise signals openness: a question, an incomplete list, an appeal for confirmation. A fall-rise signals reservation or implication, and it is among the most useful tones in committee: \"The principle is sound\", with a fall-rise on \"principle\", tells the room that something else is not, without your having to say so.\n\nTwo further features shape how authority sounds. Declination is the gradual lowering of pitch across an utterance; a statement that declines and ends on a clear fall sounds settled. Pitch reset, a return to a higher starting pitch at the start of a new topic, is how a speaker marks a paragraph aloud; skilled chairs and broadcasters use it to signal an agenda transition without announcing one. Key, the overall height of a unit relative to its neighbours, does contrastive work: a high-key \"not\" set against a mid-key surround makes a correction audible with no extra volume.\n\nThe high rising terminal on declaratives is well documented in Australian English, where it has long functioned conversationally as a check on the listener's engagement rather than as a marker of doubt. In a board, a SCSA panel or the principal's office, however, it is heard as tentativeness, and on a recommendation it invites negotiation: \"The working party recommends option B?\" sounds like a proposal awaiting permission. Related habits are the disguised imperative (\"Could we perhaps move to item six?\") and the hedge that drags a contour upward: \"sort of\", \"I think\", \"just\". Each can be the right choice for warmth or diplomacy. Each should be a choice, and under pressure the default should be a fall.\n\nGenuine questions should sound like questions. Polar questions typically rise; questions opening with a wh-word typically fall, and a chair's \"Any further discussion?\" on a fall is an invitation from the person who holds the floor rather than a hope. Lists rise on each non-final item and fall on the last, which tells the room the list is complete.\n\nAnxiety distorts intonation in specific ways. Laryngeal tension raises habitual pitch and compresses range, so the voice sounds higher and flatter at once. Hedging lifts the endings. Then, overcorrecting, many speakers press the final syllable below their comfortable range into creaky voice, or vocal fry, which in a senior room sounds like weariness or dismissal rather than certainty. The fall that carries authority is a clean step down on the nucleus to a comfortable low note, still in modal register, followed by silence.\n\nHear it as harmonic resolution. The statement is a cadence and the nucleus is where it resolves; a cadence does not resolve below its key or louder than its phrase, it resolves cleanly and stops. The meter tracks end-of-phrase rise. On statements it should read flat or falling. Where it shows a rise, find the word that carried it and say the sentence again.", "model": "I'm asking for the Level 3 coordinator role | to be *advertised* this year. || The department now runs *eleven* ensembles | across *three* programs. | Coordination sits on a *point-three* allocation, | and the gap shows in the *timetable*, | the *budget*, | and *staff turnover*. || I've costed the role, | and the costing is in the *paper*. || I'd like a *decision* | before the staffing round *closes*.", "mistake": "I suppose what I'm asking is whether the Level 3 coordinator role could be advertised this year? The department's running eleven ensembles across three programs now, and coordination's on a point-three allocation, so it does show in the timetable and the budget and staff turnover. I've costed it in the paper, so ideally a decision before the staffing round closes?", "remember": ["Fall cleanly on the nucleus of every statement.", "Step down to a low note, never into fry.", "Use the fall-rise deliberately to signal reservation.", "Reset pitch upward to open a new topic.", "Rise only on genuine questions and unfinished lists."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-5-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Rise, then fall", "how": "Say each line three times: with a rising end, then with a clean fall on the nucleus, then with the fall followed by a held beat. On the last item add a fourth version with a fall-rise on \"principle\" and hear the reservation it implies.", "items": ["The working party recommends option B.", "The weighting will remain at fifty per cent.", "We are not in a position to support the amendment.", "I'll need the revised figures by Friday.", "That is the board's decision.", "The principle is sound."], "checks": ["Could you hear the rising version requesting permission?", "On the fall, did the pitch step down on the nucleus rather than drift down over several words?", "Did the fall stay in modal voice, clear of creak or fry?", "Did the meter show no end rise on the second and third versions?", "Did the fall-rise on \"principle\" imply a reservation without a word added?"]}, {"id": "voice-5-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Proposal into position", "how": "Say the weak version with its natural rise and hedges, then the strong version on a clean fall. Keep the strong one courteous: firmness lives in the contour, not in coldness.", "items": ["The working party recommends option B? => The working party *recommends* | option *B*.", "Could we perhaps move to item six? => We'll move to item *six*.", "I just think the weighting might be a bit high? => The weighting is too *high*.", "Is it all right if I take questions at the end? => I'll take questions at the *end*.", "So we're sort of hoping for a decision today? => We're asking for a *decision* | *today*.", "I'm not sure the evidence quite supports that? => The evidence does not *support* that."], "checks": ["Did every strong version end on a fall on the marked nucleus?", "Did you drop the hedges as well as the rise?", "Did the strong versions remain courteous in tone?", "Did the meter show a rise on the weak versions and none on the strong?"]}, {"id": "voice-5-d3", "type": "read", "title": "Lists that close", "how": "Read each list with a slight rise on every item but the last, a clean fall on the final item, and a held beat after it.", "items": ["The submission addresses *rationale*, | *structure*, | and *assessment*. ||", "The panel will weigh *merit*, | *reach*, | and *viability*. ||", "I'd like to thank the *authority*, | the *universities*, | and the *teachers* who trialled the course. ||", "With us tonight are the *Chair* of the board, | the *Mayor*, | and our patron, Professor *Lindsay*. ||", "The risks are *cost*, | *timing*, | and *reputation*, | and the *greatest* of these is *timing*. ||", "Item *four*, | the *budget*; | item *five*, | the *venue*; | and item *six*, | *general business*. ||"], "checks": ["Did each non-final item rise slightly?", "Did the final item fall unmistakably?", "In the risk list, did \"timing\" fall harder the second time than the first?", "Did you hold the beat after each list?"]}, {"id": "voice-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Stating a position", "how": "Speak to the decision-maker named, using the structure shown. Every statement ends on a fall; one deliberate fall-rise is allowed where you concede a point; the only rise is a genuine question.", "items": ["To the principal: why the department needs a Level 3 coordinator.", "To a SCSA panel: why the practical weighting should not be reduced.", "To a university committee: why a performance unit should carry full credit toward the degree.", "To the festival board: why the budget needs a contingency line.", "To a parent body: why a student cannot leave the program mid-course.", "To an arts funding panel: why the project should be funded over three years rather than one."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did the meter show no end-of-phrase rise on any statement?", "Did any hedge (\"sort of\", \"maybe\", \"I think\", \"just\") lift an ending?", "Did you reset pitch audibly when you moved from reason to example?", "Did your final point fall and then stop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "pair", "task": "Read the weak phrasing aloud with its natural rises, then the strong phrasing with every statement on a clean fall and one deliberate fall-rise where a reservation belongs. Then deliver the strong version from memory to an imagined panel chair, with a pitch reset at the start and a held beat at the end.", "seconds": 40, "checks": ["Did every statement in the strong version end on a fall?", "Was the fall-rise placed on the one word that carries the reservation?", "From memory, did the falls hold without drifting into fry?", "Did you hold the final beat instead of adding a softener?"]}}, {"id": "voice-6", "title": "Projection Without Shouting", "goal": "Size the voice to any room, from a board table to an unamplified hall or a lectern microphone, with power from breath and resonance and a free throat.", "teach": {"body": "Loudness has three sources, and only two are sustainable. Subglottal pressure, generated by the respiratory system, drives the vocal folds; the efficiency of fold closure determines how much of that pressure becomes acoustic energy; and the vocal tract, pharynx, mouth and lips, shapes and amplifies the result. Shouting raises loudness by increasing laryngeal effort: the larynx rises, the extrinsic muscles of the neck engage, pitch climbs and the folds collide with greater force. It sounds pushed, it narrows the range, and it is the usual mechanism of the vocal fatigue to which teachers, as an occupational group, are well documented to be vulnerable.\n\nProjection is the alternative: more support, efficient closure, an open and stable tract. The breath work of the first unit raises the pressure; the articulation of the second makes the signal intelligible; resonance does the rest. Classical singers carry over an orchestra largely because of the singer's formant, a clustering of resonances around 3 kHz, where orchestral energy is relatively weak and human hearing is acutely sensitive. Trained speaking voices show a related, less pronounced brightness, sometimes called the speaker's ring. A forward, slightly bright tone, with space at the back of the mouth and the jaw free, carries further at the same effort than a dark, pressed one. Semi-occluded vocal tract exercises, phonating through a straw or on a lip trill, are the standard means of finding efficient closure without strain, and a minute of them before a long speaking day is time well spent.\n\nNoise triggers the Lombard effect: speakers in noise involuntarily raise intensity and pitch and lengthen their vowels. Over a reception, a conference foyer or a dinner with speeches, the effect is cumulative and tiring. The expert response is never to compete with the room. Wait it out, or arrange to be introduced, and then begin at a level just above what the room will be once it is quiet.\n\nEach room has a size. A boardroom or committee table is conversational, directed at the most distant member; projection at a table reads as lecturing, and among senior colleagues it costs more authority than it buys. An unamplified hall wants the back wall as target, a slower tempo, shorter thought groups and finished consonants. A ceremony or conference on a public address system wants conversational level and constant microphone technique. Level changes sharply with distance: in open space it falls by roughly six decibels with each doubling of distance, so small movements toward and away from a lectern microphone produce large swings, and turning the head to address one side of the room takes the voice off-axis. Directional microphones also show the proximity effect, an increase in bass response at close range, which flatters a low voice up to a point and then muddies it. Speak slightly across the capsule to keep plosives off it, keep the distance fixed, and turn from the shoulders, not the neck.\n\nUnder pressure projection fails in three ways. Tension migrates to the neck and jaw, and the voice thins and rises. The speaker compensates for nerves with volume and arrives at the conclusion tired. Or, at a lectern, he leans in to the microphone on the important sentences, which booms, and away on the asides, which vanish. The test is simple: projection that is working costs the throat nothing.", "model": "The microphone has decided to *rest*, | so I'll do without it. || I'll be watching the *back row*; | if I lose you, | *tell* me. || || This session concerns *assessment in performance*, | and it runs until *four*. || Our three speakers will take *fifteen* minutes each, | and we'll take questions *together*, | at the *end*. || Our first speaker | is Professor *Anne Rowe*.", "mistake": "(Chin forward, pitch climbing) Right, the microphone seems to have given up, which happens in this room more than you'd think, so I'll push a bit, and do wave if you can't hear at the back. This session's on assessment in performance and it runs until four, our three speakers have fifteen minutes each, and questions at the end, so please welcome Professor Anne Rowe.", "remember": ["Power from breath and resonance, never the neck.", "Aim at the farthest listener, not the front row.", "Never compete with noise; wait it out.", "Conversational level at a table and on a microphone.", "Fix microphone distance; turn from the shoulders."]}, "drills": [{"id": "voice-6-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "Supported count", "how": "Follow each instruction on the pattern shown. Volume rises from the waist and ribs; the neck, jaw and pitch stay exactly as they are at boardroom level.", "items": ["Breathe low. Count one to six on the out-breath at boardroom level, aimed at the far end of the table.", "Breathe low. Count one to six aimed at the back of a seminar room, effort at the waist and ribs, neck unchanged.", "Breathe low. Count one to six aimed at the back row of a hall, jaw free and tone forward.", "Breathe low. Count one to six on a crescendo from table to hall level, with pitch held level throughout.", "Breathe low. Count one to six on a diminuendo from hall to table level, with the consonants as clean at six as at one.", "Breathe low. Glide gently through your speaking range on a lip trill, then speak \"The meeting is open\" at hall level on the same easy closure."], "pattern": [3, 0, 6, 2], "checks": ["Did the neck stay relaxed at hall level, with no visible tension?", "Did pitch stay level as intensity grew?", "Was the effort felt at the waist and ribs rather than the throat?", "After the lip trill, did the spoken line feel easier than before it?"]}, {"id": "voice-6-d2", "type": "repeat", "title": "Size to the room", "how": "Say each line four times, resizing in turn for a boardroom table, a seminar room, an unamplified hall and a lectern microphone. Only the size changes; pitch and tempo stay put, except that the hall version slows slightly.", "items": ["Good afternoon, and welcome.", "I declare the meeting open.", "Our next speaker is Professor Hart.", "The motion is carried.", "Please be seated.", "I'd like to hear from members who have not yet spoken."], "checks": ["Were the four sizes clearly distinct?", "Did pitch stay put as the size grew?", "Did the boardroom version stay conversational rather than projected?", "Did the microphone version return to conversational level rather than hold hall level?", "Did the hall version stay free of strain in the neck?"]}, {"id": "voice-6-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "Throat stays free", "how": "Use the front camera as a mirror. Follow each instruction, watching the neck, jaw, chin and shoulders for the whole time shown.", "items": ["Say \"Members of the board, good evening\" at table level, then at hall level. Watch the neck: no cords or veins standing out at hall level.", "Fingertips lightly on the front of the throat. Count to ten on a crescendo. The larynx should not climb under your fingers.", "Watch the jaw on \"Distinguished guests and colleagues\" at hall level. It drops for the vowels and never sets.", "Watch the shoulders as you take a breath for the back row. They stay down.", "Deliver a ceremonial welcome at hall level with the chin level, neither thrust forward to reach the back nor tucked.", "Address the left of an imagined hall, then the right. The eyes lead, the shoulders follow, and the head stays balanced over the spine."], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did the neck stay smooth at hall level?", "Did the larynx stay low under your fingers through the crescendo?", "Did the chin stay level throughout?", "Did the shoulders stay down on every breath?"]}, {"id": "voice-6-d4", "type": "read", "title": "At the lectern microphone", "how": "Hold the phone as a lectern microphone, a hand-span away and slightly off-axis. Read at conversational level, keeping the distance fixed even on stressed words, and turn from the shoulders when addressing different parts of the room.", "items": ["Thank you, *Chair*. || Distinguished guests, | colleagues, | it is a *pleasure* to be here.", "Our *keynote* this morning | is Professor *Paul Bennett*, | from the School of *Music*.", "Before we begin, | a *practical* note: | the *exits* are to your *left* and *right*.", "I'll be *brief*, | because the *program* is not.", "We'll take a *break* at this point, | and resume at *eleven-fifteen*, | *promptly*.", "Thank you for your *attention*, | and for your *patience* with the *timetable*.", "Thank you very *much*. || That was *Body and Soul*, | and I'm *Dijon Summers* on bass."], "checks": ["Did the distance from the phone stay constant, including on stressed words?", "Were /p/ and /b/ free of pops?", "Did you stay at conversational level rather than projecting into the microphone?", "When you turned, did the voice stay on-axis because the shoulders moved, not the neck?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver a ceremonial closing to the back of the largest room available, unamplified, with power from breath and resonance. Then deliver it again with the phone held as a lectern microphone at fixed distance and conversational level, turning to address both sides of the room. Record both: the second should sound smaller in effort and no smaller in authority.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["In the unamplified delivery, did the throat stay free at full size?", "On the microphone, did you drop to conversational level and keep the distance fixed?", "Did the consonants carry in both recordings?", "Did your voice feel as fresh at the end of the second delivery as at the start of the first?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Train of thought", "intro": "This strand trains the line through what you say: the governing sentence first, a shape you can hold under pressure, signposts the room can hear, and the means to leave the line and find it again. Among people who argue for a living, a fast associative mind is an asset only when the room can follow it, and when you can too.", "units": [{"id": "thought-1", "title": "The governing sentence first", "goal": "Open any contribution with the one sentence that would stand as the minuted position, so that everything after it is support the room may take or leave.", "teach": {"body": "Every contribution in a committee has a governing sentence: the one that would survive if the minute-taker could record only a single line. Barbara Minto built the Pyramid Principle on putting it at the top; military staff writing calls it BLUF, bottom line up front; a good appellate advocate opens with \"the appeal should be allowed for two reasons\" and only then gives them. The discipline is identical in every version. The claim comes first, in one sentence, and everything after it is support.\n\nThe cognitive case is stronger than convention. Bransford and Johnson's well-known experiments showed that listeners given the frame before the detail comprehend and recall far more than listeners given the same detail with the frame withheld. Without the headline, the room is not storing your reasons; it is holding them in suspension, guessing what they are for. In an SCSA committee of quick, well-read people that suspension does not last. Someone forms a view of your point before you reach it, and from then on they are listening to confirm the guess rather than to hear you. Put the claim first and every subsequent sentence lands on a prepared surface.\n\nFor an associative mind the stakes are higher. Your reasoning is networked: regional access connects to accompanist funding, which connects to the last review, and each node is genuinely relevant. Spoken in the order you found it, the network becomes a narrative of your search. Spoken from the headline outward, the same material becomes elaboration, and any branch you take hangs off a line the room already holds.\n\nThe governing sentence has four properties. It is a proposition, not a topic: \"the practical weighting should stay where it is\" rather than \"I wanted to say something about weighting\". It is unconditioned: the qualifications follow it. It fits a single intonation phrase, so it can carry one falling nuclear tone; a terminal rise on a headline signals incompleteness and invites the interruption you are trying to pre-empt. And it is the sentence you would keep if the chair cut you off. Test it on that basis, every time.\n\nUnder pressure it fails in recognisable ways, and the capable professional's versions are the dangerous ones, because they sound reasonable. The credential preamble: \"Having sat on the panel for some years\". The concessive run-up: \"I don't disagree with much of what's been said, but\". The diplomatic conditional: \"I wonder whether we might at least consider\". The deferred verdict, where an impeccable analysis ends in \"so on balance I'd probably lean towards keeping it\". Each spends the room's best attention, the first few seconds, on something other than your position, and the last hands your authority to the hedges.\n\nElite speakers know the corollary: the headline is often the whole contribution. A member who says \"I support the motion; the mover has made the case\" and stops has used less airtime and acquired more weight than one who supports it for four minutes. Brevity after a clear claim signals confidence that the case stands.\n\nThe technique is simple to state and hard to execute with adrenaline up. Before you indicate to the chair, find the sentence, not the contribution. Say it at a measured pace, fall on the final stressed word, and hold a full beat. That beat is where the room registers the position and where you decide, with the chair's eye on you, how much support it actually needs. Usually it is less than you prepared.", "model": "Chair, | my view is that the practical component should *stay* at its current weighting. || Two reasons. || The consultation shows that regional schools *can* resource it, | and reducing it would change what the course is *for*. || I'm happy to take the committee through the regional responses, | but that is the position, | and I'd ask that it be recorded as the committee's *recommendation*.", "mistake": "I've spent some time with the consultation responses, and there are real concerns from regional schools about accompanists, which I think deserve weight, and there's the broader question of what the course is actually for, so on balance I'd probably lean towards keeping the practical weighting where it is, if the committee is comfortable with that.", "remember": ["State the proposition before any context.", "Make it a claim, not a topic.", "Qualifications follow the headline; they never precede it.", "One intonation phrase, a falling tone, then a full beat.", "Often the headline is the whole contribution."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-1-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Headline, then a held beat", "how": "Read each item aloud. Bring the stressed word down on a clear fall and hold a full beat of silence before the support sentence, so the headline is heard as complete.", "items": ["The practical component should *stay* at its current weighting. || The consultation supports it.", "I support the *motion*. || The mover has made the case.", "The festival returned a *surplus*. || Modest, | but the first in three years.", "I recommend the panel fund this application in *full*. || Partial funding buys half a project.", "The draft rubric conflates *interpretation* with *accuracy*. || They need separate criteria.", "Principal, | I need a decision on the Friday slot by *Thursday*. || After that the bands lose the term."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the headline fall to its lowest pitch on the stressed word?", "Did I hold a full beat of silence before the support sentence?", "Was the headline a single breath with no internal hesitation?", "Would the headline stand as the minuted position without the second sentence?"]}, {"id": "thought-1-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Run-up to verdict", "how": "Say the weak version with full conviction, as a capable colleague would, then the strong version. The difference should be in structure, not in confidence.", "items": ["Having looked closely at the consultation responses, and bearing in mind the regional feedback, I'd probably lean towards retaining the current weighting => The current weighting should stay. | The consultation supports it.", "I don't disagree with much of what's been said, and I think the intent is right, but I do have some reservations about the timeline => The timeline is too short. | The intent is right.", "I wonder whether we might at least consider deferring this to the next meeting, given the papers only arrived yesterday => I move that we defer this to the next meeting. | The papers arrived yesterday.", "As someone who has sat on assessment panels for some years now, my sense is that the descriptors are a little ambiguous => The top-band descriptors are ambiguous. | I can show the committee where.", "There are a number of considerations here, financial and reputational, and it's genuinely finely balanced, but on the whole => I recommend we decline the sponsorship.", "Just to give the board some context on how we arrived at the figure, we obtained three quotes, and the middle one => I recommend the middle of the three quotes. | It is the only one that includes insurance."], "checks": ["Did the strong version place the proposition within its first six words?", "Did every qualification stay out of the strong version's first sentence?", "Did the strong version end on a fall, not a rise?", "Did I deliver the weak version convincingly, so the contrast was structural?"]}, {"id": "thought-1-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "The minuted sentence", "how": "Use the prep time only to find the governing sentence. Say it, hold the silence, and stop. Nothing else.", "items": ["The chair of the SCSA syllabus committee turns to you: do you support the revised course outline?", "A member of a university academic board asks where the school sector stands on the proposed entry requirement.", "At the end of a long meeting the principal asks: do we hold the specialist auditions in Term 2 or Term 3?", "An arts funding panel convenor asks for your recommendation on the borderline application.", "The festival association chair asks whether the treasurer supports the new ticketing platform.", "A conference moderator asks for your view in one sentence before the panel closes."], "structure": "ONE", "prep": 5, "seconds": 12, "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Was my first sentence a proposition rather than a topic?", "Did I stop after the headline and hold the silence?", "Did I avoid a credential, a concession or a conditional before the claim?", "Did the sentence finish within one breath, on a fall?"]}, {"id": "thought-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Verdict, then the case", "how": "Open with the governing sentence and hold a beat. Give one reason and one concrete example, then return to the verdict in different words and stop.", "items": ["School council: should the school raise the voluntary contribution for specialist programs?", "SCSA assessment committee: should the practical examination include an unprepared component?", "University academic committee: should the conservatorium accept a portfolio in lieu of an audition for regional applicants?", "Festival association board: should the festival accept a sponsor whose brand sits uneasily with a schools' event?", "The principal's office: why should Music retain its full staffing allocation next year?", "Arts funding panel: should multi-year funding be concentrated in fewer organisations?"], "structure": "PREP", "prep": 10, "seconds": 45, "targets": {"fillers": 1, "wpm": [130, 160]}, "checks": ["Did the verdict arrive before any background?", "Did I hold a beat after it?", "Was the reason a single sentence and the example specific enough to check?", "Did the close restate the verdict in new words without adding material?", "If cut off at eight seconds, would the room have had my position?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Read the scenario and take a position. Deliver it aloud with the governing sentence first, then cut it to the minimum the room strictly needs and deliver it again, at less than half the length of the first attempt.", "seconds": 45, "checks": ["Was my first sentence the position, unconditioned?", "Was the second attempt less than half the length of the first?", "Did both attempts end on a falling, complete sentence?", "Did any hedge survive into the second attempt?"]}}, {"id": "thought-2", "title": "Structures as rails", "goal": "Choose a structure in the first second of a question and hold it as rails, so every part is delivered and the last part never goes missing.", "teach": {"body": "Working memory is narrow, and it narrows further in exactly the conditions in which you need it. Nelson Cowan's synthesis puts its capacity at around four chunks rather than Miller's famous seven, and Amy Arnsten's work on the prefrontal cortex explains why it degrades under acute stress: the catecholamine surge that sharpens vigilance takes the prefrontal networks that hold information online partly offline. An attentive committee, a question with an edge, and a mind generating connections faster than it can discard them are all competing for those few slots. A structure is a way of offloading. You hold one shape, which costs a single chunk, and the shape tells you what comes next.\n\nMinto's SCQA (situation, complication, question, answer) is the consultant's version; the barrister's is the set of propositions announced at the start of submissions. For spoken work in your rooms, a small set covers almost everything.\n\nPREP (point, reason, example, point) argues a position. The returning point is not repetition; it is the landing, and in committee it is the sentence the chair will paraphrase into the minutes.\n\nTWO (two points, the first, the second, so) lists. Two is the number you can always hold under pressure. Three is rhetorically attractive and operationally risky, because the tricolon's third limb is the one that evaporates when the chair glances at the clock. If you genuinely have three, announce two and fold the third into the so.\n\nWSN (what, so what, now what) reports. It converts narrative into interpretation and ends on an action, which is what a board wants from a report and seldom gets.\n\nPCF (problem, cause, fix) proposes a repair. One sentence each for the problem and the cause, and the fix carries the time. It keeps you out of the history of the problem, where tangents breed and where a committee's patience drains away.\n\nPPF (past, present, future) frames an occasion or a trajectory: a valedictory, a conference retrospective, an anniversary concert. STAR (situation, task, action, result) answers a selection panel's behavioural question.\n\nChoosing is the real skill, and it should take a second, not a deliberation. While the question is still being put, ask one thing: am I arguing, listing, reporting or fixing? The answer gives the shape. A slightly wrong structure held firmly beats an ideal one held loosely; a room forgives imperfect architecture far more readily than a contribution that visibly loses its way.\n\nElite speakers make the scaffolding audible without making it mechanical. In a seminar room \"two things\" can sound like a workshop technique; \"I'd make two points\" or \"there are two difficulties\" carries the same frame in academic register. The commitment is identical. The phrasing matches the room.\n\nThe failures under pressure are specific. Abandonment: a better idea arrives during the reason and you follow it, leaving the example and the return unspoken. Mismatch: you narrate when the chair asked for a recommendation, so the room hears a report and waits for a verdict that never comes. Inflation: a third point that turns out to restate the first. And the announced structure not delivered: say two to a room of academics and they will count.\n\nWhen a better idea arrives mid-shape, let it arrive, note it, and finish the shape anyway. Offer it afterwards as a separate contribution, where it will be heard properly.", "model": "There are *two* difficulties with the proposed timetable. || The *first* is that Concert Band 1 loses its only full-ensemble rehearsal, | the one the festival program depends on. || The *second* is timing: | the change lands in week three, | a fortnight before adjudication. || *So* I'd ask the executive for one further term on the current arrangement, | with a review in week *eight*.", "mistake": "I've got some concerns with the timetable, which I'll try to keep brief. The Friday slot is the obvious one, because it's our only full rehearsal, and that matters more than it looks, because the festival program is built around it, which actually goes back to how the program was set up, and then it falls right before adjudication, and there's a related staffing issue, so I'd really prefer we held off for now.", "remember": ["Arguing PREP, listing TWO, reporting WSN, fixing PCF.", "Choose in one second, then commit.", "Announce two; fold the third into the so.", "Finish the shape before chasing the new idea.", "Match the scaffolding phrase to the room's register."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-2-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Hear the skeleton", "how": "Read each model aloud. Give the structural words slightly more time than the content around them, so the skeleton is audible under the argument.", "items": ["PREP. | The unprepared component should *stay*. || It is the only part of the examination that tests musicianship rather than preparation. || The candidates who handle it well are consistently the strongest musicians in the cohort. || So it *stays*.", "TWO. | I'd make *two* points on the entry proposal. || The *first* is equity: | a portfolio pathway reaches students an audition never sees. || The *second* is rigour: | without moderation it becomes a softer door. || *So*, | support in principle, | conditional on moderation.", "WSN. | The festival returned a *small* surplus. || That is the first in three years, | and it means the reserve has stopped falling. || *Now* the board can commit to the venue twelve months out.", "PCF. | The *problem* is that council papers arrive the night before. || The *cause* is a deadline set after the executive meets. || The *fix* is to move the deadline to the *Friday* prior, | and table anything later as late business.", "PPF. | When this program began it had *one* band. || Tonight it has eleven ensembles and a waiting list. || What it becomes *next* depends on the people in this room.", "STAR. | [A time a program you ran came under strain]. || My task was to [the one thing that had to be fixed]. || I [the action you took]. || [The *result*, as a number or a date]."], "checks": ["Could I hear the structural words above the content?", "Did each section take its own breath group?", "Did the final sentence of each item sit lower than the first?", "Did I give the structural words slightly more time, without rushing them?"]}, {"id": "thought-2-d2", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Name the shape, then hold it", "how": "During the prep time, say aloud only the name of the structure you have chosen: PREP, TWO, WSN, PCF, PPF or STAR. Then answer using it in full. The choice and its completion matter more than the content.", "items": ["The board chair: the sound contractor failed on festival night. What happened?", "SCSA committee: should composition be assessed by portfolio or by examination?", "The principal: what do you need from the executive this term?", "University academic committee: why are instrumental enrolments falling across the state?", "Parent body annual general meeting: what changed in the specialist program this year?", "Arts funding panel: why does your organisation need operational rather than project funding?"], "structure": "ANY", "prep": 3, "seconds": 40, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did I name a structure aloud before the first word of the answer?", "Did the answer complete every part of the structure I named?", "Was the structure right for the question: arguing, listing, reporting or fixing?", "Did the final sentence close the shape rather than open a new idea?"]}, {"id": "thought-2-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Two, in committee register", "how": "Preview two points in the register of the room (\"I'd make two points\", \"there are two difficulties\"), deliver each, and close with so. If a third arrives, fold it into the so or let it go.", "items": ["A new member of the SCSA committee asks what they should watch for in moderation meetings.", "The school council asks what you would change about instrumental allocations across the network.", "A conference panel asks what distinguishes a sustainable school music program.", "The principal asks what concerns you most about next year.", "The festival association board asks what it should stop doing.", "A university admissions committee asks what school-sector applicants are not being prepared for."], "structure": "TWO", "prep": 8, "seconds": 50, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did I preview two in the room's register rather than a workshop's?", "Did I deliver exactly two, each clearly marked?", "Was any third point folded into the so rather than appended?", "Did the so draw a conclusion rather than summarise the two points?"]}, {"id": "thought-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Problem, cause, fix", "how": "One sentence for the problem, one for the cause, then spend the rest on the fix. End on the fix and its first concrete step.", "items": ["Council meetings routinely run well over time.", "Moderation samples from regional schools arrive too late to be cross-marked.", "The festival's adjudication results are disputed every year.", "Senior students are withdrawing from ensembles in Year 12.", "Department meetings produce decisions that are never actioned.", "The arts learning area's shared performance calendar keeps generating clashes."], "structure": "PCF", "prep": 10, "seconds": 60, "targets": {"fillers": 2, "wpm": [130, 160]}, "checks": ["Was the problem stated in one sentence?", "Was the cause a single, specific mechanism rather than a list?", "Did the fix take more than half the speaking time?", "Did I stay out of the history of the problem?", "Did I end on the fix and its first concrete step?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Read the scenario and decide within one second whether you are arguing, listing, reporting or fixing; name the structure aloud and deliver it in full. Then deliver it again in a different structure, and say briefly which served the room better and why.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did I choose within a second?", "Did both deliveries complete every part of their structure?", "Could a listener name each structure from the delivery alone?", "Was my one-sentence judgement specific about why one shape served better?"]}}, {"id": "thought-3", "title": "Signposting for the ear", "goal": "Make the architecture of any contribution audible through brief signposts and pitch resets, so the room always knows where you are, and so do you.", "teach": {"body": "A reader sees the architecture of a page before reading a word of it: headings, paragraph breaks, the length of what remains. A listener has none of that. Speech is linear and evanescent, so the architecture must be carried in the signal itself. Linguists call the words that do this discourse markers, and Deborah Schiffrin's work is the standard account; at the level of the whole text, Ken Hyland's term metadiscourse covers language about the organisation of what is being said. Classical rhetoric gave the opening announcement of structure its own name, the partitio, and treated it as a regular part of a speech.\n\nThe words are only half of it. The other half is prosody. Spoken discourse groups into what intonation researchers call paratones, the spoken equivalent of paragraphs. A new paratone begins with a pitch reset, an onset noticeably higher than the end of what preceded it, and the previous one closes low, usually with a longer pause. Practised broadcasters and senior counsel do this without thinking: the voice drops and slows at the close of a point, there is a beat, and the next point begins on a fresh, higher onset. Listeners hear the section break even when no marker is spoken. When the reset is missing, even well-chosen signposts are swallowed, because they arrive at the pitch and pace of the content around them.\n\nThere are four functions. The preview commits to the shape: \"I'd make two points.\" The marker opens each section: \"First.\" \"The second point.\" The transition closes one section and opens the next in a single move: \"That's the cost. The risk is a different matter.\" The landing draws the conclusion: \"So.\" Each is brief, delivered a fraction slower than its surroundings, and followed by a small pause. The pause is what turns the word from filler into structure.\n\nThere is a private benefit, and for an associative thinker it is the main one. A spoken preview is a commitment you hear yourself make. Once you have said \"two points\", a running count persists whether you attend to it or not, and when a tangent opens in the middle of the first point, the unanswered second is already waiting to pull you back. The signpost turns the room into external memory.\n\nTwo refinements matter in senior rooms. \"The second point\" restates the frame and is plainer than \"secondly\", which tends to license lists that never end. And so should be reserved for the landing, where it means therefore. If it also opens your sentences as a filler, you have blunted your strongest closing signal, and articulate listeners notice.\n\nThe failures are of three kinds. Over-signposting, the academic vice: \"What I'd like to do in the next few minutes is make a couple of brief points, the first of which concerns\" spends a sentence on nothing. A preview should be four words, not twenty. Under-signposting, where points run together and the room cannot tell whether you have moved on; capable speakers often fold a second point into a subordinate clause of the first, and it disappears. And false signposting, which erodes trust quickly: two announced and three delivered, \"finally\" said twice, \"in short\" followed by the long version.\n\nUnder pressure the specific failure is speed. Adrenaline compresses the pauses first, the reset flattens, and the signposts are still being said but no longer heard. Slow the markers deliberately. They are downbeats, not pickups.", "model": "I'd make *two* points on the draft. || *First*, | the assessment outline is sound, | and I'd endorse it as written. || That's the outline. || The *second* point concerns the exemplars: | they sit at the top band only, | and teachers most need to see the *middle*. || *So* | endorse the outline, | and ask the writers for exemplars across the full *range* before the next meeting.", "mistake": "I have a few comments on the draft. The assessment outline is sound, and I'd endorse it, although it's worth saying how it relates to the exemplars, because the exemplars only sit at the top band, which, to be fair, is partly a resourcing question, and teachers really need to see the middle range, so I'd endorse it, subject to that, or perhaps we note it.", "remember": ["Preview the count in four words or fewer.", "Close each section low; open the next higher.", "Say \"the second point\", not \"secondly\".", "Reserve so for the landing.", "Deliver exactly what you announced."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-3-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Reset and downbeat", "how": "Read each item aloud. Close each section low and slow, take the pause, and begin the next marker on a clearly higher onset.", "items": ["I'd make *two* points. || *First*, | the timeline is achievable. || *The second point*: | the budget is not.", "That's the *cost*. || The *risk* is a different matter.", "There are *two* difficulties with the motion. || *First*, | it pre-empts the review. || *The second*, | it binds the next council. || *So* | I'd ask the mover to withdraw it.", "That covers the *audit*. || Now the *forward estimates*.", "*Two* items before we close. || *First*, | the date of the next meeting. || *The second*, | the vacancy on the finance subcommittee.", "*So*, | to bring it together: | the program *continues*, | and the fee *holds*."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did each section close lower and slower than it began?", "Did each new marker start on a higher onset than the close before it?", "Was there an audible pause after every signpost?", "Did so appear only at the conclusion?"]}, {"id": "thought-3-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Metadiscourse, trimmed", "how": "Say the cluttered signpost, then the clean one. Hear how much shorter the clean one is, and how much more clearly it marks the place in the structure.", "items": ["What I'd like to do in the next few minutes is make a couple of brief points, the first of which concerns => Two points. | First,", "Turning now, if I may, to the second of the matters I mentioned at the outset => The second point.", "Secondly, and this relates in some ways to what I said a moment ago => The second point follows from the first.", "In terms of the other side of the ledger, as it were, the financial dimension => That's the case for it. | Now the cost.", "So, in summary, so to pull all of that together, so essentially => So.", "I'll come to the question of timing in a moment, but before that, if I can just go back => Scope first. | Then timing.", "And then, I suppose, by way of a final observation, if I might just add one more thing => One last point."], "checks": ["Was the clean version four words or fewer before its content?", "Did I pause after the clean version before continuing?", "Did the clean version make the count or the place in the structure obvious?", "Did the clean version sound like a senior speaker, not a workshop?"]}, {"id": "thought-3-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Preview, mark, land", "how": "Preview the count, mark each point aloud, use one transition sentence between them with an audible reset, and close with so.", "items": ["Open a university academic committee item you are leading: the proposed change to entry requirements.", "Brief the school council on the state of the instrumental program.", "Introduce the second half of a conference session you are chairing.", "Report to the festival association board on the adjudication review.", "Set out for the principal what Music needs from other learning areas during concert week.", "Open an SCSA reference group's discussion of the draft exemplars."], "structure": "TWO", "prep": 8, "seconds": 45, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did I preview the count before any content?", "Did I mark the second point aloud with \"the second point\" or an equivalent?", "Did a transition close the first point before the second opened?", "Was there an audible pitch reset at the start of the second point?", "Did so appear only at the conclusion?"]}, {"id": "thought-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "The signpost as lifeline", "how": "Signpost fully, but let one genuine tangent in during the first point. Use the next marker, said slowly and on a high onset, to bring yourself back.", "items": ["Tell the principal what this year's auditions revealed about primary feeder programs.", "Explain to the festival association board why volunteer numbers fall in the final week.", "Give a conference audience the case for specialist music programs in public schools.", "Report to the school council on the instrument hire scheme.", "Explain to a university panel how school ensemble programs feed tertiary study.", "Brief a new SCSA committee member on how moderation works in practice."], "structure": "TWO", "prep": 10, "seconds": 60, "targets": {"fillers": 3, "wpm": [130, 160]}, "checks": ["Did the next marker bring me back within a sentence of the tangent ending?", "Was the break between points audible as a pitch reset, not only as words?", "Did I deliver exactly the number I previewed?", "Did I close with so and stop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage and mark its architecture: where each section begins and ends, and where a preview, marker or landing is missing. Deliver it aloud, adding only those signposts and the pitch resets that carry them, and no other words.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["Did I add a preview of four words or fewer?", "Did every new section begin with a marker on a higher onset?", "Did I add nothing beyond signposts?", "Did the close fall and stop?"]}}, {"id": "thought-4", "title": "Bracket and return", "goal": "Recognise a digression as it opens, bracket it audibly in a phrase, and return to the line by name, in your own contributions and from the chair.", "teach": {"body": "The digression is not a fault. Quintilian treats it, under the Greek name parekbasis, as a legitimate device in the Institutio Oratoria, provided it is brief and the speaker returns. The connection your mind makes mid-sentence is often the most interesting thing anyone says in the meeting. The difficulty is the unmanaged digression, which takes the room with it and leaves no path back, so that the original point is either lost or recovered by a chair who now owns it. The skill is bracketing: open the departure deliberately, keep it short, close it aloud, and return by name.\n\nThere are three moves, and each has a physical signature.\n\nNotice. Digressions announce themselves in the body before they announce themselves in the argument. The rate lifts, the pitch range widens, the hands become busier, and there is the small reward of a new connection. For an ADHD mind that reward is strong, and it arrives before any judgement about relevance. Learn your own signs; the meter will show you the moment words per minute jumps in the middle of a thought.\n\nBracket. If the aside is worth having, mark it as an aside in the voice. Skilled speakers drop the register slightly and narrow the pitch range for a parenthetical, as a broadcaster lowers the voice for a qualification, and then restore the main register on return. The listener hears the brackets as surely as a reader sees them. If the aside is not worth having, park it in a phrase: \"That's a separate question.\" \"I'll leave that for another day.\" \"A matter for the review.\" Parking aloud tells the room the departure was intended, which preserves its confidence in you, and it moves the idea out of your working memory and into the record, where it stops pulling at you. If it genuinely matters, ask that it be minuted for a future agenda, then let it go.\n\nReturn. Name the point you are returning to. \"Back to the weighting.\" \"The second point, then: the cost.\" \"Where I was: the timeline.\" The name is essential. \"Anyway\" returns you to nowhere, and invites a colleague to supply the destination for you. If you previewed the structure, the return is already built: the next marker is the return.\n\nAmong capable professionals the failures are subtle. The apology: \"Sorry, I'm digressing\" draws attention to the departure without closing it, and lowers your standing for no gain. The over-long bracket: one or two sentences is an aside; a paragraph is a change of subject. The summary return, where you lose your place and fall back on restating everything, which costs the room twice. And the meta-digression, most common in highly verbal people: digressing about the fact that you are digressing.\n\nFrom the chair the same three moves work on the room. Australian meeting practice, as set out in Joske and in Renton, expects the chair to keep debate relevant to the question before the meeting. The well-judged ruling is rarely a ruling at all. It acknowledges the contribution, gives it a home, and returns the meeting to the item by name: \"That deserves its own item. Let's minute it for the next meeting and come back to the motion.\" Delivered evenly, at the bottom of your range, it is received as service rather than correction. Delivered quickly and higher, it sounds like irritation, and the member will defend the tangent rather than release it.\n\nThe test of the skill is that nobody remembers the digression as a digression. They remember an aside that enriched the point, or a matter properly parked, and a speaker who never lost the line.", "model": "The practical weighting should *stay* where it is. || It is the only component that rewards what students do in *ensembles*, | which, | incidentally, | raises the larger question of how ensemble work is credited across the arts. || That's a matter for the *review*. || *Back* to the weighting: | it stays, | because the consultation supports it and nothing in the data argues otherwise.", "mistake": "The practical weighting should stay where it is, because it's the only component that rewards ensemble work, and actually that's a much bigger issue, how ensemble work gets credited across all the arts subjects, we've been circling it for years, sorry, I'm digressing, but my point is that I think the current weighting is right.", "remember": ["Notice the lift in rate and pitch.", "Lower the register for an aside.", "Park it in a phrase; never apologise.", "Return by naming the point.", "From the chair: acknowledge, give it a home, return."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-4-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Hear the brackets", "how": "Read each item aloud. Drop the register and narrow the range for the aside, pause after the parking phrase, then restore full voice and stress the word that names the return.", "items": ["The fee holds at *three hundred*. || There's a wider question about hire, | but that's for the review. || *Back* to the fee: | three hundred.", "I support the *amendment*. || Its drafting history is interesting, | and beside the point. || The amendment *improves* the motion.", "The exemplars need a *middle* band. || How they were commissioned is a separate question. || The *middle* band, | then: | that's the request.", "We need two more *adjudicators*. || The fee schedule is its own conversation. || *So*: | two adjudicators, | confirmed by Friday.", "That deserves its own *item*. || Let's minute it for the next meeting. || *Back* to the motion.", "The reserve should not fall below *six months* of operating costs. || I'll leave the investment policy for another day. || *Six* months, | as a floor."], "checks": ["Was the aside audibly lower and narrower in range than the main line?", "Did I pause after the parking phrase?", "Did the return name the point rather than say \"anyway\"?", "Was the return back at full voice and the main register?"]}, {"id": "thought-4-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Lost to returned", "how": "Say the weak return as a fluent colleague would, then the strong one. Hear how the strong version names the destination and carries no apology.", "items": ["Sorry, I'm digressing slightly, but just to go back to what I was saying => Back to the weighting.", "That's probably a conversation for another time, I suppose, if people are interested => That's for another meeting.", "But I don't want to get into all that now, it's a whole other thing, anyway => I'll leave that there. | The second point: the cost.", "Where was I? The timeline, I think, yes, the timeline => The timeline.", "I could say a great deal more about that, but in the interests of time, I'll just, yes => A matter for the review. | Back to the motion.", "I realise I'm now going off on a tangent about my own tangent => That's a separate question. | Back to the fee.", "With respect, I think that's slightly outside the scope of the item, if the member doesn't mind => That deserves its own item. | Let's minute it and return to the motion."], "checks": ["Did the strong version name the destination?", "Was the strong version free of apology and qualification?", "Did the strong version sound settled, at the lower end of my range?", "Was the strong version under eight words before the return?"]}, {"id": "thought-4-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "One bracket, then the line", "how": "Make your case in PREP. After the reason, take one genuine aside of no more than two sentences, bracket it in the voice or park it in a phrase, and return by naming the point.", "items": ["Tell the principal why Concert Band 1 needs a protected Friday rehearsal.", "Argue to the SCSA committee for retaining an unprepared performance component.", "Introduce the next tune at a jazz club, with one aside about the composer.", "Make the case to a funding panel for multi-year support of the festival.", "Argue to the school council that the voluntary contribution should not rise.", "Propose to a university committee that school-sector teachers sit on its admissions panel."], "structure": "PREP", "prep": 8, "seconds": 50, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Was the aside two sentences or fewer?", "Was it bracketed audibly, by register or by a parking phrase?", "Did the return name the point?", "Did I complete the PREP after returning, including the closing point?"]}, {"id": "thought-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Bracket from the chair", "how": "You are chairing. Voice the member's off-topic contribution in a single line, then acknowledge it, give it a specific home (the minutes, a future agenda, a subcommittee) and return the meeting to the item by name.", "items": ["Item: next year's meeting dates. A member begins on the condition of the hall.", "Item: the hire fee schedule. A member raises a complaint about an individual tutor.", "Item: festival ticket pricing. A board member begins a history of the venue.", "Item: endorsement of the draft syllabus. A committee member reopens a structural decision settled last year.", "Item: Year 7 intake numbers. A colleague raises their own timetable allocation.", "Item: the budget. A member asks to revisit last month's minutes, already confirmed."], "structure": "ABA", "prep": 8, "seconds": 30, "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did I acknowledge the contribution without engaging its substance?", "Did I give it a specific home rather than a vague \"later\"?", "Did I return the meeting to the item by name?", "Was my pitch low and my pace unhurried throughout?"]}], "apply": {"source": "story", "task": "Read the story, then retell it aloud. At one point take a deliberate digression about something it connects to, bracket it in the voice or park it in a phrase, and return to the exact point in the story by naming it.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did the digression stay under two sentences?", "Did I bracket or park it without apologising?", "Did I return to the exact point in the story, by name?", "Did the retelling still reach its ending within the time?"]}}, {"id": "thought-5", "title": "Anchors, not a script", "goal": "Deliver a prepared speech from a verbatim opening, three ordered anchors and a verbatim close, without notes, and recover instantly from disruption.", "teach": {"body": "A memorised script is stored as a chain, each sentence cueing the next. That is why it is fragile. A late arrival, a microphone that pops, a dignitary's phone in the second row, and one link breaks; the speaker stands searching for an exact word while the room watches the search. Reading avoids the break but costs the room: the eyes go down, the intonation flattens into the read-aloud contour, and the timing becomes the page's rather than yours. Neither suits a ceremony, a conference address or a valedictory, where the room expects both preparation and presence.\n\nAnchors are sturdier because they are stored differently. An anchor is a short phrase or image holding one section of the speech. You memorise the anchors and their order, not the words between them; under each you know what you want to say, and you say it fresh. If a sentence goes, the speech does not. You move to the next anchor, and the room never knows.\n\nThree is the working number. It sits comfortably inside a stressed working memory, it gives a speech a beginning, a development and an end, and if one anchor goes you can still find the other two. Five is the ceiling for a long address. Beyond that you have rebuilt the script in shorthand.\n\nMemorise two things verbatim: the first sentence and the last. The serial position effect, among the oldest findings in memory research, shows that beginnings and endings are retained best, by your audience as well as by you. The opening is also where arousal peaks; with the first sentence ready, the articulators know their job while the breath settles. The closing sentence gives you somewhere to land, so the speech ends on a deliberate fall rather than \"so, yes, thank you\".\n\nThe method of loci keeps the order. Cicero attributes it to Simonides of Ceos, and Eleanor Maguire's study of memory champions found that most of them relied on exactly this kind of spatial route rather than on exceptional native memory. Choose a route you know physically, your own front door, hallway and kitchen, and place one vivid image for each anchor along it. To recall the speech, walk the route. Spatial memory holds up well under stress, and it gives you the order for free.\n\nSome occasions call for a hybrid, and judging which is part of the craft. A formal citation for an honorary award, the exact words of a eulogy about the person, a statement the chair must make in prescribed form at an annual general meeting: these are read, openly and well, because exactness is the courtesy. The anchors then govern only what surrounds them.\n\nUnder pressure the failures are predictable. Anchor compression: adrenaline accelerates you through the middle anchor, which is usually the substance, and the speech comes out short and thin. Anchor drift: anchors written as abstractions (\"impact\", \"legacy\") that cue nothing when you need them; write them as images or claims. The chain break inside an anchor, where you reach for the rehearsed wording rather than any wording. And the elongated close, where relief arrives early and you keep talking past the sentence you prepared.\n\nRehearse aloud, always, and differently each time. If the middle comes out in the same words on the third run, you have memorised a script; change it deliberately until it does not. Then time it, because a speech delivered from anchors under adrenaline usually runs faster than it did in the kitchen, and the room's patience is set by the program, not by you.", "model": "Good evening, | and *welcome* to the final night of the festival. || Tonight a whole season's work is distilled into *six* performances. || Some of these players began the year unable to read a chart; | tonight they will *swing* one. || None of it happens without the teachers who arrange, | drive, | and stay *late*, | and without the families who make the time. || Listen generously, | and thank you for being *here*.", "mistake": "Good evening, and welcome to the final night. Before we start, a word on how the program came together, because the scheduling this year was genuinely complicated, two venues, adjudicators arriving on different days, which meant, and this is worth knowing, that the junior bands moved to the afternoon, so, anyway, six bands tonight, and my thanks to the teachers and the families, and please enjoy the music.", "remember": ["Memorise the first and last sentences verbatim.", "Hold three anchors along a route you know.", "Write anchors as images or claims, not abstractions.", "Say the middle fresh every time.", "Land on the prepared close, then stop."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-5-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Lock the opening sentence", "how": "Say each opening four times: slowly; at speaking pace; with the stress moved to a different word; and with your eyes off the phone, to the far wall. By the fourth it should arrive without effort and fall cleanly at the end.", "items": ["Good evening, and welcome to the final night of the festival.", "Thank you, Chair; I'll take the report as read and speak to three figures.", "I'd like to begin by acknowledging the Whadjuk Noongar people, on whose country we meet.", "It is a privilege to present this award, and a pleasure to explain why.", "Good morning; I'm here on behalf of the syllabus committee with one request.", "We are here to remember a colleague who made this program what it is."], "checks": ["Did the fourth repetition come without looking?", "Did moving the stress change the emphasis without breaking the sentence?", "Did the final word fall to the bottom of my range every time?", "Was the pace of the fourth run chosen, not rushed?"]}, {"id": "thought-5-d2", "type": "recall", "title": "Three anchors, in order", "how": "Read the anchors in the prep time and place each one in a room of your house as an image. Then speak the talk from memory, in order, in your own words.", "items": ["Welcome / a season distilled into one night / the teachers behind the bands", "The festival result / why the surplus matters / what the board decides tonight", "What the program asks of a family / what three years gives a child / how selection works", "The proposal / what the consultation showed / the recommendation to the committee", "The award / the moment the recipient's work changed the program / why tonight", "The question the panel was asked / what we found / what it means for schools"], "prep": 10, "seconds": 50, "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did all three anchors appear in order?", "Did I use my own words rather than the anchor wording?", "Did I move to each new anchor without a filler at the join?", "Did the middle anchor get its full share of time rather than being compressed?"]}, {"id": "thought-5-d3", "type": "recall", "title": "Fixed ends, fresh middle", "how": "The first and last items are verbatim sentences; say them exactly. The middle three are anchors; say them fresh, then stop on the closing sentence.", "items": ["Good evening, and welcome to the final night of the festival. / a season distilled / the bands tonight / the teachers behind them / Listen generously, and thank you for being here.", "Thank you, Chair; I'll take the report as read and speak to three figures. / income against budget / the one cost that surprised us / the reserve / I recommend that the report be received.", "Thank you for the invitation to speak to this conference. / why public schools belong in this conversation / what a specialist program actually does / what we need from the sector / I'll leave you with that question.", "It is a privilege to present this award. / who the recipient is to the program / the work that earned it / what it changed / Please join me in congratulating her.", "We are here to remember a colleague who made this program what it is. / how I first met her / what she built / what we owe her / Thank you, for all of it."], "prep": 15, "seconds": 80, "targets": {"fillers": 2, "wpm": [125, 155]}, "checks": ["Was the opening sentence word for word?", "Was the closing sentence word for word, with nothing after it?", "Did the middle anchors arrive in order, in fresh words?", "Did the closing sentence end on a full fall?"]}, {"id": "thought-5-d4", "type": "recall", "title": "Recall under disruption", "how": "Speak from the anchors. After the first anchor, stop and count three seconds silently, as if a latecomer has crossed the front row, then resume at the second anchor without going back or apologising.", "items": ["Welcome / a year of firsts / the students who led it / the people behind them / goodnight", "The audition data / what changed in the format / the waiting list / the decision for council", "The motion / its cost / its risk / why I support it", "The award / the recipient's first year / the moment it turned / why tonight", "The panel's question / the evidence / the gap in the evidence / the recommendation"], "prep": 15, "seconds": 80, "targets": {"fillers": 2, "pauses": 1}, "checks": ["Did I resume at the next anchor rather than repeat the last?", "Was the silence clean, with no filler or apology after it?", "Did the remaining anchors arrive in order?", "Did I bring the final anchor down on a fall and stop?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage once, then put it away. Reduce it to a verbatim opening, three anchors as images and a verbatim close, place the anchors along a route in your house, and deliver it without the text; then deliver it a second time with the middle in entirely different words.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["Were the opening and closing sentences exact both times?", "Did the three anchors come in order both times?", "Were the two middles in genuinely different words?", "Did I deliver both without looking back at the text?"]}}, {"id": "thought-6", "title": "The unexpected question", "goal": "Meet an unexpected or loaded question with a composed pause, choose the answer and its shape, deal with any embedded premise, deliver it, and stop.", "teach": {"body": "The unexpected question is where a fast mind is most exposed. Before the question is finished you have five possible answers, three qualifications and a related episode from a decade ago. The pull is to start speaking before choosing, because silence feels like failure. In a senior room it is not. A composed pause before an answer sounds like consideration; an instant answer at speed sounds like reflex, and a rising pitch on it, like anxiety.\n\nBuy the seconds honestly. Nothing contrived is needed: look at the questioner, let the breath out, and wait. Or use a line that is true: \"There are two parts to that.\" \"Let me take the second part first.\" Restating the question is honest and useful, because it confirms the question and, done precisely, sets its terms: \"You're asking whether the fee will rise next year.\" What costs you is filling the seconds with \"um, so, look, I mean\": time bought dishonestly, audible to everyone, and visible on the meter.\n\nSpend the seconds on one decision: what is the answer, in one sentence? Then choose its shape. A fair question you did not expect takes the headline from the first unit, then PREP or TWO if there is more. A loaded, hostile or leading question takes ABA: acknowledge, bridge, answer. Acknowledge the concern briefly without accepting its premise (\"I understand why families worry about cost\"). Bridge with a short phrase (\"what I can tell you is\"). Answer with what you actually stand behind. Media training teaches ABA, and it is often used to evade. Used well it does the reverse: it lets you answer honestly without being steered into answering a different question.\n\nSenior questioners embed premises. \"Given that the program serves a small elite, how do you justify its cost?\" contains a claim you have not agreed to. Answer the justification and you have conceded the premise. Name it, briefly and without heat: \"I wouldn't accept that it serves an elite; it selects on aptitude, from every suburb we draw on.\" Then answer. The best senior public servants at Senate estimates do exactly this: they correct a premise in a sentence, at the bottom of their range, and move straight to the answer.\n\nAustralian practice also offers an honest exit that is too often forgotten. In parliamentary committees, and in most formal meetings that follow their conventions, a question can be taken on notice: \"I'll take that on notice and come back to the committee by Friday.\" It is not an evasion if you follow through. It is far better than speculating, and it is the right answer to a question whose honest answer is \"I don't know\". Say it plainly, commit to a time, and stop.\n\nThen stop. This is the hardest part. When the answer is complete, close it on a falling tone, hold the questioner's eye and allow the silence. The silence after an answer is not yours to fill. Speakers who continue tend to add a qualification that weakens what they said, or new material that opens a second line of questioning. On a panel, a slight nod and a look back to the moderator ends your turn; in the chair, \"next question\" does.\n\nUnder pressure the failures are recognisable. The run-up: three sentences of context before the answer arrives. Defensive escalation, where pitch and pace rise with each sentence. The conceding acknowledgement (\"you're right that it's expensive, but\"), which hands over the premise in the first clause. And answering the question you wished had been asked, which every attentive room notices and none forgets.", "model": "|| You're asking whether the fee will *rise*. || I understand that cost is a real concern for families, | and I'd gently set aside the suggestion that it's already been decided: | it hasn't. || What I can tell you is | the council has *frozen* it for next year, | and any change after that would come to this body *first*. || That's the position. ||", "mistake": "That's a fair question, and I think it's important to say that fees are something the council takes very seriously, and there's been a good deal of discussion, including in the review a few years ago, so I wouldn't want to pre-empt anything, but my understanding is that it's frozen for next year, at least for the moment.", "remember": ["Pause before the first word; silence reads as thought.", "Find the one-sentence answer before you speak.", "Name a false premise briefly, then answer.", "Loaded question: acknowledge, bridge, answer.", "Answer, fall, stop and hold the look."]}, "drills": [{"id": "thought-6-d1", "type": "hold", "title": "The composed pause", "how": "Read each question silently and hold the silence without moving or filling it. Say a one-sentence answer aloud, then hold the silence again after it.", "items": ["Why is Music asking for more than any other learning area?", "Is the festival financially sustainable beyond next year?", "What is the weakest part of the draft syllabus?", "What will you do if the specialist program's funding is withdrawn?", "Are external practical examinations fair to regional candidates?", "Why should this panel fund a school-sector organisation at all?"], "hold": 2, "targets": {"fillers": 0, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did I hold the first silence without a filler, an audible breath or a shift of posture?", "Was the answer a single sentence?", "Did the answer end on a fall?", "Did I hold the second silence rather than add to the answer?"]}, {"id": "thought-6-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Filled time to bought time", "how": "Say the weak opening, then the strong one. Hear the difference between filling time and buying it, and between conceding a premise and declining it.", "items": ["Um, look, that's a really good question, and I think, I mean => There are two parts to that.", "I'm not entirely sure, I'd have to check, I probably should know this => I'll take that on notice. | You'll have it by Friday.", "Well, it's complicated, there's a lot of history to it, and => The short answer is no. | The history explains why.", "You're right that it's expensive, but => I understand the concern about cost. | What I can tell you is", "I suppose I'd question the premise a little, if that's all right => I wouldn't accept that framing.", "Right, so, I hadn't anticipated that one, but off the top of my head => You're asking whether the program will continue."], "checks": ["Did the strong version contain no fillers or softeners?", "Did the strong version concede nothing I don't accept?", "Was the strong version at the lower end of my range and unhurried?", "Was it honest about what I actually know?"]}, {"id": "thought-6-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Premise, bridge, answer", "how": "Acknowledge the concern in a single clause without accepting the premise, name the premise if it is false, bridge in a short phrase, give the answer you stand behind, then stop.", "items": ["A council member: given the program serves a small elite, how do you justify its staffing?", "An SCSA committee member: isn't a practical examination simply a test of who can afford private lessons?", "A journalist: is it true the festival nearly folded last year?", "A university academic: why should we listen to the school sector on entry requirements when its graduates arrive underprepared?", "A parent at the annual general meeting: isn't the three-year commitment just a way of protecting enrolment numbers?", "A funding panel member: why should public money support jazz, a niche genre, over community music?"], "structure": "ABA", "prep": 5, "seconds": 35, "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did the acknowledgement avoid conceding the premise?", "If the premise was false, did I name it briefly and without heat?", "Did the answer address the real question rather than a safer one?", "Did I stop as soon as the answer was complete?"]}, {"id": "thought-6-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Cold questions, clean stops", "how": "Take each question cold. Buy two seconds honestly, choose the shape yourself (a headline, PREP, ABA, or taking it on notice), answer, then stop and hold the look for two seconds.", "items": ["The principal, in the corridor: what would you change if you led the arts learning area?", "A university panel chair: what is the single biggest misconception about school music?", "A council member: you are also treasurer of the festival association. Isn't that a conflict when council considers festival matters?", "A conference delegate: if you could rewrite one line of the syllabus, which would it be?", "An SCSA officer: can you give the committee the current figures on Year 12 music enrolments in public schools?", "A board member, after your report: are you confident in these numbers?"], "structure": "ANY", "prep": 2, "seconds": 40, "targets": {"fillers": 1, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did I pause before the first word?", "Was the shape I chose right for the question, including taking it on notice where I could not know?", "Did I stop as soon as the answer was complete, on a fall?", "Did I hold the look through the silence after?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Read the scenario as though the question has just been put to you by the most senior person in the room. Pause, identify any embedded premise, answer aloud with ABA or a headline, then stop. Deliver it again, cutting everything after the answer.", "seconds": 40, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did I buy the time without fillers?", "Did I identify and deal with any premise before answering?", "Did the answer arrive within my first two sentences?", "Was the second attempt shorter, and did it end in silence?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Words", "intro": "This strand trains the words themselves, at the standard of rooms where everyone is articulate: when to leave silence, how to qualify without retreating, which word is exact, which register the room is in, what to say when the moment turns hard, and which forms make a sentence outlast the meeting. In a committee or on a podium, authority is lost less through argument than through small leaks: the filler, the protective hedge, the placeholder noun, the over-marked refusal. These six units close them, one at a time.", "units": [{"id": "words-1", "title": "Silence Instead of Filler", "goal": "Hold the floor in a contested committee or on a podium with unfilled pauses, syntax, gaze and pitch, so that no um, so, look or I mean survives even under questioning.", "teach": {"body": "Disfluency is not noise. Herbert Clark and Jean Fox Tree showed in 2002 that uh and um behave like words: speakers select them, and they signal the expected length of a delay, uh a minor one, um a major one. Their function is interactional. In the turn-taking system that Sacks, Schegloff and Jefferson described, a point of syntactic and intonational completion is a place where someone else may legitimately take the floor. The filler is a claim on the floor across a gap that would otherwise be open.\n\nThat is why fillers survive in very able speakers. In a committee of articulate colleagues the floor is genuinely contested, and the speech planner reaches for the one floor-holding device it trusts. The cost is status. Frieda Goldman-Eisler's work on spontaneous speech found that hesitation clusters before words of high information content, the least predictable word in the phrase. A filled pause in that position tells the room you were searching. An unfilled pause in the same position sounds like selection.\n\nThe expert answer is to hold the floor by other means. Three work reliably. Syntax first: pause inside an unfinished structure, not at its end. 'The difficulty with the second proposal is | moderation' cannot be claimed in the gap, because the grammar has announced that more is coming. Pause after a full stop and you have offered the floor. Second, gaze and gesture: keep your eyes on the room and a hand held mid-gesture; a dropped gaze and a settled hand both signal yielding. Third, pitch: a phrase that ends level or slightly suspended projects continuation, where a full fall projects completion. Use the fall only when you mean to finish.\n\nThe pause has a physical shape. At the end of the phrase, close the lips and take the in-breath through the nose. The vocal folds abduct to let air in, so there is no voicing available and the um has nowhere to go. The nasal breath is also quiet, which matters at a lectern microphone, where an oral gasp sounds like nerves.\n\nUnder pressure the failure is predictable. Interrogating panels draw it out at three places: the first word of an answer, the seam between points, and immediately before a technical term or a proper name. Watch for the substitutes that careful speakers adopt once they have trained out um: the launching 'so', the stretched determiner ('theee'), the throat-clear, 'I mean', 'look', the tongue click. They are the same signal in different clothes. The launching 'so' has become close to universal among educated speakers as an answer opener; in a senior room it reads as a run-up. Begin on the content word.\n\nThe other pressure failure is the chained turn. 'And' and 'so' at clause boundaries keep a turn alive without ever completing it, and fillers breed at every link. Finish the sentence, land it, take the nasal breath, and begin the next sentence on its subject. If a colleague takes the floor in that gap, a competent chair will return it; if you are the chair, it is yours to return.\n\nTrain by catching, not by suppression. When you hear one, stop, close the lips, breathe, and say the sentence again from its first word. The repetition retrains the seam, and within a few weeks the nasal breath starts arriving there unbidden.", "model": "Thank you, Chair. || The difficulty with the second proposal | is *moderation*. || If schools set their own performance tasks, | we lose the common reference point | that makes a grade in Albany | mean what a grade in Joondalup *means*. || I'd keep the externally set task, | reduce it from three works to *two*, | and put the time we recover | into *consensus* moderation. || That is the trade | I'd recommend to the *committee*.", "mistake": "Thank you, Chair. So the difficulty, um, with the second proposal is really moderation, and, I mean, that's partly because if schools set their own tasks we lose the common reference point, and so a grade in Albany doesn't necessarily mean what it means in Joondalup, which actually connects to the wider question of how we use exemplars at all, so I'd, look, probably keep the external task, and maybe reduce it.", "remember": ["Pause inside the clause when you need thinking time.", "Close the lips; breathe through the nose.", "Keep gaze up and hand alive to hold the floor.", "Begin every answer on its content word.", "Finish the sentence; never chain with 'and' or 'so'."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-1-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Read the Rests", "how": "Read each item aloud, closing the lips and taking a silent nasal breath at every | and holding a full second at every ||. Nothing voiced in any gap: no throat-clear, no stretched vowel.", "items": ["Chair. || I'll speak to the *second* recommendation | and leave the first to Professor *Hall*.", "The draft syllabus asks for *three* assessment types. || The evidence supports *two*.", "Distinguished guests, | colleagues, | families. || Welcome to the *sixtieth* year of music at this school.", "The treasurer's report is in your *papers*. || Two items require a decision *tonight*.", "I've read the submission *closely*. || The case is strong. | The costing is *not*.", "Before we vote, | one point of *clarification*. || The figure on page four | covers *two* years, not one."], "targets": {"fillers": 0, "pauses": 3}, "checks": ["Was every gap free of voiced sound, including throat-clears and stretched vowels?", "Were your lips closed before each nasal in-breath, with no audible gasp?", "Did each || last at least a full second?", "Did the meter show zero fillers and at least three pauses per item?"]}, {"id": "words-1-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Strip the Run-Up", "how": "Say the weak line as written, then the strong line with a closed-lip pause wherever a filler or run-up stood. The strong line must begin on its subject or content word.", "items": ["So, um, I think the core issue here is really assessment validity. => The core issue | is *validity*.", "Thank you, Chair, and, um, just to build on what Margaret said, I mean, I sort of agree. => I agree with Margaret, | with one *reservation*.", "So the, uh, the budget position is, look, it's basically sound. => The budget position | is *sound*. || One line needs attention.", "So what we found, um, was that the, the moderation samples were inconsistent. => The moderation samples | were *inconsistent*. || Here is where.", "I mean, uh, the honest answer is, you know, we don't have that data yet. => We don't have that data *yet*. || We will by *March*.", "So, look, um, on behalf of the board, I'd like to, uh, welcome everyone. => On behalf of the board, | *welcome*."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did each strong line begin on its subject or content word, with no run-up sound?", "Was every pause in the strong line unvoiced?", "Did the strong line fall in pitch onto its starred word and stop?", "Was the strong line at least a third shorter in time than the weak one?"]}, {"id": "words-1-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Silence at the Seams", "how": "The lines form one answer to a panel. Say each line, then hold silence for the full count with lips closed and gaze up, signalling that you have not finished, before the next line.", "items": ["The short answer is *no*.", "The proposed weighting rewards a skill the course does not *teach*.", "Students with private tuition arrive with it; | students without it *don't*.", "So the weighting would measure the tuition, | not the *course*.", "There is a better instrument for what the panel wants: | a set *aural* task.", "I'd retain the current weighting | and review it after one *cohort*."], "hold": 2, "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did you hold every silence for the full two seconds, lips closed?", "Did any line begin with so, and, look or a stretched vowel?", "Did your gaze stay up through each hold?", "Did only the final line end on a full, conclusive fall?"]}, {"id": "words-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Clean Under Questioning", "how": "Answer each question as if a panel member has just put it to you and others are ready to come in. Begin on a content word, pause inside clauses rather than filling, and stop when the point is restated.", "items": ["A panel member asks: why should performance carry more weight than the written examination?", "The principal asks: what would you protect first if the Music budget fell by a fifth?", "A university committee asks: what does a first-year music student most often lack?", "A board member asks: why should the association fund a youth stage that loses money?", "A conference delegate asks: what does selective entry actually select for?", "A funding panel asks: why is your program better placed to deliver this than a tertiary institution?"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 5, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 1, "pauses": 6}, "checks": ["Was your first word a content word, with no 'so', 'look' or 'I mean'?", "Did the meter show one filler or none?", "When you needed thinking time, did you pause mid-clause rather than after a full stop?", "Did every sentence finish before the next began, with no 'and' chaining?", "Did you stop on the restated point, with no trailing 'so yeah'?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver the passage once from the page. Then deliver it from memory as if to a panel free to interrupt, holding the floor at every seam with syntax, gaze and a silent nasal breath, never with sound.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["Did the from-memory version contain no fillers and no run-up 'so'?", "Did you pause inside clauses at the points where you were retrieving the next idea?", "Was there no audible breath at the microphone?", "Did you end on a completed sentence, a full fall and silence?"]}}, {"id": "words-2", "title": "Qualify Without Retreating", "goal": "Separate epistemic qualification from self-protective hedging, so that every claim and request is either stated plainly or bounded by one exact, localised qualifier, with no boosters to compensate.", "teach": {"body": "Hedging is not one habit. Robin Lakoff, in Language and Woman's Place (1975), grouped hedges, tag questions and rising declaratives as markers of tentativeness. William O'Barr and colleagues then tested the cluster in courtroom testimony: the same evidence delivered in what they called a 'powerless' style, with hedges, hesitation forms, intensifiers and questioning intonation, was judged less credible and its witness less competent than the 'powerful' version, for male and female witnesses alike. The content was identical. The verdict on the speaker was not.\n\nThe academic complication is real, and a senior room will expose anyone who ignores it. In scholarly discourse, hedging is a norm, not a weakness. Ken Hyland's work on academic writing shows hedges doing precise epistemic work: marking the limits of evidence, deferring to the field, inviting ratification. A professor who said 'this proves' where 'this suggests' was warranted would lose the room. Stripping hedges wholesale is the wrong lesson. The skill is to distinguish two kinds.\n\nEpistemic qualifiers bound the claim, and they belong to the evidence: 'on one year of moderation data', 'in the schools we sampled'. Affective hedges protect the speaker, and they belong to the nerves: just, I think, sort of, maybe, I'm not sure if this is right but, sorry, does that make sense. The first kind raises your standing in an academic room. The second lowers it everywhere. The test: delete the word and ask what changes. If the scope of the claim changes, it was epistemic and it stays. If only your exposure changes, it was affective, and it goes.\n\nThe elite form is the localised qualifier. Diffuse doubt ('I think it might possibly be worth considering') spreads uncertainty across the whole proposition. A localised qualifier commits to the claim and names its exact boundary: 'The weighting should move to sixty per cent. The one thing I can't yet show is the effect on regional schools; I'll have it for the November meeting.' That is more honest than fog, and it gives the room one specific thing to test, which is how serious rooms extend trust.\n\nHyland's counterpart to the hedge is the booster: clearly, obviously, undoubtedly, I strongly believe. Capable speakers who have trained out hedging often swing into boosting under pressure, and among academics an unearned 'clearly' invites the question 'clear to whom?'. The booster is the hedge's anxious twin. Use neither unless the evidence asks for it.\n\nRequests have their own solution. Meeting procedure supplies performative verbs that carry authority without aggression: I move, I propose, I recommend, I'd ask the committee to note. They make the minimiser redundant. 'I move that the report be received' does not need 'just'.\n\nThe sound matters as much as the vocabulary. The high rising terminal is well documented in Australian English, and in conversation it does friendly, checking work. On a recommendation it converts a claim into a request for permission. The strong version steps down onto its last stressed syllable and stops. Then the silence. In a committee, the beat after a falling statement is where the room either accepts the claim or takes it up, and either outcome is fine. Filling that beat with 'if that makes sense' is the one response that is not.\n\nThe pressure failure is cumulative: one hedge licenses the next, and by the third sentence the recommendation has dissolved. Catch the first.", "model": "Chair, | I move that the performance weighting rise to *sixty* per cent. || The reason is *validity*: | the written paper currently decides grades | in a course whose outcomes are *performed*. || The one thing I can't yet show | is the effect on *regional* schools, | where access to accompanists is thinner. || I'll have those figures | for the *November* meeting. || I'd ask the committee | to support the motion in *principle* tonight.", "mistake": "Chair, I think, and I may be wrong on the detail, there's possibly a case, at least on validity grounds, and subject to the regional figures, which I haven't fully seen, for considering whether the performance weighting might move towards something like sixty per cent, though obviously that's a matter for the committee. Does that seem reasonable?", "remember": ["Delete the word; keep it only if the claim's scope changes.", "Bound the doubt in one place, with a date.", "Use move, propose, recommend instead of minimisers.", "Drop boosters along with hedges.", "Fall onto the last stressed syllable, then hold silence."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-2-d1", "type": "swap", "title": "Delete the Affective Hedge", "how": "Say the hedged line, then the strong line. Keep any qualifier that bounds the claim, drop every one that only protects you, and let the strong line fall onto its starred word.", "items": ["I just wanted to quickly flag a possible concern with the costing? => One concern | with the *costing*.", "I think we should probably defer this to the next meeting. => I move that we *defer* this to November. || The figures aren't in.", "Sorry, I'm not sure if this is right, but the denominator looks wrong. => The denominator on page six | is *wrong*. || It counts withdrawals.", "It's clearly, obviously the strongest submission we've had. => It's the strongest submission | this *round*.", "I guess my view is sort of that the syllabus is overloaded. => The syllabus is *overloaded*: | four units of content | in three terms of *time*.", "Does that make sense, or should I explain it differently? => *Questions*?", "I was wondering whether you might possibly have time to meet? => I'd like *thirty minutes* with you before the board meets."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the strong line keep every qualifier that bounds the claim and drop every one that protects you?", "Did the strong line contain no just, sorry, I think, clearly or obviously?", "Did it step down in pitch onto the starred word and stop?", "Did you hold a beat of silence before the next item?"]}, {"id": "words-2-d2", "type": "repeat", "title": "Land the Recommendation", "how": "Say each line three times. First with a high rising terminal, as if seeking permission. Second level. Third stepping down onto the starred word, then a full second of silence.", "items": ["I recommend we retain the external *task*.", "The motion is *carried*.", "The board should not underwrite a second *night*.", "The evidence supports *two* assessment types, | not three.", "The association's accounts are in *order*.", "I don't support the *amendment*."], "checks": ["Could you hear three distinct endings across the passes?", "On the third pass, did the meter show no end-of-phrase rise?", "Did the third pass sound like a decision a minute-taker would record?", "Did you hold the silence after the third pass without adding anything?"]}, {"id": "words-2-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "The Performative Request", "how": "Make each request in a single sentence built on a performative verb (move, propose, recommend, ask), add one reason, and stop. No apology, no minimiser, no checking tag.", "items": ["Move, at the school board, that the specialist music levy be held at this year's rate.", "Ask the principal to protect the program's timetable lines in next year's school plan.", "Ask an SCSA panel chair to have your dissent from the recommendation minuted.", "Ask the festival board to approve an unbudgeted fee for an interstate adjudicator.", "Ask a university colleague to withdraw, in the meeting, a claim they made about your students.", "Ask a funding panel to reconsider an application it has already declined."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 5, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did your first sentence contain a performative verb and the request itself?", "Did you give exactly one reason?", "Did you avoid every minimiser, apology and checking tag?", "Did you stop without softening the close or rising at the end?"]}, {"id": "words-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Calibrated Certainty", "how": "Give a recommendation on each question in your first sentence. Then name exactly one bounded unknown and when it will be resolved. No other hedge and no booster anywhere.", "items": ["Should Year 12 composition be assessed by portfolio rather than examination?", "Should the association move the festival to a single venue next year?", "Should selective music entry include a written aural task as well as an audition?", "Should the school board endorse the principal's proposed timetable model?", "Should a university performance degree weight the ATAR Music result in its entry decisions?", "Should the department replace paper moderation with recorded samples?"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 10, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Was the recommendation your first sentence, with no preamble?", "Did you name one bounded unknown and the date it will be resolved?", "Did you avoid boosters such as clearly and obviously as well as hedges?", "Did every sentence fall at the end, including the qualifier?"]}], "apply": {"source": "pair", "task": "Say the weak version, then the strong version. Then deliver the strong version twice more: once for an academic committee, keeping one precise epistemic qualifier, and once for a board, with none. Each version must be shorter than the last.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did every version remove every affective hedge from the weak one?", "Did the academic version's single qualifier bound the claim rather than protect you?", "Was the board version the shortest and still courteous?", "Did every version end on a falling full stop?"]}}, {"id": "words-3", "title": "The Exact Word", "goal": "Speak at the bottom rungs of abstraction without a script: owned technical terms kept, placeholder abstractions replaced, numbers given with denominators, and verbs freed from nominalisation.", "teach": {"body": "S. I. Hayakawa's ladder of abstraction is the right instrument. At the bottom is the thing you could point at: one Year 12 recital, marked a full grade apart by two experienced markers. Higher: inconsistency in moderation. Higher again: quality assurance. At the top: standards. Each rung covers more and shows less. Senior rooms drift upward because abstraction is safe, collegial and hard to contest. It is also forgettable. The concreteness effect is among the most robust findings in memory research, and Allan Paivio's dual-coding account explains it: concrete words can be held both verbally and as an image, abstract ones largely only verbally. 'Quality assurance concerns' is gone by the next item. 'One recital, two markers, a grade apart' is still in the room at the next meeting.\n\nPrecision in a senior room is not plainness. In an SCSA panel, 'standards-referenced', 'construct validity' and 'consensus moderation' are exact and owned, and replacing them with homely words would cost precision and standing at once. The target is the placeholder abstraction: rigour, quality, engagement, excellence, outcomes, support. These words feel substantive and commit to nothing. The test is whether a colleague could disagree with you. 'We need more rigour' is unfalsifiable. 'The current task can be passed without reading a score' can be argued with, which is what makes it worth saying.\n\nNumbers have their own discipline. Gerd Gigerenzer's work on risk communication shows that people reason far better with natural frequencies than with percentages or conditional probabilities. 'Nine in ten' outperforms 'ninety per cent' for the ear, and 'twelve of our forty students' outperforms both when the denominator matters. Always supply the denominator and the comparison: a fifty per cent rise from two students to three is a different fact from a fifty per cent rise from two hundred to three hundred. Committees with statisticians on them notice relative figures offered without absolutes, and they draw conclusions about the speaker. One number per sentence is about what a listener can hold.\n\nThe verb should do the work. Michael Halliday called the turning of processes into nouns grammatical metaphor; the plainer term is nominalisation. It is the native grammar of policy: 'the implementation of a review of assessment provision'. In writing it packs information densely, which is why academics use it. Spoken, it is costly: the listener has to unpack each noun back into a process in real time, and no one in the sentence is doing anything. Pull the verb out and give it a subject: 'The panel will review how schools assess performance.' Orwell's rules in 'Politics and the English Language' hold as a spoken discipline: the short word where it is as exact, the active voice where the actor matters, the cut where nothing is lost.\n\nEnglish tends to place new information late, a pattern grammarians call end focus, and the nuclear stress of an intonation phrase falls on its last content word by default. Put the exact word last and the stress falls on it without effort: not 'Twelve is the number who withdrew', but 'The number who withdrew was twelve.' Exact words need less vocal help, so you can drop volume and still be heard.\n\nUnder pressure the climb is involuntary. A hostile question pushes speakers up the ladder, towards the abstract and unassailable, at exactly the moment the room wants evidence. When you hear yourself say 'a range of factors', stop for a beat and name one.", "model": "Last year | two experienced markers | placed the same Year 12 recital | a full *grade* apart. || That is not a training problem. || The marking key describes *qualities*, | tone, phrasing, control, | without saying what each sounds like at each *band*. || I propose that the panel | record an *exemplar* at every band | and attach it to the key. || The cost is two days of panel *time*.", "mistake": "There are some ongoing concerns around consistency in the moderation space, and I think we need to look at strengthening the quality assurance framework to ensure greater rigour and better outcomes across the system going forward.", "remember": ["Keep owned technical terms; replace placeholder abstractions.", "Ask whether a colleague could disagree with it.", "Give every number its denominator and comparison.", "Free the verb and give it a subject.", "Put the exact word last."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-3-d1", "type": "swap", "title": "Free the Verb", "how": "Say the nominalised line, then the line with the verb freed and an actor named. Let the stress fall on the freed verb or the final content word.", "items": ["The committee will undertake a review of the implementation of the new assessment requirements. => The committee will review | how schools are *assessing* the new tasks.", "There has been a reduction in the level of participation in regional areas. => Fewer regional students are *enrolling*.", "The provision of feedback to schools will occur following the completion of moderation. => We'll send schools feedback | once moderation *ends*.", "A decision was made to postpone consideration of the proposal. => The board *postponed* the proposal.", "Our recommendation is the adoption of a consensus moderation model. => We recommend | *consensus* moderation.", "Ensuring the alignment of the syllabus with tertiary expectations is a priority. => We want the syllabus | to produce students a university can *teach*."], "checks": ["Did each strong line have a named actor and a finite verb?", "Was each strong line at least a third shorter?", "Did the nuclear stress fall on the freed verb or the final content word?", "Did you keep any owned technical term the weak line contained?"]}, {"id": "words-3-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "One Rung Down", "how": "Say the abstract line, then the concrete one. The concrete line must be something a colleague could dispute on the facts, and it must end on its image or number.", "items": ["There are some equity concerns with the proposed audition model. => Students without private teachers | have never seen a *sight-reading* test.", "Applications have increased significantly. => *Sixty-two* students applied, | up from *forty*.", "The festival delivered strong community outcomes. => Eleven schools played, | and four of them had never *entered* before.", "There are rigour issues with the current task. => A student can pass the task | without reading a *score*.", "Resourcing remains a challenge for the department. => We have one practice room | for *ninety* students.", "Graduates of the program do well. => Of last year's twenty-four graduates, | *nine* went on to tertiary music."], "checks": ["Could a colleague dispute each concrete line on the facts?", "Did each number come with a denominator or a comparison?", "Did each concrete line end on its exact word?", "Did you say the concrete line more quietly and still sound more certain?"]}, {"id": "words-3-d3", "type": "recall", "title": "Concrete Anchors", "how": "Read the anchors during prep, then deliver the contribution in order once they disappear, as if to the board or panel named in the first anchor. Every anchor must come out as its exact noun or number, never a vaguer substitute.", "items": ["Moderation panel / two markers / the same recital a grade apart / an exemplar at every band / two days of panel time", "Festival board / eleven schools / a deficit of four thousand dollars / adjudicator fees doubled / a sponsor for the youth stage", "Selection committee / sixty-two applicants / thirty-four places / nine from schools with no music teacher / a written aural task", "Principal's office / one practice room / ninety students / two students practising in a stairwell / the old library annexe", "Program review / twenty-four graduates / nine into tertiary music / four into teaching / renewal for three years"], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did every number come out exactly as written, with its denominator where one was given?", "Did you avoid every placeholder abstraction (quality, rigour, engagement, outcomes)?", "Did the anchors come out in order?", "Did your final sentence end on a concrete word?"]}, {"id": "words-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Describe It Exactly", "how": "Speak on each item using at least three details the room could picture or verify and one number with a denominator and a comparison. Any abstraction you use must be made concrete in the same sentence.", "items": ["Tell an SCSA panel what a weak Year 12 performance examination actually sounds like.", "Tell the school board what the specialist program costs per student, and what that buys.", "Describe to a university committee what your strongest graduate could do on arrival.", "Tell a funding panel what its last grant changed, in observable terms.", "Describe to the principal what the music wing looks like at seven-thirty on a Tuesday morning.", "Tell a conference what separates a very good student performer from an exceptional one."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 10, "structure": "WSN", "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did you give at least three details a listener could picture or check?", "Did you give one number with both a denominator and a comparison?", "Did every abstraction you used become concrete in the same sentence?", "Did you use no nominalised verb (conduct a review, make a decision)?"]}], "apply": {"source": "fact", "task": "Read the fact. State it three ways: as a natural frequency for a parent body, as a figure with denominator and comparison for a board, and in one sentence for an academic committee that keeps any warranted qualifier. Put the number last each time.", "seconds": 45, "checks": ["Did the number come at the end of each sentence?", "Did the parent version use a natural frequency rather than a percentage?", "Did the board version give both the denominator and a comparison?", "Did the academic version keep the qualifier the source warrants, and no more?"]}}, {"id": "words-4", "title": "Register and Tenor", "goal": "Read the tenor of a mixed senior room and pitch vocabulary, sentence length, formality and pace to it from the first sentence, without drifting upward under status pressure.", "teach": {"body": "Register is the fit between language and situation. Michael Halliday's three variables remain the cleanest analysis: field, what is being talked about; tenor, the relationship between the participants, including status and distance; and mode, the channel and how prepared the language is. Martin Joos's The Five Clocks offers a complementary scale of formality, from frozen through formal, consultative and casual to intimate. Most senior speakers get field right. The errors are in tenor and mode.\n\nYour rooms are unusually varied in tenor. An SCSA panel mixes at least three registers: the bureaucratic (outcomes, implementation, stakeholders), the academic (construct, validity, evidence base) and the practitioner's (what a Year 11 cellist can actually do in March). The speaker who carries such a room usually speaks the practitioner's content with the academic's precision, and translates the bureaucratic into plain process. The speaker who fails tries to out-bureaucrat the bureaucrats. A board has its own register: fiduciary, risk-aware, brief. A university committee rewards the qualifier that marks evidence and penalises the hedge that marks nerves. A ceremony sits closest to Joos's frozen style: fixed forms, an Acknowledgement of Country given in its settled words at an unhurried pace, names and post-nominals said in full and correctly.\n\nHoward Giles's communication accommodation theory names the ways to miss. Convergence, moving your speech towards the listener's, generally earns approval. Over-accommodation, converging further than the listener expects, comes across as patronising. Under-accommodation, failing to move at all, suggests indifference or rank. The parent body that hears 'consensus moderation' has been under-accommodated. The panel member who receives your most folksy register in reply to a technical question has been over-accommodated, and suspects you of avoiding the point.\n\nThe specific pressure failure for highly intelligent speakers is upward drift. William Labov used 'hypercorrection' for speakers who, conscious of a prestige norm, overshoot it. In a room of very articulate people, status anxiety produces the spoken equivalent: longer sentences, Latinate vocabulary, stacked subordinate clauses, 'in terms of' and 'in relation to' doing work that 'about' would do better. It is the register of someone auditioning for the room. The people who own a room tend to speak more plainly than the people trying to join it.\n\nA big word earns its place when it is exact and the room owns it: 'quorum' to a board, 'hemiola' in a composition panel, 'ultra vires' when the association's lawyer is present. It also earns its place when the occasion asks for elevation, which ceremonies do: 'we honour' rather than 'we want to say thanks'. It loses its place when a plainer word is equally exact, when the room does not own it, or when its function is to show that you do. Ask whether the most senior person in the room would use it unprompted.\n\nRegister lives in the body as well as the lexicon. Formal and frozen registers are slower, with longer phrases, wider pitch movement on the nucleus and fuller vowels. Consultative register, the committee's natural home, is a little quicker, with shorter phrases and more decisive falls. Shift pace and phrase length first; vocabulary tends to follow.\n\nMode matters too. A paper read aloud carries written grammar into a spoken channel, and dense nominal groups that work on the page become unprocessable at speaking pace. If you must read, rewrite for the ear.\n\nThe constant across every room is you: judgement, values and dry humour stay fixed. Vocabulary, sentence length, formality and pace move.", "model": "The draft says the course will *leverage* authentic assessment | to enhance student outcomes. || In practice, | that should mean one thing. || A student performs a program | that a teacher at another school | could mark the *same* way. || That's the test I'd apply to every task in this draft. || Could two markers | who have never met | agree on the *grade*? || Where they couldn't, | the task needs a *clearer* descriptor, | not a longer one.", "mistake": "In terms of the authentic assessment component, I'd suggest that the efficacy of the proposed tasks is contingent upon the degree to which inter-rater reliability can be operationalised across a diversity of school contexts.", "remember": ["Read the tenor before choosing a word.", "Practitioner content, academic precision, plain process.", "Under status pressure, shorten the sentence.", "Use the big word only when exact and owned.", "Change pace and phrase length first."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-4-d1", "type": "swap", "title": "Translate Across Tenor", "how": "Say the first version, then the version for the room named in brackets. Keep the meaning exact while changing vocabulary, sentence length and pace.", "items": ["(Parent body) Students in the specialist program are subject to a three-year enrolment commitment. => When your child joins the program, | they commit to *three* years, | Year 7 to Year 9.", "(Board) The festival's financial sustainability is contingent on diversification of revenue streams. => Most of our income is ticket sales. || One wet weekend | and we're in *deficit*.", "(SCSA panel) The kids just aren't getting enough time on the instrument. => Contact time for performance | falls short of the *standard* the syllabus sets.", "(Ceremony) Big thanks to Ruth for everything she's done for music here. => Tonight we *honour* Ruth Calder, | who led music at this school for *eighteen* years.", "(University committee) The task is a bit useless at telling good students from great ones. => The task doesn't *discriminate* | at the top of the range.", "(Conference) In terms of the operationalisation of inclusive pedagogical frameworks in the ensemble context. => Here is what inclusion looks like | in a fifty-piece concert *band*."], "checks": ["Did the second version use only words that room would use unprompted?", "Was the meaning identical between the two versions?", "Did your pace and phrase length change, not only the vocabulary?", "Did you still sound like yourself in both?"]}, {"id": "words-4-d2", "type": "repeat", "title": "One Matter, Three Rooms", "how": "Say each matter three times: to an SCSA panel, then to the school board, then to a parent body. Change vocabulary, sentence length and pace each time; keep every fact identical.", "items": ["The Year 12 performance examination is moving from November to October.", "The specialist program levy will rise next year.", "Internal moderation found the department's grades sitting above the state benchmark.", "A senior member of the department is leaving mid-year.", "The festival will run at a smaller venue next year.", "Selective entry will include a written aural task from next year."], "checks": ["Were the facts identical across all three versions?", "Did the panel version use owned technical terms and no bureaucratic filler?", "Was the board version the shortest, and framed by risk or cost?", "Did the parent version state what it means for their child, with no acronym?"]}, {"id": "words-4-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Read the Room Fast", "how": "Speak to the room named in each item, in its register from your first word. One point, said once, then stop.", "items": ["At a university academic board: introduce yourself and your interest in the agenda item on entry pathways.", "At a state conference plenary: open your session in the first fifteen seconds.", "At the school board: open the item on the specialist music levy.", "At the festival gala: introduce the interstate adjudicator from the stage.", "At the valedictory ceremony: introduce the recipient of the music prize.", "In the principal's office: raise a staffing gap before it reaches the executive."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 5, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did your first sentence already sound like that room?", "Did you use any word the most senior person there would not use unprompted?", "Was your length and pace right for the room?", "Did you stop once the point was made?"]}, {"id": "words-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Same News, Two Registers", "how": "Deliver the news to the first room, pause three seconds, then deliver it to the second. The second version must share every fact and not a single sentence with the first.", "items": ["The festival ran a deficit. First to the association's board, then to the audience on closing night.", "Moderation found your school marking high. First to the SCSA panel, then to your department.", "A long-serving colleague is retiring. First to the school board as a staffing matter, then at the valedictory ceremony.", "A university has dropped its music entry prerequisite. First to the academic committee, then to Year 10 parents.", "The program's funding has been renewed for three years. First to the funding body's panel, in thanks, then to your staff.", "The state honour ensemble has selected two of your students. First to the principal, then from the stage at the next concert."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 10, "structure": "WSN", "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did the two versions share every fact and no sentence?", "Did your pace and phrase length change between them?", "Did you avoid both over-accommodation and upward drift?", "Did you sound like the same person in both?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver the passage in its own register. Then deliver the same content to an SCSA panel in consultative register, and again from a festival stage between sets, each version in no more than two thirds of the previous one's time.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did the first version suit its occasion in pace and form?", "Did the panel version translate any bureaucratic phrasing into plain process?", "Was each version no more than two thirds the length of the one before, with nothing essential lost?", "Did every big word in any version pass the exact-and-owned test?"]}}, {"id": "words-5", "title": "Formulas for Hard Moments", "goal": "Retrieve and deliver stored, exact formulas for disagreeing, refusing, conceding, taking a question on notice, holding procedure, closing an item and interrupting, compressed to one account and delivered without delay.", "teach": {"body": "Acute stress degrades the faculty a hard moment needs. Amy Arnsten's work on the prefrontal cortex shows that the high catecholamine levels released under stress impair the working memory and flexible control on which spontaneous sentence planning depends, while more habitual responses are relatively spared. In practice, the adrenaline of a contested meeting makes the composed sentence harder to build and the rehearsed one easier to retrieve.\n\nLinguistics supplies the other half. Andrew Pawley and Frances Syder argued in 1983 that nativelike fluency rests on a large store of prefabricated sequences retrieved whole, and Alison Wray's account of formulaic language develops the point: stored chunks reduce processing load. Fluent speakers are not composing every sentence. They are retrieving phrases and fitting the joins. You know this from the bandstand: the vocabulary you can play at tempo is the vocabulary you have practised as units.\n\nConversation analysis explains why these moments go wrong for capable people. Anita Pomerantz and others described preference organisation: agreement, acceptance and compliance tend to be delivered promptly and plainly, while disagreement, refusal and rejection, the dispreferred responses, tend to be delayed, prefaced, mitigated and accounted for. In ordinary talk that structure is a courtesy. Under stress the capable professional over-marks it: a long delay, three prefaces ('look', 'I hear you', 'to be fair'), two or three accounts, a trailing softener. The room reads the over-marking as weakness, and every additional account is another point to contest. The elite form keeps the structure and compresses it: one acknowledgement, one account, the position, no delay.\n\nThe bank, by move.\n\nDisagreeing: shared aim, difference, one reason. 'We want the same thing, a task that discriminates at the top. I don't think this one does, because it rewards preparation time.' Avoid 'with respect', which signals its opposite.\n\nRefusing: one no, one reason, one alternative where you have one. 'I can't support October. I can support the second week of November.' Then silence.\n\nConceding: specific, prompt, unhedged. 'You're right about the cost. I'd underestimated it.' A clean concession buys credibility for every point you hold.\n\nTaking a question on notice: the phrase is parliamentary, familiar from Senate Estimates, and it means you will not answer from memory what you can answer properly later. It requires a date, or it sounds like evasion. 'I'll take that on notice and circulate the figure before the next meeting.'\n\nHolding procedure: as a member, 'through the chair' and 'on a point of order' are formal access to the floor. Use them precisely and sparingly, never as weapons. As chair, close an item with summary, decision and transition: 'We'll leave it there. The motion is carried; the treasurer will report in March. Next item.' The words 'next item' do the closing. Say them, and turn the page.\n\nInterrupting: name first, then a reason that serves the room. 'Peter, I'll stop you there, because we're at time and I want Jane's view before we vote.' The name buys the floor; the reason makes it legitimate.\n\nDelivery: slightly slower and lower than the surrounding talk, a full fall on the last word, then a held beat. Rushed, these phrases sound defensive. At an even pace they sound like procedure, which is what they are. Rehearse them aloud until they are yours; a phrase you have only read silently is not yet one you can say at tempo.", "model": "Thank you, Professor *Lowe*. || We want the same thing: | a task that separates the very good from the *exceptional*. || I don't think this one does. || It rewards preparation *time*, | and preparation time follows *resources*. || You're right that the current task is too *long*, | and I'd support cutting it. || On the regional figures, | I'll take that *on notice* | and circulate them before the *March* meeting. || Chair, | I'm content to leave it *there*.", "mistake": "Look, with respect, Professor Lowe, I hear what you're saying, and I suppose that's partly fair about the length, but I'm not sure I'd totally agree, because there are a few issues around resourcing and timing and equity, so maybe we could come back to it?", "remember": ["One acknowledgement, one account, the position, no delay.", "Refuse with one reason and one alternative.", "Concede specifically, then hold every other point.", "Every 'on notice' carries a date.", "Close with the decision, then 'next item'."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-5-d1", "type": "read", "title": "The Phrase Bank", "how": "Read each formula slowly and a little lower than your speaking pitch, bringing the starred word down with a full fall and holding the stop. Repeat each until it comes out without looking.", "items": ["We want the same *thing*. || I see the route *differently*.", "I can't support *October*. | I can support *November*.", "You're *right* about the cost. || I'd underestimated it.", "I'll take that *on notice* | and circulate it before the next *meeting*.", "Through the chair, | a point of *clarification*.", "We'll leave it there. | The motion is *carried*. || Next *item*.", "Professor Lowe, | I'll stop you *there*, | because we're at time.", "I'd ask that my dissent be *minuted*."], "targets": {"fillers": 0, "wpm": [110, 140]}, "checks": ["Did you stay inside the pace range on every item?", "Did each formula end with a full fall and a held stop?", "Did any formula pick up a preface or softener you didn't read?", "Could you say each one with the screen hidden by the end?"]}, {"id": "words-5-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Compress the Dispreferred", "how": "Say the over-marked version, then the compressed one, starting it with no delay. Notice how many points of attack the first version offers and the second removes.", "items": ["Look, with respect, I hear what you're saying, but I'm not sure I'd totally agree. => I see it *differently*. || Here's why.", "I'd love to help, it's just really difficult at the moment with reports and the concert and everything. => I can't take it on this term. || I can next *term*.", "I suppose that's partly fair, in a way. => You're *right* about the timeline.", "I'm not sure off the top of my head, I'd have to check, maybe. => I'll take that on notice | and answer you by *Friday*.", "Should we maybe think about moving on, if everyone's okay with that? => The item is *deferred*. || Next item.", "Sorry, sorry, can I just come in there quickly? => Through the chair, | a *brief* point."], "checks": ["Did each compressed line contain at most one account?", "Was there no preface (look, I hear you) before the position?", "Did you begin the compressed line without a delay?", "Did the on-notice line carry a specific day?"]}, {"id": "words-5-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Hold After the No", "how": "Say each line to a specific person across the table, then hold silence for the full count with steady gaze. Do not soften, explain or add a second account in the silence.", "items": ["No. | The association won't underwrite a second *night*.", "I don't support the amendment, | and I'll be voting *against* it.", "That sits outside this committee's *remit*.", "You're right. | I had that figure *wrong*.", "I'll take that on notice | and circulate it by *Friday*.", "We're at time. || Next *item*."], "hold": 4, "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did you hold the full four seconds after every line?", "Did you resist adding a softener or second reason in the silence?", "Did your gaze stay level and on the person throughout?", "By the last line, did the silence feel like part of the sentence?"]}, {"id": "words-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Under Fire", "how": "Respond as the moment happens, using a formula from the bank within your first sentence. Acknowledge once, bridge to your position, give one account, then stop.", "items": ["A panel member says specialist music programs are an inequitable use of public money.", "A board member suggests the treasurer should have seen the deficit coming.", "A university colleague says your students arrive technically strong and musically naive.", "A board member keeps speaking well past time on an item you need to close as chair.", "The principal asks you to absorb a staffing cut without reducing the program.", "A journalist at the festival launch asks why fees rose when sponsorship also rose."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 8, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did you name the move (disagree, refuse, concede, on notice, close) in your first sentence?", "Did you acknowledge once, without apology or preface?", "Did you give one account rather than a list?", "Did you end on a falling statement and hold the silence?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Answer the scenario aloud as if the person is across the table. Use at least two different moves from the bank word for word, keep each dispreferred response to one account, and close with a decision, a date or 'next item'.", "seconds": 60, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did you use two bank formulas without hedging either?", "Did each disagreement or refusal carry exactly one account?", "Did you close with a decision, a date or 'next item'?", "Did you stay slower and lower than your conversational pace throughout?"]}}, {"id": "words-6", "title": "Forms Worth Owning", "goal": "Deploy tricolon, antithesis, anaphora, the rule of three, the callback and the short sentence after the long one by name and on purpose, dosed precisely for a deliberative room, a conference or a ceremony.", "teach": {"body": "Rhetorical devices are forms in the musician's sense: patterns the ear predicts and is satisfied to hear completed. Max Atkinson's analysis of political oratory in Our Masters' Voices, and John Heritage and David Greatbatch's study of British party conference speeches, found that a small number of structures, above all the contrast and the three-part list, reliably prompted audiences to applaud on cue. The finding cuts both ways. The forms work, and audiences have been trained by politicians to hear them, which is why an over-formed speech sounds like a politician.\n\nSix are worth owning.\n\nThe rule of three is the grouping principle: three items feel complete, two feel unfinished, four feel like a list. The tricolon is its tightest realisation, three parallel units of matched grammatical shape. Make the third the longest. Otto Behaghel observed that languages tend to place longer constituents later, his law of increasing members, and English end focus means the final unit takes the nucleus anyway. Lincoln's 'of the people, by the people, for the people' is matched in length; the spoken version that lifts a room usually rises.\n\nAntithesis sets opposed ideas in parallel grammar so each sharpens the other. Kennedy's line at Rice University, choosing the moon for its difficulty rather than its ease, is the canonical spoken example. Match the grammar across the halves exactly, and the contrasting words will carry contrastive accents without any push. Of the six, antithesis is the most committee-safe, because it does analytical work: it states a distinction the room has to deal with.\n\nAnaphora repeats the opening of successive clauses. It builds intensity and gives the listener a handle on structure. At a ceremony, three repetitions. In a deliberative room, two at most, and only for a list the room must retain.\n\nThe callback returns, near the close, to a phrase or image planted early. Comedians use the term; jazz musicians hear it as the head returning after the solos. It tells the room the speech had architecture and signals the ending without 'in conclusion'. Plant it plainly. Return with its meaning altered by what has happened in between.\n\nThe short sentence after the long one is the most useful form in any room. The long sentence suspends the listener across accumulating clauses. The short one resolves it, and as a single intonation phrase it takes full nuclear stress naturally. It is the downbeat after a long anacrusis.\n\nDose is register. In an SCSA panel or a board, one form per contribution, preferably antithesis or the short sentence; more than that sounds like a prepared speech in a deliberative room, and deliberative rooms distrust prepared speeches. At a conference keynote, two or three per section. At a ceremony, a toast or a eulogy, all six are available, provided each carries meaning. The diagnostic: if a listener could name the device before grasping the point, it is too much.\n\nThe pressure failures are specific. Under adrenaline, tempo rises and the pause before the third unit of a tricolon vanishes, so it falls flat. The prepared form is delivered verbatim while the surrounding sentences are improvised, and the change in fluency exposes it as scripted. The anaphora runs to four. The callback is forgotten, leaving the planted image unresolved, like a dominant that never goes home.\n\nMethod: draft the plain version. Find the one sentence that matters most. Give that sentence a form, and leave the rest plain so it can be heard.", "model": "In Year 7, | Amelia auditioned on a borrowed *viola*. || Since then she has led the string orchestra, | played under three conductors who did not always agree, | and, in a hall that lost power at the interval, | finished a Bach suite by the light of forty *phones*. || She isn't receiving this prize because she is *gifted*. | She's receiving it because she never stopped *listening*. || Six years ago, | a borrowed viola. || Tonight, | her *own*.", "mistake": "I'd like to say a few words about Amelia, who came to us in Year 7 and auditioned on viola, a borrowed one actually, and has since led the string orchestra, under, I think, three different conductors, and there was the concert where the power went, which some of you will remember, so I suppose what I'm saying is that this prize recognises her commitment over six years.", "remember": ["Draft plain; give the key sentence a form.", "Make the third unit the longest.", "Match the grammar across a contrast.", "One form per contribution in a deliberative room.", "Plant the callback early; return it changed."]}, "drills": [{"id": "words-6-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Name It, Then Say It", "how": "Say the device's name aloud, then read the line, stressing the starred words so the form is audible from the stress alone.", "items": ["Tricolon: | The syllabus should be *clear*, | *teachable*, | and fair to a student in a school with no music *teacher*.", "Antithesis: | The task measures *preparation*. | It should measure *musicianship*.", "Anaphora: | *For* the students who stayed late, | *for* the staff who stayed later, | *for* the families who drove them home.", "Short after long: | We spent the year rebuilding a timetable, rehousing two ensembles, renegotiating a venue and auditing six years of accounts no one had opened since the last treasurer resigned. || They *balance*.", "Callback: | We began tonight with a borrowed *viola*. || We end with her *own*.", "Antithesis: | We don't select students who can *play*. | We select students who can *learn*."], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Was each form audible from the stress pattern alone?", "In the tricolon, did the third unit carry the heaviest stress?", "In each antithesis, did the two stressed words contrast in pitch?", "Did the short sentence land with less effort than the long one?"]}, {"id": "words-6-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Give It a Form", "how": "Say the flat line, then the formed one. Name the device silently before you say the strong line, and check that the form serves the meaning rather than decorating it.", "items": ["The festival matters to students, families and the wider arts community. => The festival is where students *perform*, | families *listen*, | and a city remembers it has a jazz *scene*.", "The current task tests preparation more than musicianship. => The task rewards *preparation*. | It should reward *musicianship*.", "We need clearer descriptors, better exemplars and more moderation time. => We need clearer *descriptors*. | We need better *exemplars*. | And we need time for markers who have never met | to agree on a *grade*.", "It was a difficult year for the association but we're now in surplus. => It was a year of a lost venue, a resigned treasurer and a sponsor who withdrew in March. || We're in *surplus*.", "This award recognises sustained commitment. => This prize isn't for the most *gifted* player. | It's for the one who never *stopped*.", "Music education develops more than technical skill. => We teach students to *read*, | to *listen*, | and to walk onto a stage in front of a thousand people and *mean* it."], "checks": ["Could you name the device in each strong line?", "In each contrast, did both halves share the same grammar?", "Did every three-part line rise in length to its third unit?", "Did the strong line sound like you rather than a politician?"]}, {"id": "words-6-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Tune the Tricolon", "how": "Say each tricolon three times. First at an even pace. Second with a half-second pause before the third unit. Third slowing slightly through the third unit and stepping down in pitch onto its last word.", "items": ["Clear enough to *teach*, | rigorous enough to *trust*, | and fair to every student who *sits* it.", "To the *board*, | to the *sponsors*, | and to every volunteer who stacked chairs at *midnight*.", "We draft it, | we test it, | and then we let the schools *tell* us.", "They came *early*, | they stayed *late*, | and they played as if it *mattered*.", "Thank you for your *time*, | your *candour*, | and your patience with the *treasurer*."], "checks": ["Could you hear three distinct shapes across the passes?", "Did the pause before the third unit make it hit harder?", "On the third pass, did the final word fall in pitch?", "Did any pass tip into sounding theatrical for a board or panel?"]}, {"id": "words-6-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Build It Live", "how": "Speak on each occasion using at least two named devices and a callback from your first sentence to your last. Where the item says one device only, use exactly one, and make it antithesis or the short sentence.", "items": ["A sixty-second toast at a retiring principal's farewell dinner.", "Closing remarks to a state music education conference.", "Opening the festival's final night from the stage.", "Presenting the music prize at the valedictory ceremony.", "A one-minute case to an arts funding panel, one device only.", "Opening the first board meeting of the year as chair, one device only."], "seconds": 60, "prep": 15, "structure": "PPF", "targets": {"fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did your last sentence call back to your first, with its meaning changed?", "Could you name every device you used?", "Did you follow at least one long sentence with a short one?", "In the one-device items, did you use exactly one?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver the passage as written. Name aloud each device it uses and judge whether the dose suits its occasion. Then deliver it for a board or panel stripped to a single form, and once more for a ceremony with one added callback.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did you find and name every device in the passage?", "Did the deliberative version contain exactly one form?", "Did the ceremonial callback return to something from the opening, changed in meaning?", "Did the ceremonial version feel stronger without feeling heavier?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Body", "intro": "This strand trains what a room reads before and beneath the words: stance, hands, gaze, face, position, and the body under adrenaline. Among people who argue for a living, the body often decides whether a point is heard as a position or as a hope. You already hold authority behind a bass and in front of a band; the work is holding it at a committee table, a lectern and a council chair, with nothing in your hands and something at stake.", "units": [{"id": "body-1", "title": "Stance and stillness", "goal": "Plant a standing or seated base before the first word and hold it through the opening under scrutiny, without sway, swivel or head-bob.", "teach": {"body": "In senior rooms stillness reads as status. The mechanism is perceptual before it is social: the visual system is built to detect change, and movement in peripheral vision captures attention involuntarily. A speaker who sways divides the room's attention between the argument and the body, and the room experiences that as unease without being able to name it. The social reading follows. The person who does not need to move is read as the person who is not looking for an exit. Watch a senior silk, a seasoned vice-chancellor or a good presiding member: the base is fixed, and what moves is the face and the hands, and only when they carry something.\n\nThe standing base. Feet under the hips, weight over the midfoot rather than parked on the heels, knees unlocked. Locking the knees hyperextends the joint, tips the pelvis forward, deepens the arch of the lower back and disables the calf-muscle pump that returns blood from the legs; it is why parade grounds forbid it and why people faint at long ceremonies. Above that, think of the head balanced on the top of the spine, at the atlanto-occipital joint, rather than held. The Alexander Technique calls the relationship of head, neck and back the primary control, and the practical point is vocal: the commonest stress reflex is a small backward and downward pull of the head on the in-breath, which compresses the neck and stiffens the larynx. Posture here is not appearance. It is the shape of the instrument.\n\nThe seated base. Most of your consequential speaking is done sitting: SCSA committees, council, boards, the principal's office. Sit in the front half of the chair, upright on the sitting bones rather than rolled back onto the sacrum, both feet flat, forearms resting on the table edge. The seated sway is the swivel chair turned a few degrees left and right, the gradual recline, and the leg jiggling under the table, which travels up into the shoulders and is magnified on a video call.\n\nHow it fails. Under adrenaline the body takes one of a few exits: the lateral sway, the heel-to-toe rock, the settle onto one hip, the slow drift backwards. The settle onto one hip is the staffroom habit: it looks casual, which suits an aside and undercuts a recommendation. The subtler fault, and the one that survives into polished professionals, is the head bob, a small nod on every stressed syllable. It looks like eagerness, and it divides one point into several small ones. Keep the head still on the stress and let pitch and duration carry it.\n\nThe planted start. Arrive, stop, settle the weight, take one breath, find one person, then speak. Those three seconds feel long from inside and read as composure from outside. A first sentence spoken on the move, or before the room has quietened, is lost, and it hands the room the tempo. At a ceremony, when the master of ceremonies gives you the lectern, acknowledge them with a nod, arrive, and wait for the room. In the chair, feet flat and hands still before the opening words; a chair who waits in stillness quietens a talking room faster than one who raises the voice.\n\nHold the base through the first thirty seconds, when adrenaline peaks and the room forms its judgement. After that you may move, and a later unit deals with how. Every movement resolves back to this base, as a phrase resolves to the tonic.", "model": "[Introduced. A nod to the chair. Walk to the lectern. Stop. Plant. One breath. Find one face.] Thank you, *Chair*. || Let me begin | with a question this forum | has not yet *asked*. || Not whether the new course is *rigorous*. | We have spent three sessions on *that*. || The question is *who* it is for. || [Still. Head level.] Because a syllabus | that only students with private *tuition* can reach | is not rigorous. | It is *exclusive*.", "mistake": "You begin 'Thank you, Chair' while still squaring your notes, then add a sentence explaining why you are raising this now; as the explanation grows you settle onto your left hip and start nodding on every stress, so 'exclusive' arrives as one more small emphasis rather than a verdict.", "remember": ["Arrive, plant, breathe, find one face, then speak.", "Unlock the knees; balance the head, don't hold it.", "Seated: front of the chair, feet flat, forearms resting.", "Keep the head still on the stress.", "Every movement resolves back to the base."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-1-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Build the base", "how": "Prop the phone at chest height and frame yourself from head to hips, then seated from head to table. Take each position, hold it, and say the line while holding.", "items": ["Standing, feet under the hips. Lock the knees hard, then release to soft. Say: 'Chair, I'll speak to item four.' Notice what releases in the throat.", "Rock back onto the heels, then forward onto the toes, then settle over the midfoot. Say: 'The recommendation before you is unanimous.'", "Pull the head back and down a fraction, as if bracing, and say: 'I move that the report be received.' Then let the head balance forward and up, and say it again.", "Settle onto one hip, then return to centre. Say: 'This is a recommendation, not a discussion paper.'", "Seated, roll back onto the sacrum, then come up onto the sitting bones in the front half of the chair, feet flat, forearms on the table edge. Say: 'I declare the meeting open.'", "Seated base, nothing moving for the whole hold, chair not turning. Say: 'Before we proceed, I want to put something on the record.' Then stay still."], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did the top of your head stay in the same place in the frame while you spoke?", "Were the knees unlocked in every standing item?", "Did the balanced-head version sound freer than the braced one?", "Seated, did the chair stay still and both feet stay flat?"]}, {"id": "body-1-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "The planted start", "how": "Start two steps out of frame, or seated with the chair pushed back. Arrive, stop, plant, take one breath, find the lens, then say the line, and hold the base to the end of the time.", "items": ["Walk in after an imagined introduction, nod once to the chair, plant, breathe: 'Good morning, Chair.'", "Seated, pull the chair in, settle feet and forearms, and wait for an imagined room to quieten: 'I declare the meeting open, and I acknowledge the Whadjuk Noongar people, on whose land we meet.'", "Walk to an imagined conference lectern, plant, find a face in the second row: 'I have twenty minutes, and one argument.'", "Walk on at a presentation evening, stop at the lectern, and wait three full seconds: 'Distinguished guests, families, and graduates.'", "Seated across from the principal, settle fully before speaking: 'I've come with a proposal, and a deadline.'", "Walk to the front of an arts funding panel, plant, breathe: 'I'll be brief, and I'll be specific.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Was there a visible stop before the first word?", "Did the breath come after the stop and before the word?", "Had your head stopped moving before you spoke?", "Did you stay planted through the last word, with no step or settle after it?"]}, {"id": "body-1-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Still through silence", "how": "Stand or sit in the base. Say each line, then hold the silence with nothing moving: no nod, no settle, no glance at notes.", "items": ["The motion is *carried*.", "I don't accept that *characterisation*.", "The panel's decision | is *final*.", "We will not be withdrawing the *submission*.", "I'll take that question | on *notice*.", "I'd ask the committee | to consider what it is actually *voting* for.", "Thank you. || The meeting is *closed*."], "hold": 4, "checks": ["Did the head stay still on the stressed word?", "Did anything move during the silence, including a nod?", "Did the shoulders stay where they were as the silence ended?", "Did the silence look chosen rather than stuck?"]}, {"id": "body-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Planted under questioning", "how": "Plant before the timer starts, standing or seated as the real room would have you. Speak without the feet moving, the chair turning or the head nodding on the stresses.", "items": ["To a SCSA syllabus committee: should the Music ATAR course retain a compulsory aural component?", "To the festival association board: the case for capping entries per school.", "To the school council: why a selective program in a public school is defensible.", "To a university academic committee: should performance be assessed by a panel or by a single examiner?", "To an arts funding panel: why a youth jazz festival deserves multi-year rather than annual funding.", "To the principal and two deputies: why the music program needs a timetabled ensemble block."], "seconds": 75, "prep": 15, "structure": "PREP", "targets": {"fillers": 2}, "checks": ["Did the feet, or the chair, stay still for the full time?", "Did you plant before the first word?", "Did the head stay level on your strongest point?", "Did you hold the last beat still rather than relaxing on the final word?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Deliver the passage twice to the propped phone: first standing at an imagined lectern, then seated at an imagined table. Plant before the first word each time, and do not move the base until a full beat after the last word.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did each version begin from stillness, not on arrival?", "Did the head stay level through every stressed word?", "Were the knees as soft, or the seat as upright, at the end as at the start?", "Did you hold a beat of stillness after the final word?"]}}, {"id": "body-2", "title": "Gesture and the hands", "goal": "Rest the hands in a home position that reads as assured, land each gesture's stroke on the stressed syllable, and handle notes, microphone and pen without leakage.", "teach": {"body": "Below the face, the hands are the most watched part of a speaker and the most likely to leak arousal. The aim is not economy for its own sake. It is gestures that carry meaning, a rest position to return to between them, and nothing in between.\n\nThe rest position. Arms hanging loose, or the hands meeting lightly at about navel height, fingertips touching or one hand resting in the other. Seated, the forearms rest on the table, hands apart or loosely together, visible. What these replace: arms folded, hands below the table, hands behind the back, the low clasp in front of the body, and the steeple held for minutes, which tends to read as calculation rather than authority. A tight grip on a pen is fidgeting with a prop.\n\nThe mechanism. David McNeill's taxonomy is still the useful one. Deictic gestures point: to the paper, to a member, to an option. Iconic gestures depict something concrete, the size of a hall or a sequence of events. Metaphoric gestures give an abstract idea a shape: two hands holding two options, a hand placing a principle on the table. Beat gestures are small strokes that mark rhythm and stress. Adam Kendon analysed the gesture into phases, now usually described as preparation, stroke, hold and retraction. The stroke is the meaningful part, and in fluent speech it lands with or slightly ahead of the stressed syllable, the prosodic peak. When the stroke lags the stress, the gesture looks like an afterthought; when it is reliably synchronised, the listener hears the stress as larger.\n\nGesture also serves the speaker. Susan Goldin-Meadow's work shows that gesturing while explaining lightens the speaker's cognitive load, and studies that restricted speakers' hands found them less fluent, particularly with spatial content. Sitting on your hands to look composed costs you words.\n\nAt senior register, scale and frequency come down. A committee table rewards gestures inside the frame of the forearms: a stroke and a hold rather than a flourish. The hold is the most underused phase. A hand that stays in place to the end of a clause tells the room the thought is not finished, which is a quiet way of keeping the floor. The retraction home tells the room the point is complete, and makes the next gesture count. Give each idea a place in the space in front of you and keep it there; when you return to the first option ten minutes later, the same hand to the same place recalls it without a word.\n\nObjects. Notes on a lectern stay flat, and pages are slid, not lifted. In the hand, card rather than paper, held still at waist height. A handheld microphone sits about a fist below the chin, angled to the mouth, and turns when the head turns; the free hand does the gesturing. A pen goes down before you speak, because in the hand it becomes a baton, and you have spent twenty-six years making a moving hand mean time.\n\nHow it fails. The capable professional rarely fidgets. The fault is subtler: one gesture used for every point, usually a chopping beat, so emphasis becomes texture; gestures that rise above the shoulders as the argument heats; a palm-down press under conciliatory words; and the self-touch that appears only under challenge, a hand to the jaw or the back of the neck, which the room reads as the moment the point reached you.", "model": "[Seated. Forearms on the table.] There are *two* readings of this clause. || [Left hand places the first on the table.] On the first, | the school *must* offer the course. || [Right hand places the second.] On the second, | it *may*. || [Both hands hold. Eyes to the deputy chair.] The difference is not *drafting*. | It is about who carries the *cost*. || [Hands home.] I'd ask that we settle *that* | before we settle the *wording*. || [Left hand returns to the first place.] If the answer is *must*, | the Authority should say who *funds* it. || [Hands home.] If it is *may*, | we should say so *plainly*, | and stop pretending the course is *universal*.", "mistake": "You set out the two readings with the same small chopping beat on 'first', 'second', 'must' and 'may', so neither reading takes a place on the table, and on 'who carries the cost' your hand drifts to your jaw as the deputy chair frowns.", "remember": ["Land the stroke on the stressed syllable, not after.", "Hold to keep the floor; retract to close.", "Give each idea a place, and keep it there.", "Put the pen down before you speak.", "Below the shoulders, inside the frame of the forearms."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-2-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Find home", "how": "Frame yourself from head to waist, standing and then seated. Take each position, say the line in it, and note which versions read as hiding or calculating.", "items": ["Standing, arms loose at the sides. Say: 'I'll speak to item four.' Then hands meeting lightly at navel height, and say it again.", "Arms folded. Say: 'I'm happy to take questions on the report.' Drop to home and repeat; watch the shoulders.", "Hands in the low clasp in front. Say: 'The panel has reached a decision.' Lift to navel height and repeat.", "Hold a steeple for the whole line: 'I've read the submission closely.' Release to home and repeat; decide which carries authority.", "Seated, hands below the table. Say: 'I'll speak against the motion.' Bring the forearms onto the table, hands visible, and repeat.", "Seated, pen in hand. Say: 'Item six is the budget.' Put the pen down, hands home, and repeat."], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Were both hands visible in every home version?", "Did the shoulders drop when the arms opened?", "Were the hands completely still in home, thumbs and fingers included?", "Could you say what made the steeple read differently from home?"]}, {"id": "body-2-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Stroke on the stress", "how": "Say each line with one gesture whose stroke lands on the starred word, hold it to the end of the clause, then retract home. Watch for any lag between word and hand.", "items": ["Deictic, toward an imagined paper on the table: 'The answer is in *Appendix* C.'", "Metaphoric, two hands weighing: 'We can *amend* the motion, | or we can *defeat* it.'", "Metaphoric, one hand placing a principle on the table: 'Assessment should measure the *music*, | not the *money*.'", "Iconic, a sequence laid out left to right: 'Draft in *March*, | consultation in *May*, | approval in *August*.'", "Beat, two small strokes only: 'That is *not* | what the minutes *say*.'", "Open palm toward an imagined member: 'I think the *Deputy* Chair | raised this first.'"], "seconds": 12, "checks": ["Did each stroke arrive with or just before the starred word?", "Did you hold the gesture to the end of the clause?", "Did you retract fully home before the next line?", "Did every gesture stay below the shoulders?"]}, {"id": "body-2-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "Notes, microphone, pen", "how": "Use real objects or stand-ins: a card, a bottle for the microphone, a pen, a bench for the lectern. Follow each instruction while saying the line, and watch the object as closely as the face.", "items": ["Notes flat on the lectern. Read the first line, look up, then speak: 'Three findings, and one recommendation.' Slide the page to turn it; do not lift it.", "Card in one hand at waist height. Glance down, look up, then say: 'The review panel met on four occasions.' The card does not move while you speak.", "Microphone a fist below the chin. Turn the head left, then right, on: 'Good evening, and thank you for staying for the panel.' The microphone follows the mouth.", "Microphone in one hand; the free hand makes one metaphoric gesture on: 'The question is *equity*, | not *excellence*.'", "Pen in hand at a table. Watch whether it beats on the stresses as you say: 'I don't think that *follows*.' Put it down and say it again.", "Under challenge: say 'That's a fair criticism' with one hand drifting to the jaw, then again with both forearms resting still on the table."], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did the notes and card stay flat and still?", "Did the microphone stay the same distance from the mouth through each head turn?", "While held, did the pen beat time on the stresses?", "In the last item, did the still version look more assured than the first?"]}, {"id": "body-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Argue with the hands", "how": "Seated at a table, forearms resting, explain the matter using at least one deictic gesture, one metaphoric weighing gesture and one hold that keeps the floor through a clause. Retract home between gestures, and return each option to the place you first gave it.", "items": ["To a SCSA committee: the two models for moderating practical performance marks.", "To the school council: two ways to fund instrument replacement over five years.", "To the festival board: two adjudication formats, and which you favour.", "To a university course committee: the difference between assessing interpretation and assessing accuracy.", "To the principal: why the ensemble timetable conflicts with Year 11 electives, and the two ways out.", "To an arts funding panel: what the grant buys in year one, and what it buys in year three."], "seconds": 75, "prep": 15, "structure": "TWO", "checks": ["Did you start and finish in home?", "Did each option keep the place your hands first gave it?", "Did at least one gesture hold through a clause while you kept the floor?", "Did the hands stay off face, neck and pen for the whole time?"]}], "apply": {"source": "pair", "task": "Seated at a table, say the weak phrasing, then the strong one. Give the strong line's key idea a gesture whose stroke falls on its stressed word and holds to the end of the clause; then say it once with no gesture and decide which the line needs.", "seconds": 30, "checks": ["Did the stroke arrive on the stressed word, not after it?", "Did the hold end with the clause rather than before it?", "Did the gesture fit the meaning: weighing for a choice, placing for a principle?", "Did you retract fully home after the line?"]}}, {"id": "body-3", "title": "Gaze and the floor", "goal": "Hold one person for one complete thought, use gaze to allocate and keep the floor at a table, and look away to think without losing the room.", "teach": {"body": "Gaze is how a room decides whether you are speaking to it or past it, and at a table it does a second job: it allocates the floor. Adam Kendon's studies of conversational gaze in the 1960s found a regular pattern. Speakers tend to look away as they begin a long utterance and look back toward its end, and the return of the speaker's gaze is one of the cues a listener uses to take the turn. You use the pattern without knowing it. The craft is using it on purpose.\n\nOne person per thought. In a hall or a conference room, give one person a complete thought, a clause that ends at a pause, then move to someone else at the pause. The person addressed feels it, and at a distance so does the section around them. Moving the eyes mid-clause cuts the thought in half and accelerates you; moving at the pause binds gaze, phrase and breath together, and slows you. The lighthouse sweep connects with no one. Visit zones out of order, include the far corners and the ends of the front row, and under stage lighting look where the faces would be, at eye height.\n\nThe table. At a committee table the rules are closer and more political. Hold the eyes of the person you are answering for the thought that answers them, release to the table for the general point, and return to them for the close. Include the people beside you, who are the easiest to omit and who notice. When someone else speaks, look at them steadily; a chair who reads papers while a member speaks has told the member what the chair thinks of the contribution.\n\nGaze as floor control. As chair, the turn of your head to the next speaker gives them the floor before your words do. As a member, the reverse applies. If you are interrupted and mean to continue, keep your eyes on the person you were addressing, not on the interrupter, and let a hand hold in place; turning to the interrupter concedes the floor. Breaking gaze downward at the end of a sentence signals that you have more to say. Holding gaze at the end of a sentence invites a reply. Be deliberate about which you are offering.\n\nLooking away to think. Glenberg and colleagues showed that averting the gaze improves recall on demanding questions: faces are costly to process, and looking away frees capacity. It is legitimate, and in front of very articulate people it reads as considered rather than evasive, provided it is a single movement. Look down or to the side, not up, which looks like searching for an answer that is not there. Keep the face and torso toward the room, and bring the eyes back to a person before the first word of the answer. Think away; speak to someone.\n\nHow it fails. Reading notes with glances up, so the room gets your forehead. Speaking to the most senior person while nominally addressing the table. Anchoring on the one sympathetic face. Holding the questioner for the first sentence, then drifting to the table as the answer gets difficult, which the questioner reads as retreat. And the one to watch in yourself: eyes moving as fast as ideas arrive, so the room sees a tangent coming before it hears one.", "model": "[To the member who asked.] You asked whether the program is *selective*. || It is, | and I won't pretend *otherwise*. || [To the far end of the table.] What selection buys | is a cohort that can play *together* | from the first *week*. || [To the member beside you.] What it costs | is that some families *miss* out, | and that cost is *real*. || [Back to the questioner.] So the question for this council | is not *whether* we select. | It is how *widely* we look.", "mistake": "You hold the questioner for the first clause; then a second thought about feeder schools occurs to you, your eyes go to it before your words do, drift to your notes, glance at the principal on 'real', and return to the questioner only after the last word, so the close reaches her as an afterthought.", "remember": ["One person, one thought; move at the pause.", "Answer the asker, and close on the asker.", "Interrupted, keep your eyes on your addressee.", "Think looking down or aside; speak to someone.", "Look steadily at whoever has the floor."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-3-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Hold for the thought", "how": "Treat the lens as one person's eyes. Hold it until the final word is complete and one beat after, then release the gaze downward for one second. Blink normally.", "items": ["You asked whether the program is *selective*. | It *is*.", "I'd like to hear the Deputy Chair on *this* one.", "That isn't what the panel *found*.", "There are *two* options, | and I support the *second*.", "I'll stop there, | and invite *questions*.", "I'm not *finished*, | and I'd ask to *continue*."], "seconds": 12, "checks": ["Did the eyes stay on the lens until a beat after the last word?", "Did you blink normally rather than stare?", "Was the release downward, not up or sideways?", "Did the face stay soft while the gaze held?"]}, {"id": "body-3-d2", "type": "read", "title": "Zones of the hall", "how": "Stand facing a room with five objects as zones, two in far corners and one at the end of the front row. Move to a new zone only at each | or ||, never mid-phrase, and never in left-to-right order.", "items": ["Good morning. | It's a privilege to open this *conference*. || We have two days, | forty *papers*, | and *one* question running through them.", "Distinguished guests, | families, | and *graduates*. || Tonight is about what you *made*, | and who helped you *make* it.", "The review began with a *simple* premise. || That a syllabus should describe what musicians actually *do*. || It ended somewhere more *interesting*.", "We meet on Whadjuk Noongar *boodja*. || I pay my respects to Elders | past and *present*, | and to any Aboriginal people with us *tonight*.", "I'll close with *thanks*. || To the adjudicators, | who listened to every *band*. || And to the volunteers, | who carried every *chair*."], "checks": ["Did every eye movement happen at a pause mark?", "Did all five zones receive a whole thought?", "Did the end of the front row get one?", "Did the eyes settle and stay, rather than sweep?"]}, {"id": "body-3-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "Look away, come back", "how": "Read the question aloud, look down or to the side once to think, then bring the eyes back to the lens before the first word of your answer. Answer in two or three sentences.", "items": ["A SCSA committee member: how would you defend the weighting of the aural examination to a university admissions officer?", "A councillor: what would you cut first if the music budget fell by a fifth?", "A board member: is the festival financially sustainable without the state grant?", "A professor of education: isn't a selective program in a public school a contradiction?", "The principal: what would you need to justify a Level 3 position for music?", "A panel host: what does jazz teach that classical training doesn't?"], "seconds": 25, "checks": ["Was the look away a single movement, down or aside, never up?", "Did the eyes return before the first word?", "Did the face and torso stay toward the lens while the eyes were away?", "Did the eyes stay steady within each sentence of the answer?"]}, {"id": "body-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Around the table", "how": "Sit at a table with four objects as members, two beside you and one opposite cast as the questioner. Answer the questioner first, give each general point to one other member, and close on the questioner; once, imagine an interruption from your left and keep your gaze where it is until the clause is finished.", "items": ["The Deputy Chair asks the council why concert attendance fell this year.", "A SCSA committee member asks why the draft rubric changed after consultation.", "A board member asks why the festival ran over budget on adjudication.", "A deputy principal asks the department why the instrument audit is late.", "A university committee member asks what evidence supports a change to audition criteria.", "A parent body representative asks why ensemble rehearsals clash with interschool sport."], "seconds": 75, "prep": 20, "structure": "WSN", "checks": ["Did the answer begin and end on the questioner?", "Did each general point go to a single member?", "Did both members beside you receive a thought?", "When interrupted, did your gaze stay on your addressee until the clause was finished?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Answer the scenario aloud to an imagined table of five with the questioner opposite. Look away once to think, acknowledge the questioner eye to eye, take the bridge to the table one thought per member, and bring the answer back to the questioner; if the scenario has an adversary, give them a full thought, not a glance.", "seconds": 75, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did you look away once, deliberately, before speaking?", "Did the acknowledgement go to the questioner, eye to eye?", "Did each thought go to one person, including any adversary?", "Did you finish on the questioner and hold one beat?"]}}, {"id": "body-4", "title": "Face at rest, under fire", "goal": "Keep a resting face that reads as attentive rather than severe, listen visibly from the chair without signalling agreement, and take criticism with a face that stays open.", "teach": {"body": "Your face speaks when you are not speaking. In a meeting you chair, or a committee where you are one voice among twelve, you spend most of the time listening, and the room reads your face throughout. Many people, autistic people often among them, have a resting expression that others read as severe or displeased when it is in fact concentration. There is no point arguing the room out of that reading. You can set a different default.\n\nThe resting face. Not a smile. A smile held for minutes stiffens into a grin, and under pressure a fixed smile looks like nerves or contempt. The resting face is a release: the jaw unclenched with the teeth slightly apart, the corners of the mouth neutral rather than drawn down, the forehead smooth, the eyes soft. The masseter is among the first muscles to brace under stress, and a clenched jaw tends to fix the tongue and larynx with it, so the release is vocal as well as visual. Bringing to mind a colleague you respect does much of the rest.\n\nThe felt smile. Guillaume Duchenne distinguished the smile made with zygomaticus major alone, which lifts the corners of the mouth, from the smile that also recruits orbicularis oculi, the muscle ringing the eye, which narrows it and lifts the cheeks. Paul Ekman later named the second the Duchenne smile. Listeners register the difference without thinking about it. You cannot sustain it on demand, so reserve smiling for what earns it and let it arrive and fade. Onset and offset matter as much as the smile itself: one that switches off looks like social performance.\n\nThe listening face. Eyes on the speaker, resting face, and let the brows do the work: a small lift for something new, a slight draw together for something complex. Nod rarely and slowly, on main points. Rapid repeated nodding signals hurry, and from the chair it signals agreement. The chair's face is part of the record in a way the minutes are not: a chair who nods along with one side has, for the room, taken it. A slow single nod says 'I have that', not 'I agree'.\n\nThe criticised face. When a councillor, a panel member or a parent body representative criticises you or the program, the face goes to one of a few places: the tightened mouth, the brows lifted in disbelief, the head tilted back, or the half-smile, the most damaging, because it reads as dismissal. Hold the resting face, keep your eyes on them, and give one slow nod at the point you can honestly acknowledge. Exhale through the nose. Do not draft your reply on your face; draft it in the pause after they finish. Among very articulate people, an open face under attack is often more persuasive than the reply, because it shows the attack did not land where it was aimed.\n\nCongruence. The face has to agree with the words, and it has to agree in time. Thanks delivered with a neutral face, a concession with a tightening at the corner of the mouth, bad news with the polite social smile still in place: the room believes the face. The polished version of the fault is the expression that arrives a beat late, warmth that follows the word rather than leading it. Let the face change just before the phrase it belongs to, as a good accompanist voices the chord a fraction before the soloist needs it.", "model": "[Resting face. One slow nod at the point about reporting.] Thank you. || You're right that the board received the adjudication costs *late*, | and that is *my* responsibility as treasurer. || [Brows level. Eyes on her.] They ran over because we added a *second* panel for the junior division, | and on the *merits* | I would make that decision *again*. || [The face eases, just before the next phrase.] What I *will* change | is when you *hear* about it. || From next month, | the board sees the figures *before* the festival, | not *after*.", "mistake": "You listen to the criticism with a slight half-smile and two quick nods, say 'You're right' with the brows still lifted, and let the face soften only after 'what I will change', so the concession looks reluctant and the commitment looks like relief.", "remember": ["Unclench the jaw: teeth apart, forehead smooth.", "Nod once, slowly, at what you can acknowledge.", "Draft the reply in the pause, not on the face.", "Let the face change just before its phrase.", "Let a real smile arrive and fade."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-4-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Set the resting face", "how": "Frame the face closely. Do each instruction while watching, then say the line holding the result.", "items": ["Clench the jaw hard, then release until the teeth part. Say: 'We'll move to item seven.' Listen for the change in the vowels.", "Frown, then let the forehead go smooth without lifting the brows. Say the same line.", "Bring to mind a colleague you respect, without smiling. Say: 'Good morning, everyone. Thank you for the papers.'", "Hold a broad social smile for five seconds, then release to the resting face. Say: 'Welcome back after the break.'", "Resting face, eyes on the lens, for the full hold. Say: 'Please, go on.' Watch for the corners of the mouth drawing down.", "Resting face, then let a felt smile arrive and fade on: 'And that, | for the record, | was *unanimous*.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Were the teeth apart and the jaw loose?", "Did the forehead stay smooth on the resting face?", "Did the corners of the mouth stay neutral rather than drawn down?", "Did the felt smile reach the eyes and fade gradually?"]}, {"id": "body-4-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Listening from the chair", "how": "Say the member's line aloud, hold the listening face to the lens for the rest of the time, then give the chair's reply. One slow nod at most.", "items": ["'I don't think this committee has seen the evidence for the change.' Then: 'Thank you. I'll ask the working party to table it.'", "'The festival has relied on volunteers for too long.' Then: 'That's a fair point, and a large one.'", "'With respect, the draft reads as if it was written for one school.' Then: 'I'd like to hear more on that.'", "'I move that we defer the item.' Then: 'Is there a seconder?'", "'The consultation was a formality.' Then: 'Noted. I'll come back to that.'", "'This is the strongest submission we've received.' Then: 'Thank you. Other views?'"], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Did you nod no more than once, and slowly?", "Did the brows carry the response rather than the mouth?", "On the motion and the praise, did the face stay neutral enough that neither side could claim you?", "Did the reply match the face that preceded it?"]}, {"id": "body-4-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Take the hit", "how": "Say each criticism aloud as if a member has just said it to you. Hold the resting face through the silence with at most one slow nod, exhale through the nose, then say only: 'Thank you.'", "items": ["To be blunt, | the report is *thin*.", "The board was *never* told about this.", "Your program serves a very *small* number of families.", "I'm not sure the department has the *capacity* to deliver this.", "This rubric would not survive five minutes with a *university* panel.", "Frankly, | I expected *more* from the chair."], "hold": 4, "checks": ["Did you avoid both the half-smile and the lifted brows?", "Did the head stay level rather than tilt back?", "Did you exhale before 'Thank you'?", "Did 'Thank you' come at your normal pitch and pace?"]}, {"id": "body-4-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Criticised, then answer", "how": "Read the criticism aloud, hold the open face for one full breath, then answer. Let the face change just before each part of the answer: serious through the acknowledgement, steady through the bridge, warmer only where the answer earns it.", "items": ["A councillor: 'Why is music funded more generously than any other learning area?'", "A SCSA committee member: 'Your proposal would advantage schools with specialist programs, including yours.'", "A festival board member: 'The treasurer's report raises more questions than it answers.'", "A university academic: 'Jazz pedagogy in schools is mostly enthusiasm and very little method.'", "The principal: 'I've had complaints from three heads of learning about the ensemble timetable.'", "A panel host: 'Isn't specialist music simply a subsidy for families who can already afford lessons?'"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 10, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did the face stay open while the criticism was delivered?", "Did the acknowledgement look meant?", "Did each change of expression come just before its phrase, not after?", "Did you avoid any smile through the difficult part?"]}], "apply": {"source": "pair", "task": "Say the weak phrasing, then the strong one, to the lens as if answering a criticism at a board table. Before the strong line, hold the resting face through one breath; during it, check that the face leads each phrase rather than follows it.", "seconds": 30, "checks": ["Did the face match the strong line's meaning from its first word?", "Was the jaw loose throughout?", "Did any smile appear only where the words earned it?", "With the sound off, could someone tell the weak line from the strong one?"]}}, {"id": "body-5", "title": "Space, position, movement", "goal": "Choose where to stand or sit in any senior room, move only on transitions, and use a lectern, table or stage without hiding behind it or wandering across it.", "teach": {"body": "Position tells a room who you are in relation to it before you speak, and movement then confirms that or erodes it. The principle carries from the bandstand: move on the transition, plant for the point.\n\nDistance. Edward T. Hall's proxemics, set out in The Hidden Dimension, distinguished intimate, personal, social and public distance, and the categories still describe what a speaker manages. A committee table works at social distance, roughly one to four metres, where a lean of the torso is a large signal. A conference lectern works at public distance, where small signals vanish and only clear changes of position read. Calibrate scale to distance: at the table, a lean of a hand's breadth is an intervention; on a stage it is invisible.\n\nMovement with purpose. Every move should mean something: a new section, a change of register, a step toward the room for the personal passage, a step back to cede the space. Walk during the link sentence, plant, and deliver the point still. The obvious failure is pacing, a walk without destination that the room follows like a rally. The professional's failure is the drift: a half-step forward on emphasis, a half-step back on qualification, unnoticed by the speaker and read by everyone else as advance and retreat. If you move, take two or three clear steps and stop completely.\n\nThe lectern. A shelf, not a shield. Stand a hand's width behind it, upright, hands resting lightly on its edges or in home, never gripping and never leaning on the forearms, which drops the chest and with it the voice. For a passage that needs warmth, a story or a personal acknowledgement, step to its side; return to it for what is formal: names, citations, lists, the reading of an award. A fixed microphone governs more than the lectern does, so a step away from it is either brief or a decision to project.\n\nThe table. Seating is a statement. Robert Sommer's studies of small-group seating found that people choose corner or side-by-side positions to cooperate and opposite positions to compete. Use it: sit at the corner beside the person you need to persuade, not across from them. Forearms on the edge, both feet flat. Lean in for the one point you care most about, then return; a speaker who leans in throughout has nothing left to signal with. A long recline signals withdrawal or judgement. In the principal's office take the chair offered, and if there is a choice between the desk and a side table, the side table makes it a conversation between colleagues.\n\nStanding and sitting. Standing gives height, breath and command; sitting signals collaboration. Standing when everyone else is seated changes the temperature of a room, and Westminster practice marks the convention from the other side: when the Speaker rises, members sit. Stand in a seated meeting only when you mean to, and only when the occasion's conventions allow it.\n\nCeremony. You are the frame, not the picture. At a presentation, step back from the lectern as the recipient comes forward, turn your body toward them, and applaud; the space is theirs until they leave it. On a stage with an ensemble behind you, stand downstage and to one side, angle toward the players to introduce them, and face the house to address it. When you hand over, the two steps back are the announcement.", "model": "[Behind the lectern, hands resting.] This award recognises a body of *work*, | not a single *night*. || [Step to the lectern's side. Plant.] When I first heard this student, | she was *twelve*, | and she argued with me about a *voicing*. || She was *right*. || [Back behind the lectern. Card flat.] For four years of leadership in the *ensemble* program, | the award goes to | [the name, read from the card]. || [Two steps back. Turn toward her. Applaud. When she has left the stage, return to the lectern and plant.] Our next award | is for a student who has never sought the *front* of the stage, | and has held up the *back* of it | for five *years*.", "mistake": "You stay behind the lectern for the story, then extend it with a second anecdote you had not planned, taking a half-step forward on 'she was right' and a half-step back as you look for the way out of the detour; you are still at the lectern reading the card as the recipient arrives, so for two seconds the space is still yours.", "remember": ["Move on the transition; plant for the point.", "Scale the move to the distance.", "Beside the lectern for warmth, behind it for formality.", "Sit at the corner of the person you need.", "At a presentation, step back: the space is theirs."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-5-d1", "type": "mirror", "title": "Move, then plant", "how": "Prop the phone at the end of a space with room for three steps. Say the link sentence while you walk, stop completely, then deliver the point standing still.", "items": ["Walk: 'Which brings me to the funding model.' Plant: 'It is not *sustainable*.'", "Walk: 'The second finding.' Plant: 'Enrolments are falling across the *sector*, | not just *here*.'", "Walk toward the lens: 'Something more personal.' Plant: 'I teach the students who *sit* it.'", "Walk back, ceding the space: 'And now, | the people who did the *work*.' Plant: 'The working *party*.'", "Catch the drift: say 'We *must* act, | although it may take *time*', and check whether you stepped in on 'must' and back on 'although'. Repeat with the feet still.", "Stay still for both: 'Chair.' || 'I'll *rest* there.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Did you walk only during the link sentence?", "Did you stop completely before the point?", "In the drift item, were the feet still on the second attempt?", "Were the steps clear and few, not a shuffle?"]}, {"id": "body-5-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Lectern and table", "how": "Use a bench or a stack of books as the lectern, then a table and chair. Follow each instruction while saying the line.", "items": ["A hand's width behind the lectern, hands resting lightly: 'Distinguished guests, | it is a privilege to present these *awards*.'", "Lean on the forearms, then come upright. Say the same line and listen for the drop in the voice when leaning.", "Step to the lectern's side for: 'When I first heard her play, | she was *twelve*.'", "Back behind it, card flat: 'For outstanding leadership, | the award goes to...' Then two steps back, turn toward the recipient and applaud.", "Seated, forearms on the edge, feet flat: 'Let's begin. I have the apologies.' Then lean in a hand's breadth for: 'This is the item I care *most* about.' Then return.", "Seated, recline into the chair for: 'I'm not persuaded.' Then sit up and say it again."], "seconds": 20, "checks": ["Were the hands resting, never gripping?", "Did leaning on the lectern audibly drop the voice?", "Did stepping to the side change the register of the line?", "At the table, did the lean in happen once and return?"]}, {"id": "body-5-d3", "type": "recall", "title": "Block the occasion", "how": "Read the anchors, set out the furniture in your space, then deliver from memory, moving only between anchors and planting for each.", "items": ["Thank the MC / Acknowledge Country / Step aside: why this award exists / Back to the lectern: the citation / The name, then step back and applaud", "Open the conference session / The question for today / Walk: the first speaker's field / Plant: their name / Turn and hand over", "Stand: open the council meeting / Quorum and apologies / Sit: the principal's report / Lean in: the item that matters / Sit back and close the item", "Behind the lectern: the graduating class / Step aside: one story from Year 7 / Back: thanks to staff / Downstage: the final piece / Step back to the band", "Corner seat beside the principal / The proposal / The cost / The deadline / Sit back and invite the answer", "Festival opening: welcome / Walk: the bands and the adjudicators / Plant: the rules that matter / Step aside: why the festival exists / Hand over to the first band"], "seconds": 75, "prep": 25, "checks": ["Did you move only between anchors?", "Did you plant fully for each anchor?", "Did every handover include a physical move away?", "Did you keep the anchors in order?"]}, {"id": "body-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Choose the position", "how": "Decide first where you would be and whether standing or seated, take that position fully, then make one point and stop. Be in position before the first word.", "items": ["Tell the council that the music report contains a serious finding.", "Tell a parent body meeting that the interstate tour is cancelled.", "Put a staffing proposal to the principal in her office.", "Open the festival association's annual general meeting with a contested election on the agenda.", "Respond to a hostile question from the floor at a conference panel.", "Tell a SCSA working party you cannot support the draft.", "Close the end-of-year staff function with thanks."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ONE", "checks": ["Were you in position before the first word?", "Did the position fit both the room's conventions and the stakes?", "Did you hold it without drift?", "Did you stop after the one point, still?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Block the passage on paper before you speak it: where you start, the one move, where you plant again, and where the space is handed back. Deliver it with that blocking in a space set up as its real room, moving only on the transition.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did you move once, on the transition, and nowhere else?", "Did you plant fully before the next point?", "Did your position suit the room the passage is written for?", "Did you finish still, facing the room, and hold a beat?"]}}, {"id": "body-6", "title": "The body under adrenaline", "goal": "Read the adrenaline response accurately, reduce its visible effects, and use the minute before a high-stakes moment to arrive steady, slow and planted.", "teach": {"body": "Before a consequential moment the body is not malfunctioning. The sympathetic nervous system and the adrenal medulla release adrenaline and noradrenaline; heart rate and breathing rise, blood is redistributed toward the large muscles, salivary flow drops, and fine motor control coarsens into what physiologists call enhanced physiological tremor. You know the pre-gig version intimately. The version before a contested council vote or a SCSA panel is the same chemistry, with less rehearsal behind it and nothing in your hands.\n\nThree findings are worth holding precisely. First, the illusion of transparency. Gilovich, Savitsky and Medvec showed that people overestimate how visible their internal states are to others, and Savitsky and Gilovich later showed it in public speakers: observers rated them as less nervous than the speakers believed they appeared, and speakers told about the illusion gave better speeches. Second, reappraisal. Alison Wood Brooks found that people who said 'I am excited' before speaking were rated as more persuasive and confident than people who tried to calm down. Moving from high arousal to calm is a large step; reinterpreting the same arousal as readiness is a small one. Third, what did not hold: the hormonal claims made for expansive power posing failed to replicate. Posture matters for breath, voice and what the room sees, not because it reliably alters your hormones.\n\nBreath is the most direct lever. Heart rate rises a little on each inhale and falls on each exhale, a coupling called respiratory sinus arrhythmia, mediated by the vagus nerve. Lengthen the exhale and you weight the cycle toward slowing. The working pattern is in through the nose for about four, a brief pause, out through lightly pursed lips for six or seven. A variant studied at Stanford is cyclic sighing: two inhales through the nose, the second topping up the first, then a long exhale through the mouth. Two or three cycles, not twenty; overbreathing before speaking brings on light-headedness.\n\nTremor shows most in unsupported hands trying to hold still, and in anything light they hold. Give the hands support: a lectern edge, the table, forearms resting. Before you begin, press the palms together hard for five seconds and release, which spends some of the readiness in large muscle. Never hold a single sheet; it magnifies every tremor. Use card, or leave the notes flat.\n\nDry mouth. Sip room-temperature water in the minutes before, a little at a time, and rest the tongue tip behind the lower front teeth to prompt saliva.\n\nTempo. Adrenaline speeds the internal clock, so your sense of normal pace runs fast, and in a senior room a rushed opening reads as a request for permission. Plan a first sentence you could say asleep and start it slower than feels right; the meter tells the truth.\n\nThe cascade to watch. For you the adrenaline signature is less tremor than acceleration: pace climbs, qualifications multiply, the associative mind finds a tangent and the body follows it, weight shifting, hands rising, gaze darting. The body is the earliest place to catch it. When the feet want to move, plant; when the hands rise, bring them home; when the gaze darts, settle it on one person and finish the thought.\n\nThe minute before. Base. Three long exhales. Palms pressed, released. Sip. First line under the breath. One face. Then arrive, plant, breathe, begin.", "model": "[Three long exhales. Palms pressed and released. Walk to the lectern. Plant. One breath.] Chair. || I'll be *brief*, | because the committee has the *paper*. || [Still. Card flat.] The draft asks a seventeen-year-old | to analyse a score she has never *heard*. || We would not ask that of a *musicologist*. || I'd ask the committee | to restore the *recording*, | and to do it | before the draft goes to *consultation*.", "mistake": "You arrive at the lectern already into your first sentence, deliver 'I'll be brief' half again as fast as your normal pace, and by 'musicologist' have added two qualifications and a step to the left, with a single sheet trembling visibly in your hand.", "remember": ["Exhale longer than you inhale; two or three cycles.", "Call it readiness; the room sees less than you feel.", "Support the hands; card, never a single sheet.", "Start slower than feels right.", "When the tangent arrives, plant the feet first."]}, "drills": [{"id": "body-6-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "The long exhale", "how": "Breathe in through the nose, pause briefly, then out slowly through lightly pursed lips. Where the item gives a line, say it aloud on a fresh breath after the last cycle.", "items": ["Four cycles seated, one hand on the lower ribs: feel them widen sideways on the in-breath.", "Four cycles standing in the base, shoulders still, head balanced.", "Three cycles, then on a fresh breath: 'Chair, I have one point.'", "Two cyclic sighs, two sniffs in and one long exhale through the mouth, then: 'I'll speak to the recommendation.'", "Thirty seconds of brisk stepping on the spot, then three cycles, then: 'I declare the meeting open.'", "Three cycles seated at a table, small enough that no one across it would notice, then your first line under the breath, then aloud."], "pattern": [4, 1, 7, 0], "checks": ["Was each exhale clearly longer than the inhale?", "Did the shoulders stay down on the in-breath?", "Did the line after the breaths start slower than your habit?", "In the seated item, would someone across the table have seen it?"]}, {"id": "body-6-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Steady the hands", "how": "Frame the hands as well as the face. Do each instruction, then say the line while holding, and watch for tremor.", "items": ["Hold a single sheet at arm's length. Say: 'I'll read the motion as circulated.' Swap to a card and repeat; compare the tremor.", "Press the palms together hard for five seconds, release, let the hands hang. Say: 'The figures are in your papers.'", "Squeeze both fists for five seconds, release to home. Say: 'Before we proceed.'", "Fingertips resting on a table edge. Say: 'The treasurer's report is before you.'", "Microphone stand-in held close, elbow tucked to the ribs. Say: 'Good evening, and welcome to the festival.'", "Forearms resting on the table, hands still, through: 'I have *one* concern, | and it is *material*.'"], "seconds": 15, "checks": ["Did the card show less tremor than the sheet?", "Were the hands steadier after the press or squeeze?", "Holding the microphone, was the elbow tucked in?", "Did the hands stay still while you spoke?"]}, {"id": "body-6-d3", "type": "mirror", "title": "The minute before", "how": "Run the routine one step per item, in order, with water and a card within reach. Every step ends with something said aloud.", "items": ["Base, feet planted, knees soft. Say aloud: 'I'm excited.'", "Three long exhales, shoulders still, then count aloud slowly from one to five.", "Press the palms, release, hands home. Say your first line under the breath.", "Sip water; tongue tip behind the lower front teeth. Say the first line aloud, softly.", "Eyes on the lens, resting face. Say the first line at full voice, slower than feels right.", "Walk out of frame and back in, plant, breathe, and begin: 'Thank you, Chair.'"], "seconds": 12, "checks": ["Did you do every step in order?", "Did the shoulders stay down through the exhales?", "Was the first line at full voice slower than your instinct?", "Did you plant and breathe before 'Thank you, Chair'?"]}, {"id": "body-6-d4", "type": "pace", "title": "Start slow, heart up", "how": "Do thirty seconds of brisk stepping on the spot to lift the heart rate, then read each opening inside the target range. Your sense of pace will run fast; trust the meter.", "items": ["Thank you, *Chair*. || I'll be *brief*, | because the committee has the *paper*.", "Good morning. || I declare the meeting *open*. || We have a *quorum*, | and *two* apologies.", "Distinguished guests, | families, | and *graduates*. || Good *evening*.", "It's a privilege to open this *conference*. || I have *twenty* minutes, | and *one* argument.", "Madam President, | the treasurer's report is *before* you, | and it is not *good* news.", "I'll take the question on *notice*, | and I'll answer it in *writing* | by *Friday*."], "targets": {"wpm": [115, 145], "fillers": 0, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Were you inside the range from the first sentence?", "Did you hold each || for a full beat?", "Was the first sentence the slowest?", "Had the breathing settled by the third opening?"]}], "apply": {"source": "story", "task": "Read the story, then lift your heart rate with thirty seconds of brisk stepping. Run the minute-before routine and retell the story at an imagined lectern for a senior audience, opening slower than feels right with the hands supported; if a tangent arrives, plant the feet and finish the current thought before deciding whether to take it.", "seconds": 120, "checks": ["Did you run the full routine before the first word?", "Was the opening slower than your instinct?", "Did the hands stay supported and steady?", "Did you finish each thought before taking any tangent?"]}}]}, {"strand": "Tone", "intro": "Tone is what a room registers before it has weighed a word: whether you are certain, whether you are with it, whether you can be moved off your position. This strand trains the prosodic and verbal mechanics of authority, warmth, steadiness and lightness, first separately and then in combination, so that a syllabus committee, a divided board and a ceremonial lectern all hear the settled voice you already have behind the bass.", "units": [{"id": "tone-1", "title": "The sound of certainty", "goal": "Deliver a position or a ruling so it is heard as settled: nucleus on the information word, a complete terminal fall, no hedging, and a held beat after it.", "teach": {"body": "Certainty is carried less by what you say than by how each intonation unit ends. In English the nucleus, the last stressed syllable of a tone unit, carries the pitch movement that tells the listener what kind of utterance this is. A fall on the nucleus signals completion and commitment; a rise, or a fall-rise, signals that something remains open. Australian English has a well-documented habit of rising on declaratives, studied in sociolinguistics as Australian Questioning Intonation and known elsewhere as the high rising terminal. In conversation it is cooperative: it checks that the listener is following. On a committee it reads as a request for ratification, and a room of people who argue for a living will accept the invitation and begin deciding for you.\n\nCourtroom research makes the same point from another direction. William O'Barr and his colleagues, studying witness testimony in the 1970s, identified a cluster of features they called powerless speech: hedges, hesitation forms, intensifiers, tag questions, rising intonation on statements, and deferential forms where none was required. In their experiments, listeners rated witnesses who used the cluster as less credible and less competent, whatever the substance of the testimony. Each feature is small and individually innocent. Accumulated, they are the sound of someone asking permission.\n\nFour mechanics produce the opposite effect, and elite speakers use all four at once. First, the nucleus is placed late and on the word that carries the new information, so the fall coincides with the point: \"the proposal goes to the *board*\", not \"the *proposal* goes to the board\". Second, the fall is complete. Pitch drifts downward across an utterance, which phoneticians call declination, and a confident speaker lets that descent run to the floor of the range rather than arresting it halfway. Third, the key sentence is slower than its neighbours. Rate contrast does the work; slowing everything merely sounds ponderous. Fourth, the sentence is short. Commitment is economical, and the qualifying clause added to forestall an objection is usually the one that invites it.\n\nThen the beat. The silence after a completed fall is where a room accepts a statement. Senior counsel finishes a submission and waits; a practised chair announces a ruling and does not elaborate. The urge to fill that silence, with \"so\", \"anyway\" or a restatement, is the urge to check that the point has landed, and the room hears the check.\n\nUnder pressure the failures are predictable, and they arrive together. You begin before you know where the sentence ends, so you cannot plan the fall and the line trails. You stack qualifiers (\"I'd tentatively suggest we might possibly consider\") to pre-empt the professor at the end of the table. Rate climbs. The final syllables lift. In a room of articulate people each of these is registered instantly, and none is ever mentioned.\n\nThe discipline is to know the last word before you say the first. Compose the sentence, put the nucleus on the information word, let the pitch descend through the last two or three syllables to the bottom of your natural range, and stop. Do not press the whole voice lower to sound grave; a depressed larynx sounds performed and tires the folds. Finish at the floor of the range you already have. The baton taught you this long ago: an upbeat is a question, a downbeat is a decision, and an ensemble only enters on the second.", "model": "Thank you, Chair. || Two matters before general business. || The first is the *budget*. | We are over on instrument hire by a modest amount, | and the executive has a *remedy*, | which is in your *papers*. || The second is the festival *date*. | It *stays* in August. || The board weighed the alternatives in July, | and its reasons are in the *minutes*. || I'll take the budget *first*, | and questions on it at the *end*.", "mistake": "Thanks, everyone. So there are really two items, I suppose, before general business. The first is the budget, where we're running a little over on instrument hire, and the executive has what I think is a reasonable remedy, which hopefully you've had a chance to look at? And the second is the festival date, which we're proposing to keep in August, if people are comfortable with that.", "remember": ["Know the last word before you say the first.", "Put the fall on the information word.", "Slow the key sentence, not its neighbours.", "Cut every hedge that asks for ratification.", "Hold the beat after the fall."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-1-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Complete falls", "how": "Read each line aloud with the nucleus on the starred word and the pitch falling through to the floor of your range. Stop dead at each held full stop.", "items": ["The minutes of the March meeting | are *confirmed*. ||", "The committee's view is that the standard has been *met*. ||", "The draft goes to the Board | on the *fourteenth*. ||", "We do not recommend a change to the *weighting*. ||", "The motion is *carried*. || We move to item *six*. ||", "The association's accounts are audited | and in *order*. ||", "My recommendation is the *second* option. || The reasons are in the *paper*. ||"], "targets": {"fillers": 0}, "checks": ["Did the pitch fall on the starred word and keep falling to the last syllable?", "Did the meter register no end-of-phrase rise on any line?", "Was the starred word audibly longer or louder than the words around it?", "Did you stop at every held full stop with no trailing \"so\", \"um\" or audible breath?"]}, {"id": "tone-1-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Strip the hedges", "how": "Say the hedged line as it might come out at a committee table, then the committed line with a complete fall. On playback, notice which one invites the room to decide for you.", "items": ["I'd tentatively suggest we might perhaps consider deferring this to the next meeting, if that's acceptable? => I move that we *defer* this | to the next *meeting*. ||", "I wonder whether the descriptor is possibly a little ambiguous, in a sense? => The descriptor is *ambiguous*. || It can be read two *ways*. ||", "I'm not entirely sure, but I believe the auditor may have raised something about this? => The auditor raised it in *May*. || It is *resolved*. ||", "Just quickly, if I could, I might make one or two brief observations on the draft? => Two observations on the *draft*. ||", "I suppose what I'm sort of saying is the evidence doesn't really support the change? => The evidence does not support the *change*. ||", "Would it be all right if we moved on, unless anyone particularly objects? => We'll move to item *four*. ||", "With respect, and I may be wrong, I think that figure might be slightly out of date? => That figure is from *2024*. || The current figure is *lower*. ||"], "checks": ["Was the committed line less than half the length of the hedged one?", "Was the committed line free of \"just\", \"perhaps\", \"I think\" and \"sort of\"?", "Did the committed line end on a full fall, with no rise on the meter?", "On playback, did the hedged line sound like a question even where it had no question mark?"]}, {"id": "tone-1-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Land and hold", "how": "Deliver each line with a complete fall, then hold silence for the full count: no nod, no softening of the face, no added word.", "items": ["The decision is the committee's, | and it is *made*.", "We will not be reopening the *weighting*.", "That concludes the *formal* business.", "The festival proceeds as *scheduled*.", "My answer is *no*.", "The recommendation stands as *written*."], "hold": 3, "checks": ["Did you hold the full three seconds without filling it?", "Did your face stay composed rather than apologetic or expectant?", "Did the final word sit at the floor of your range?", "Did you resist any nod or head tilt during the silence?"]}, {"id": "tone-1-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Positions, stated once", "how": "State the position in your first sentence, give the single decisive reason, and stop. No hedges, no tags, no rising endings.", "items": ["Tell the SCSA syllabus committee your working group does not support the proposed change to the performance weighting.", "Tell the festival association board there will be no second stage this year.", "Tell the school council the voluntary contribution for the specialist program stays at its current level.", "Tell the principal you will not run next year's tour without a second staff member.", "Tell a university academic committee your unit will not adopt the proposed common assessment.", "Tell the department that report deadlines move forward a week."], "seconds": 30, "prep": 10, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"wpm": [120, 150], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Was the position stated in full in the first sentence?", "Did you give exactly one reason?", "Did every sentence end on a full fall, with no rise on the meter?", "Did you stop within two seconds of making the point?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Record the passage as written. Then deliver it again from its content rather than its wording, cutting every hedge, tag and intensifier, placing each nucleus on the information word and completing every fall. Compare the two recordings for duration and for end-of-phrase rises.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Was the second version measurably shorter than the first?", "Did the meter register no end-of-phrase rise in the second version?", "Did you hold a full beat after the central sentence?", "Did you avoid restating any point already made?"]}}, {"id": "tone-2", "title": "Warm without soft", "goal": "Sound attentive and on the listener's side, at a register suited to senior peers, while keeping the terminal fall and pace that make you credible.", "teach": {"body": "Social cognition research has settled on two dimensions along which people appraise one another: warmth, whether this person's intentions towards me are good, and competence, whether they can act on those intentions. Susan Fiske, Amy Cuddy and Peter Glick have argued that warmth is judged first and weighs heavily in the first impression. For a senior speaker the implication is uncomfortable. Competence alone wins a hearing but not an ally. A chair heard as purely competent is respected and quietly resisted; a chair heard as purely warm is liked and overruled. The craft is to carry both, and they sit in different parts of the utterance.\n\nWarmth is largely facial and prosodic, and it lives at the start and in the body of the phrase. Three mechanisms carry it. The first is the smile. Spreading the lips shortens the effective length of the vocal tract and raises the formants, so vowels brighten, and perception studies have shown for decades that listeners can hear a smile on audio alone. What is needed is a lift at the corners of the mouth and a softening of orbicularis oculi, the muscle around the eyes, not a display of teeth. The second is onset. A hard glottal attack, the folds pressed together before phonation and released with a click, sounds clipped and guarded; a balanced onset, where airflow and closure coincide, sounds open. The third is range within the phrase. A narrow range suggests reserve or fatigue; more movement through the middle of the sentence, before the nucleus, signals engagement.\n\nVerbal warmth works through specificity. A generic courtesy (\"thank you all so much for everything\") is heard as ritual and costs the speaker nothing. A specific acknowledgement (\"you read the submission in full, including the appendix\") is heard as attention, and attention is what senior people rarely receive. Name the person. Refer to what they actually did or said. Do it once.\n\nCompetence lives at the end, in the same terminal fall and unhurried rate you built in the first unit. That is the whole distinction between warm and soft. Soft is warmth that has leaked into the endings: a sympathetic rise, a trailing nucleus, an apology for raising the matter, a small laugh after a substantive statement to take the edge off it.\n\nThe capable professional's failure is rarely coldness. It is additive kindness: \"just\", \"only\", \"a quick one\", \"no pressure at all\", \"I hope you don't mind\". Each diminishes the message and implies something harder underneath. In the principal's office, or with a senior academic, it also sounds like deference, which reorders the hierarchy of the conversation. The second failure is register. The bright, wide-ranging warmth that settles a nervous twelve-year-old sounds patronising to a deputy director general. Warmth among peers is lower in pitch and narrower in range than warmth with the young; it is carried by the eyes and by specificity more than by melody. The third failure is overcorrection into gravity, so that good news is delivered like a diagnosis.\n\nThe sequence must be physical before it is verbal. Look at the person. Let the face soften before the first word, because the face arrives before the voice and sets it. Use the name. Make one specific acknowledgement with a balanced onset and some movement in the line. Then state your point at committee pace with a complete fall. Warm entry, firm exit, within the same breath.", "model": "Professor Hall, | thank you for reading the submission *in full*, | including the *appendix*. || Very few people *do*. || Your point about moderation is *right*, | and it has changed the *draft*. || Where we still differ is the *weighting*. || I'd value ten minutes with you before the *meeting*, | because I would rather we resolved it *here* | than at the *table*. || I'll bring the moderation *data*.", "mistake": "Professor Hall, thank you for reading the submission, including the appendix, which I know is long, and which partly exists because the consultation raised so many threads. Your point about moderation was right, and it has changed the draft, though it also connects to the weighting, which is where we may still differ, if that's a fair way to put it, so perhaps, if you had time at some point, we could talk it through?", "remember": ["Let the face soften before the first word.", "Acknowledge one specific thing, once.", "Warm in the body of the phrase, falling at the end.", "With peers, narrow the range; keep warmth in the eyes.", "Never apologise for raising the matter."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-2-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "One line, three temperatures", "how": "Say each line three times: first neutral; then with softened eyes, a slight smile and a balanced onset; then keeping that warmth while adding committee pace and a complete fall. Only the third is the target.", "items": ["Thank you for staying on after the *formal* business. || I need ten *minutes*.", "Your submission was the *clearest* we received. || It still needs a *budget*.", "I know this term has asked a great deal of the *department*. || Reports close on *Monday*.", "Welcome, everyone. || It's good to see a *full* room for an AGM.", "Dr Okafor, | that was a *generous* reading of our draft. || Let me answer the one *objection*.", "You've kept the association's books for *six* years. || We're asking for one *more*."], "checks": ["On playback, could you hear the brighter vowels in the second and third takes?", "Did the third take still end on a full fall, with no rise on the meter?", "Did the first syllable begin without a glottal click?", "Did the pitch move through the body of the phrase rather than staying flat?"]}, {"id": "tone-2-d2", "type": "mirror", "title": "Warmth at peer register", "how": "Prop the phone at eye level and work through each instruction while watching the screen. Where a line is given, say it to the camera as if to the person named.", "items": ["Release the jaw and let the lips part. Breathe through the mouth for three slow cycles.", "Lift the corners of the mouth a few millimetres and let the eyes soften. No teeth.", "Hold that face and say \"Good evening, Chair, members of council\" with a complete fall.", "Drop to a neutral face and say it again. Note how the vowels darken and flatten.", "Restore the warmth and say \"Thank you for reading the paper in full\" as if to a senior academic across the table.", "Keep the warmth and say \"I don't support the amendment.\" Check that the brows stay level and the head stays upright."], "seconds": 12, "checks": ["Did the eyes change, not only the mouth?", "Could you hear the difference in the vowels between the warm and neutral takes?", "Did the brows stay level and the head upright on the refusal?", "Did the jaw stay loose throughout?"]}, {"id": "tone-2-d3", "type": "swap", "title": "Warm, not soft", "how": "Say the soft version, then the warm one. The warm version is shorter, names one specific thing, and ends on a complete fall.", "items": ["I'm so sorry to take up your time, it's honestly just a tiny thing about the tour? => Thank you for making *time*. || I need your decision on the *tour*. ||", "No pressure at all, but if the board papers could maybe come through at some point? => Thank you for chasing the *auditor*. || The papers are due *Thursday*. ||", "It was amazing, really, everyone was fantastic, honestly, such a wonderful night! => The *second* half was the finest playing of the year. || The *first* half needs work. ||", "I hope you don't mind me saying, and it's probably nothing, but there might be a gap in the draft? => Your draft is *close*. || It has one *gap*, | in the moderation section. ||", "Thanks so much, everyone, you're all incredible, I can't thank you enough. => Thank you for giving up a *Saturday*. || The festival ran because of *you*. ||", "Sorry, I know it's been a long meeting, sorry, could we possibly do one more item? => It's been a *long* meeting. || One more item, | and it's *short*. ||"], "checks": ["Did the warm version contain one specific acknowledgement?", "Was the warm version free of apologies and intensifiers?", "Did the warm version end on a full fall?", "Did the warmth sit in the eyes and onset rather than in a sing-song melody?"]}, {"id": "tone-2-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Attentive and clear", "how": "Acknowledge the person with one specific observation, bridge in a clause, and give your answer with warmth in the body and a firm fall at the end.", "items": ["A long-serving council member tells you this will be her last year on council.", "A newly appointed member of the SCSA panel asks you privately whether she overstepped in the meeting.", "The principal thanks you for the department's results, then raises the budget overrun.", "A festival volunteer of twenty years tells you nobody consulted her about the new venue.", "A university colleague apologises for missing three consecutive committee meetings.", "The president of the parent body tells you families feel unheard about the program changes."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"wpm": [115, 145], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did you open with something specific to that person?", "Did you avoid apologising for anything you had not done?", "Did the closing sentence end on a full fall, with no rise on the meter?", "Did you stay at a peer register, without brightening into a teacher voice?"]}], "apply": {"source": "story", "task": "Read the story, then retell it aloud to an imagined board as a precedent worth their attention. Let the human moments carry audible warmth, tell the facts plainly at committee pace, and close on the lesson in a single sentence with a complete fall.", "seconds": 75, "checks": ["On playback, was there an audible shift in warmth between the human moments and the facts?", "Did you name the people rather than say \"this person\"?", "Did the closing lesson fit in one sentence and end on a full fall?", "Did you stay within a peer register throughout?"]}}, {"id": "tone-3", "title": "Firm and warm together", "goal": "Refuse, rule or deliver bad news in two or three sentences that are generous to the person and immovable on the decision, and hold that position when pressed.", "teach": {"body": "The refusal and the delivery of bad news are the two moments that most reliably expose a speaker's tone, because each tempts the speaker to rescue the relationship at the expense of the decision. The principle is to aim warmth and firmness at different objects. Warmth goes to the person: their effort, their interest, their reasons, their disappointment. Firmness goes to the decision. Cross the wires and you produce either a cold no, firm at the person, or a negotiable no, warm about the decision, which tells everyone at the table that it can be reopened by whoever pushes longest.\n\nWilliam Ury, a co-founder of the Harvard Negotiation Project, describes the well-made refusal as a positive no, in three movements: yes, no, yes. Affirm the interest you share or respect, state the no as a complete sentence, then offer what you can. \"We want moderation to be credible. We won't adopt the common task. We'll pilot cross-marking in Term 2.\" Each movement is short. The middle sentence carries no hedge, no reason fastened to it by \"because\" in the same breath, and no rise.\n\nBad news has its own discipline, borrowed from clinical communication. The SPIKES protocol, developed by Walter Baile, Robert Buckman and colleagues for oncologists, and the warning shot taught alongside it, both put the news early: a brief signal that something difficult is coming, the news in plain words, then silence. The failure every board member has witnessed is the wind-up: context, gratitude, the strength of the field, how close it was. During the wind-up the listener's anxiety climbs, and the delay is read as discomfort with the decision, which invites challenge.\n\nThree verbal mechanics matter. Join with \"and\" rather than \"but\": \"but\" retroactively cancels the acknowledgement, while \"and\" lets acknowledgement and decision stand together. Give one reason, the decisive one. A list of reasons is heard as a defence and offers a list of targets; among academics each will be contested on its merits while the decision itself slips away. Drop \"unfortunately\" and \"I'm afraid\". They cast the speaker as reluctant, which is the opposite of what a decision needs.\n\nProsodically the shape is a descent. The acknowledgement carries the warmth: balanced onset, some movement in pitch, a softened face. The decision clause is the slowest and lowest part of the utterance, narrow in range, with a complete fall. If anything in your voice is going to rise, laugh or trail, it will do so on the decision clause, because that is where the discomfort sits. Rehearse that clause in isolation until it bores you.\n\nThen stop. The silence after a decision belongs to the listener, and filling it is usually for the speaker's relief. More explanation reopens the matter; more comfort sounds like guilt. Wait, and answer what is actually said. If they press, use the broken record, a technique from assertiveness training: restate the decision in the same words, at the same pitch and rate. Verbatim repetition at an even pitch is the clearest signal available that the matter is closed, and in a formal meeting it can be followed by procedure: the member is free to raise it by motion at the next meeting.\n\nThe capable professional's version is close. The acknowledgement is genuine and the decision sound, and the delivery is let down by one reflexive softener in the middle clause, or by a second and third reason offered when the first met silence. The distance between that and the expert version is a handful of words and a willingness to wait.", "model": "Thank you for the *quality* of this application. | It was the most carefully argued in the round, | and the panel *said* so. || It has not been *funded*. || The panel's concern was *sustainability* beyond the second year. || That is addressable, | and I'd encourage you to apply again in *March*. || I'm happy to go through the panel's *notes* with you | whenever *suits*.", "mistake": "Thank you for a really excellent application, it was genuinely one of the strongest, and the panel had a long discussion, and it was very close. Unfortunately, on this occasion, it hasn't quite been funded, partly because of sustainability, and partly the budget detail, and also the number of applications was very high this round.", "remember": ["Warmth to the person, firmness to the decision.", "News by the second sentence.", "Join with \"and\", never \"but\".", "One reason: the decisive one.", "If pressed, repeat it verbatim at the same pitch."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-3-d1", "type": "read", "title": "Yes, no, yes", "how": "Read each refusal aloud. Let the first sentence carry the warmth, make the no the slowest and lowest sentence, and deliver the offer with a complete fall rather than a hopeful lift.", "items": ["I share the committee's concern for *comparability*. || We won't adopt the common *task*. || We'll pilot cross-marking in Term *Two*.", "The proposal is *thoughtful*. || It is not proceeding this *year*. || I'd like to see it at the *planning* day.", "Your daughter's commitment is *obvious*. || She is not in the *senior* ensemble next year. || She is first reserve, | and I'll tell you if that *changes*.", "Thank you for thinking of the *festival*. || We can't accept sponsorship on those *conditions*. || We'd welcome it *without* them.", "I value the department's work on *this*. || We are not funding a second *stage*. || We are funding the *adjudicators*.", "It's a *fair* request. || The answer is *no*. || What I *can* offer is a review in *June*."], "checks": ["Was the no the slowest and lowest sentence of the three?", "Did the first sentence sound attentive rather than merely polite?", "Did the offer end on a fall, with no rise on the meter?", "Did you avoid \"unfortunately\", \"I'm afraid\" and \"but\"?"]}, {"id": "tone-3-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Get to the news", "how": "Say the wound-up version, then the steady one. The steady version delivers the news by its second sentence and is followed by silence.", "items": ["Thank you all for coming, it's been an enormous year, the field was extraordinarily strong, and, well, unfortunately the grant wasn't successful. => I have *disappointing* news. || The grant was not *successful*. ||", "So, the panel deliberated for a long time, and it was very close, but I'm afraid the course wasn't accredited this round. => The course was not *accredited*. || The panel's reasons are in *writing*, | and they are *addressable*. ||", "I've given this a lot of thought, and consulted widely, and it's not easy, but we may need to restructure the ensembles. => The ensembles are being *restructured*. || I'll take you through *why*. ||", "I really hate to be the bearer of this, and it's nobody's fault, but the venue has fallen through. => We have lost the *venue*. || There are *two* alternatives, | and the board needs to choose *tonight*. ||", "You've given so much to this board, and we're all so grateful, but we've decided to go in a different direction with the role. => Thank you for six *years*. || The board has appointed someone *else* to the role. ||", "It's not ideal, I know, and I'm sorry, but the moderation meeting might have to move? => The moderation meeting moves to the *ninth*. ||"], "checks": ["Was the news in the first or second sentence?", "Did the steady version use fewer than half the words of the wind-up?", "Did you leave at least two seconds of silence after the news?", "Did your voice stay level rather than sorry-sounding on the news itself?"]}, {"id": "tone-3-d3", "type": "repeat", "title": "Turn the dial", "how": "Say each line three times: too soft (rising, apologetic), too hard (clipped, cold, hard onsets), then generous and immovable. The third is the target.", "items": ["I know how much *work* went into this. || The submission is not *proceeding*.", "That's a *generous* offer. || We won't be *accepting* it.", "I *hear* the objection. || The ruling *stands*.", "You've championed this for *years*. || It is coming out of the strategic *plan*.", "It clearly *matters* to you. || We are not reopening the *vote*.", "Thank you for your *candour*. || I still need the report by *Friday*."], "checks": ["On playback, were the three takes clearly distinct?", "Did the third take keep warmth in the first sentence?", "Did the third take's decision sound unrevisable, with no rise on the meter?", "Did the third take avoid both the apologetic lift and the clipped onset?"]}, {"id": "tone-3-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "The no, said generously", "how": "Acknowledge the person, bridge in one clause, and give the no or the news plainly with one reason. Then stop and hold the silence.", "items": ["The deputy principal asks to withdraw six of your senior students from a dress rehearsal for an interschool carnival.", "Tell the school council the capital works bid for the music wing was unsuccessful.", "Tell a senior colleague on the SCSA committee that her proposed descriptor will not be in the draft.", "An internationally known artist's agent asks the festival board for a fee it cannot meet.", "Tell a parent who chairs the parent body that her son is moving to the second ensemble.", "Tell your department the interstate tour will not run next year."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"wpm": [110, 140], "fillers": 1, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did the no or the news arrive within two sentences?", "Did you join clauses with \"and\" rather than \"but\"?", "Did you give one reason only?", "Did you stop and hold silence rather than add comfort?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Answer the scenario aloud as if the person were across the table. Then assume they push back once, with force, and answer again by restating the decision in the same words, at the same pitch and rate.", "seconds": 75, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Was the decision stated plainly, without a hedge, in the first answer?", "Was there one specific acknowledgement of the person?", "Was the restatement verbatim, rather than a fresh justification?", "Did the decision sentence sit lowest in pitch both times?"]}}, {"id": "tone-4", "title": "Matching and leading rooms", "goal": "Converge briefly on a room's state and then lead it, one vocal parameter at a time, to the state the meeting needs.", "teach": {"body": "A room has a state before you speak: a pace, a volume, a temperature, a direction of attention. Two bodies of research explain why you can change it and why you cannot simply override it. Elaine Hatfield, John Cacioppo and Richard Rapson's work on emotional contagion describes how people automatically mimic and synchronise the expressions, vocalisations and postures of those around them, and come to feel something of what they mimic. Howard Giles's communication accommodation theory describes convergence and divergence: speakers adjust rate, pitch, loudness and register towards those they wish to align with, and away from those they wish to distance. Convergence is heard as recognition. Abrupt divergence is heard as dismissal.\n\nThe practical consequence is a two-phase technique that coaches call pacing and leading. Pace: for the first few sentences, converge on the room's intensity and seriousness, though never on its panic, hostility or lethargy. Lead: then move your own rate, pitch and volume in small, discrete steps, and let the room converge on you. You do this every night on the bandstand. The first tune is played to the room as it is, and only then taken somewhere.\n\nThe anxious stakeholder: a parent body the evening after a restructuring announcement, a colleague whose program is under review. They arrive fast and high. Brightness is divergence and reads as dismissive; matching their agitation confirms it. Converge on their seriousness at a rate a little below theirs, and name the state. Matthew Lieberman and colleagues found that putting a feeling into words, which they call affect labelling, dampens the amygdala response associated with it. \"There is real concern in this room about continuity\" is not a concession; it is a regulator. Then slow further, lower the pitch, give one fact that alters the picture, and give structure.\n\nThe flat room: the fourth hour of a curriculum committee, or a conference session after lunch. Matching flatness means one brief, dry acknowledgement, not a lifeless delivery. Lead with contrast, because attention responds to change rather than to intensity: a sudden drop in volume, a much shorter sentence, a direct question to a named member, a page reference that makes everyone move.\n\nThe tense board. Converge on gravity: quieter, fewer words, no levity. Name what is at stake in a single sentence. Then lead with procedure. Much of the tension in formal meetings is uncertainty about process, and a chair who sets out the order (\"we'll hear both positions, each without interruption, then move to the motion\") reduces it more effectively than any reassurance.\n\nUnder pressure two failures dominate. Mismatching: arriving in your own state, often the brisk efficiency of the previous meeting, and expecting the room to adopt it. Over-matching: staying in the room's state too long, so that you find yourself speaking faster with the agitated or raising your volume over a heated exchange. The match should last sentences, not minutes. The lead should be gradual, a single step at a time, with a check after each.\n\nThe method, then. Before you speak, read three things: the rate of whoever spoke last, the overall volume, and where the eyes are. Open just inside that range. After two or three sentences, change one parameter, usually rate, and watch for the room's bodies to settle with you: shoulders dropping, pens down, eyes coming up. If they have not followed, hold the new level for longer rather than pushing further. A lead that outruns the room is simply another mismatch.", "model": "There is *real* concern in this room | about what the restructure means for *continuity*. || That concern is *reasonable*. || Here is what is *changing*. | The ensembles are being *regrouped*, | and every student keeps a place at their current *level*. || Here is what is *not* changing. | The *staff*, | the rehearsal *hours*, | and the *standard*. || I'll take questions in a *moment*. || First, | the *timeline*.", "mistake": "I know there's been concern, and I understand it, and I'd like to explain the background properly, because the restructure came out of a review that started last year, which looked at ensemble sizes, and staffing, and the timetable, and once you see how those fit together I think the regrouping makes sense, so let me take you through it from the start.", "remember": ["Converge for sentences, not minutes.", "Meet the seriousness, never the panic.", "Name the state before the facts.", "Lead with one parameter at a time.", "If the room hasn't followed, hold."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-4-d1", "type": "repeat", "title": "Three rooms, one line", "how": "Say each line three times: to an anxious parent body, to a flat committee in its fourth hour, and to a tense board. Change rate, volume and pitch to converge on each room's starting state.", "items": ["Here is what happens *next*.", "I have *good* news, | and *less* good news.", "We have *twenty* minutes. || Let's use them *well*.", "The position has *changed* since the papers went out.", "Let's return to the *motion*.", "Thank you for your *patience*."], "checks": ["On playback, were the three takes clearly distinct in rate and volume?", "Was the anxious-room take slower and lower than your neutral voice?", "Did the flat-room take use contrast rather than mere loudness?", "Did the board take sound grave without sounding tense?"]}, {"id": "tone-4-d2", "type": "pace", "title": "Settle the room", "how": "Read each passage as if to a tense room. Open slightly brisk and descend into the target range by the second sentence, lowering the pitch as you go.", "items": ["I'm aware there is strong *feeling* on this item. || We'll take it in *three* parts. | Each speaker will be *heard*, | and we will *vote* at the end.", "Members are *concerned* about the budget. || So let's look at the *actual* figures. | They are less alarming than the *corridor* version.", "This has been a *difficult* fortnight for the association. || Before we discuss anything, | here is the *order* of business.", "I know the announcement came as a *surprise*. || I'll tell you what was *decided*, | *why*, | and then I'll take *questions*.", "Nothing is being decided *tonight*. || Tonight the board *listens*. || Decisions come in *March*.", "The tension is *understandable*. || Here is what we *know*, | here is what we *don't*, | and here is when we *will*."], "targets": {"wpm": [105, 135], "fillers": 0, "pauses": 3}, "checks": ["Did your rate settle inside the target range by the second sentence?", "Did your pitch end lower at the close of each passage than at its start?", "Did you take every pause the markup asked for?", "Did the meter show at least three pauses per passage?"]}, {"id": "tone-4-d3", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Meet them, then move them", "how": "Converge on the room's state in your first sentence, bridge, then lead it to a specific next step by changing one vocal parameter at a time.", "items": ["Open a parent forum the evening after an email announcing changes to the specialist program.", "A curriculum committee is in its fourth hour and members have stopped reading the papers.", "Two board members have just raised their voices at each other over the festival budget.", "Address a staff meeting in week ten, after a difficult round of reports.", "Restart a conference session after a technical failure has cost fifteen minutes.", "The principal arrives at your meeting visibly agitated about a complaint from a parent."], "seconds": 45, "prep": 15, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did your first sentence show the room you had read its state?", "Did you change rate or pitch audibly after the first two sentences?", "Did you lead to one specific next step?", "Did you leave the room's state within two sentences rather than staying in it?"]}, {"id": "tone-4-d4", "type": "recall", "title": "Turn the room", "how": "Study the anchors, then speak the talk in order without looking. Converge on the room in the first anchor and lead steadily through the rest.", "items": ["Name the concern / Why it is reasonable / One fact that changes the picture / The next step / What you need from them", "The decision was hard / The concern is fairness / How places were allocated / The review process / Where to raise it", "It is late / Ten minutes, not forty / One decision only / Then we close / Thank you for staying", "This board is divided / Both positions want the same outcome / The choice in one sentence / The vote follows / Anyone not yet heard", "The panel is sceptical / What the evidence shows / What it does not show / What we are asking for / What we will report back"], "seconds": 60, "prep": 20, "targets": {"wpm": [110, 145], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Did you deliver every anchor, in order?", "Did the first anchor converge on the room's state?", "Was your voice slower and lower by the last anchor than the first?", "Did you finish on a full fall, with no trailing \"so\" or \"anyway\"?"]}], "apply": {"source": "direction", "task": "Deliver the direction to three imagined rooms in turn: an anxious parent body, a flat committee late in the day, and a divided board. Converge on each for no more than two sentences, then lead into the identical direction, so that only the opening differs.", "seconds": 60, "checks": ["Did each version open differently in rate and volume?", "Was the direction itself word for word the same in all three?", "Did the convergence last two sentences or fewer each time?", "Did you change one parameter at a time while leading?"]}}, {"id": "tone-5", "title": "Steady under fire", "goal": "Take a hostile question, an interruption or a public contradiction in a formal meeting and answer it at an even pitch, rate and volume, with a fact and without repeating the loaded frame.", "teach": {"body": "A public challenge triggers the sympathetic stress response before the prefrontal cortex has framed a reply. The breath rises into the upper chest, the extrinsic laryngeal muscles tighten and lift the larynx, fundamental frequency rises, rate quickens, and gesture becomes faster and smaller. Every one of these is legible to a room, and an articulate room reads them as evidence that the challenge has found something. Steadiness is largely a matter of the first two seconds, before any words.\n\nThe lever is the exhale. Heart rate rises slightly on inhalation and falls on exhalation, a normal rhythm called respiratory sinus arrhythmia, so a slow, quiet out-breath through the nose is the one physiological intervention available to you at the table without anyone noticing. It also produces the pause, which has its own value: an answer that arrives after a beat sounds considered, while one that arrives on the tail of the question sounds defensive. Breathe low, so the lower ribs widen rather than the shoulders lift, and begin speaking on the exhale, near the floor of your range.\n\nAnswer the question, not its tone. A hostile question usually contains a legitimate question inside a loaded frame. Extract the legitimate one and answer that, and if you restate it, restate it neutrally: \"the question is how the reserve was used\", not \"you're asking why I squandered the reserve\". Never repeat the loaded term, even to deny it. George Lakoff's work on framing makes the point that negating a frame activates it, and Nixon's \"I am not a crook\" remains the canonical demonstration.\n\nAcknowledge, bridge, answer gives the mind a rail when it is racing. Acknowledge in three or four words (\"a fair question\"; \"that deserves an answer\"). Bridge in a clause (\"here is what happened\"). Answer with a fact, a document or a date. One fact is stronger than three justifications; a defence with many parts sounds like a defence.\n\nInterruption has its own handling. If your point matters and you are mid-sentence, complete the sentence at the same volume, then hand over explicitly: \"let me finish this, then you have the floor\". Do not get louder. Get slower and slightly lower; a voice that descends while another rises over it is heard as the one in control. In the chair you also have procedure, which is steadier than argument: members speak through the chair, one at a time, and a matter not on the agenda can be taken in general business or deferred. Australian meeting practice, as set out in Joske and in Renton, gives the chair that authority. You rarely need to cite it, only to use it evenly.\n\nPublic contradiction calls for a single correction, with a source, and then movement: \"that's not my understanding; the March minutes record the resolution\". Arguing the point a second time converts a correction into a contest, and contests among clever people tend to be won by whoever appears to care least.\n\nThe failures under pressure are consistent. Matching the challenger's heat. Over-defending. The nervous laugh, which sounds like either contempt or fear. Rising pitch on the answer, which turns a fact into a plea. Speed. And the less obvious failure, which very capable people favour: going cold and clipped, which the room hears as wounded.\n\nThe target is the voice in which you would tell someone the time: level, unhurried, complete, and finished when the answer is.", "model": "A *fair* question, | and it deserves a *full* answer. || The reserve was drawn down in *June*, | to cover the *venue* shortfall. || The board resolved it on the *fourteenth*, | and the resolution is in the *minutes*. || The audited accounts show it line by *line*. || I'm happy to take you, | or any member, | through them after the *meeting*. || The next item is the *program*.", "mistake": "Well, I'd dispute that it was squandered, actually, because if you look at the minutes, which were circulated, the board made a very considered decision, and there were good reasons for it, the venue, the timing, the sponsorship, and I think it's a little unfair to suggest otherwise.", "remember": ["Exhale, then answer.", "Answer the question, not the tone.", "Never repeat the loaded word.", "Under interruption, slower and lower, never louder.", "Correct once, cite the source, move on."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-5-d1", "type": "breath", "title": "Exhale first", "how": "Follow each instruction for several cycles at the pattern shown. The exhale is longer than the inhale and the shoulders do not move.", "items": ["Breathe in through the nose, low, so the lower ribs widen at the sides. Out through the nose, slow and silent.", "Rest a hand on the upper chest. It should stay still while the ribs move.", "On each out-breath, release the jaw and let the tongue lie flat.", "Imagine a council member has just accused you of misleading the meeting. Take one full cycle before any word.", "On the final out-breath, say \"A fair question\" near the bottom of the breath, low and level."], "pattern": [4, 1, 6, 1], "checks": ["Did the hand on the upper chest stay still?", "Was the exhale audibly longer than the inhale?", "Was the final line lower in pitch than your usual speaking voice?", "Did the jaw stay released through the imagined accusation?"]}, {"id": "tone-5-d2", "type": "hold", "title": "The considered beat", "how": "Treat each line as your reply to a challenge in a formal meeting. Exhale, deliver it level with a complete fall, then hold without adding to it.", "items": ["That's not my *understanding*.", "Let me *finish* the point, | then you have the *floor*.", "The minutes record it *differently*.", "Through the *chair*, | please.", "We'll take that in general *business*.", "I've answered that. || The next *question*."], "hold": 3, "checks": ["Did you hold the full three seconds without filling it?", "Did each line stay at conversational volume?", "Did the meter register no end-of-phrase rise?", "Did your face stay neutral, neither tight nor smiling?"]}, {"id": "tone-5-d3", "type": "swap", "title": "Rattled to level", "how": "Say the rattled reply as it might come out under pressure, then the level one, slower and lower. Notice which loaded words disappear.", "items": ["No, the reserve was not squandered, nothing was squandered! => The reserve covered the venue *shortfall*. || It's in the *minutes*. ||", "Sorry, if I could just, sorry, could I finish, please? => Let me *finish*, | then you have the *floor*. ||", "That's simply wrong, I said nothing of the kind, it's a complete misrepresentation. => That isn't what I *said*. || I said the review would begin in *June*. ||", "Look, I'm really not trying to advantage my own program here, honestly. => The allocation follows the published *criteria*. || They are in the *appendix*. ||", "Ha, okay, I'm not sure why this has become personal, but look. => I can see there's *frustration*. || Here is what *happened*. ||", "You can't just raise that now, it's not even on the agenda! => That isn't on the *agenda*. || We'll take it in general *business*. ||"], "checks": ["Did the level reply avoid every loaded word in the rattled one?", "Was the level reply audibly slower and lower?", "Did the level reply contain a fact, a document or a procedure?", "Did you deliver it without a laugh or a sigh?"]}, {"id": "tone-5-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Under fire", "how": "Read the challenge, exhale, then answer with acknowledge, bridge, answer. Level pitch, even rate, one fact, and the loaded frame never repeated.", "items": ["\"Why does your program receive a disproportionate share of the arts budget?\"", "\"Isn't it the case that the festival ran at a loss while you were treasurer?\"", "\"You're a practitioner, not an assessment specialist. Why should this committee defer to you on the descriptors?\"", "\"That isn't what you told council in March.\" (said across you, mid-sentence)", "\"Parents are saying the audition process favours families who can pay for private tuition. How do you answer that?\"", "\"Honestly, why should the school keep funding a program this expensive?\""], "seconds": 40, "prep": 5, "structure": "ABA", "targets": {"wpm": [110, 140], "fillers": 1, "pauses": 2}, "checks": ["Did you take a full exhale before your first word?", "Did you avoid repeating any word from the hostile framing?", "Did your volume stay at conversational level throughout?", "Did you answer with at least one fact, document or date?", "Did you stop once the answer was given?"]}], "apply": {"source": "scenario", "task": "Treat the scenario as a hostile question put to you before a full board. Exhale and answer it aloud; then, without stopping the recording, answer an imagined interruption that repeats the loaded word, keeping pitch, rate and volume unchanged.", "seconds": 75, "structure": "ABA", "checks": ["Did you pause on an exhale before each answer?", "Did your pitch stay level across both answers, with no rise on the meter?", "Did you answer the legitimate question inside the hostile one?", "Did you decline the loaded word both times?"]}}, {"id": "tone-6", "title": "Humour and lightness", "goal": "Judge when a formal room will take lightness, choose a safe target, and deliver one dry line so the room finds it without being pushed, then return to a plain register.", "teach": {"body": "Lightness signals ease, and ease is read as security. A room that laughs together has, for a moment, shared a state, and is for some minutes afterwards more generous to the speaker. Robert Provine's observational studies of laughter found that most laughter in conversation follows remarks that are not jokes at all, and that speakers laugh more than their listeners: laughter is primarily social, a signal of shared footing. For a senior speaker, then, humour is less a matter of wit than of target and timing, and the cost of a miss exceeds the benefit of a hit, because a misfire in a formal room leaves a silence the speaker must then carry.\n\nRod Martin's research on humour styles gives the most useful map of target. Affiliative humour, which draws the room into a shared observation, and self-enhancing humour, which finds the comic side of one's own circumstances, are associated with good relationships. Aggressive humour, at another's expense, and self-defeating humour, which buys approval through self-diminishment, are associated with poor ones. The rule follows directly. Aim at the situation, the institution, the process, the weather, or yourself in a small dose. Never aim at anyone present, and above all not at anyone with less power than you, which on a committee includes the most junior member and the minute-taker.\n\nSelf-deprecation from the chair is an asset in a single dose: it tells the room you do not need its reassurance. Repeated, it becomes self-defeating and dismantles the authority built in the first unit. In academic and policy rooms it carries a further risk: a self-deprecating remark about your own expertise will be remembered, and may be quoted back to you.\n\nThe dry register suits Australian rooms and suits you. Its device is meiosis, deliberate understatement, and its near relation litotes, affirmation through a negated opposite (\"not without its challenges\"). It is delivered deadpan: no smile, no lift in pitch, the same terminal fall as a statement of fact. The room has to notice, and the noticing is the pleasure. Signal the joke and you have explained it.\n\nTiming is mechanical and learnable. Put the payoff word last, the principle of end-weight, so the laugh arrives as the sentence ends rather than across your next clause. Place a short beat before the payoff word. Then stop, and stay still. Do not laugh first, do not explain, do not add a second line to rescue the first. If it misses, continue at the same rate and pitch, so that it becomes, retrospectively, a statement. A callback, a return to something said earlier in the session, outperforms fresh material because the room is already inside it; at a long ceremony or over two days of a conference it is the most reliable laugh available.\n\nJudgement matters more than delivery. Lightness helps at openings, at transitions, at the moment after tension has visibly broken, and between tunes. It harms immediately before bad news, which makes the news sound dismissed; in grief, unless the bereaved have set the tone themselves; in reply to a hostile question, where it is heard as sarcasm; and as an in-joke, which divides the room into those who know and those reminded that they do not. On a ceremonial occasion the test is simple: would the most senior person present be comfortable repeating the line?\n\nThe capable professional's version usually has the right target and an unnecessary second sentence.", "model": "Thank you, Chair. || I was asked to speak for *ten* minutes, | which for a bass player is an unfamiliar degree of *exposure*. || I'll try to use it *responsibly*. || The committee has spent eighteen months on this syllabus, | and it shows in the one place that *matters*. || The assessment now asks students to *make* music, | not merely to *describe* it. || That is the achievement. || The rest is *formatting*.", "mistake": "Thank you, Chair. I was asked to speak for ten minutes, which for a bass player is an unfamiliar degree of exposure. We're more used to being felt than heard, so this is a novelty. I'll try to use it responsibly. The committee has spent eighteen months on this syllabus, and it shows.", "remember": ["Payoff word last, a beat before it, then stillness.", "Deliver it flat; let the room find it.", "Aim at the situation or yourself, never anyone present.", "One self-deprecating line per occasion.", "If it misses, continue at the same pace."]}, "drills": [{"id": "tone-6-d1", "type": "read", "title": "The dry line", "how": "Read each line deadpan: no smile, no pitch lift, a short beat before the payoff, and a complete fall as if stating a fact.", "items": ["The committee has met *eleven* times on this descriptor. || It is now *nearly* a sentence.", "The forecast for tonight's outdoor ceremony | is *mixed*.", "There has been a *small* change to the program. || *Most* of it.", "The treasurer's report is short, | which is the *best* kind.", "The working group reached consensus in *April*. || We had *intended* February.", "Welcome to the Annual General Meeting, | the high point of the association's *social* calendar.", "The new reporting platform is *intuitive*, | according to its *vendor*."], "checks": ["Did your face stay neutral through the payoff?", "Did the payoff word come last and fall, with no rise on the meter?", "Did you place a short beat just before the payoff?", "Did you resist smiling or laughing afterwards?"]}, {"id": "tone-6-d2", "type": "swap", "title": "Joke surgery", "how": "Say the overworked version, then the trimmed one. The trimmed version is shorter, ends on the payoff word, and is never explained.", "items": ["Ha, so this syllabus, it's been, like, quite a journey, let's say, with all the meetings and drafts and everything! => It has been a long road to this *draft*. || Mostly *uphill*. ||", "I'm hopeless with technology, honestly, I'm a total dinosaur, so if this doesn't work, sorry in advance! => If the slides work, | it is not my *doing*. ||", "So the budget, ha, I'll just say it's not amazing, it's a bit of a disaster really, ha! => The budget is *interesting*. || Treasurers choose that word *carefully*. ||", "This next piece is in seven, so if we fall apart, haha, sorry in advance! => The next one is in *seven*. || We've counted *most* of it. ||", "Thanks for coming, I know it's freezing, I'm literally dying out here, ha! => Thank you for braving the *weather*. || The heaters are reserved for the *band*. ||", "Sorry, I do tend to go on, I'm a terrible rambler, everyone says so, anyway! => I'll be *brief*. || *Relatively*. ||"], "checks": ["Was the trimmed version half the length or less?", "Did it end on the payoff word?", "Did you deliver it without a laugh in the voice?", "Was the target the situation or yourself, never someone present?"]}, {"id": "tone-6-d3", "type": "hold", "title": "Wait for it", "how": "Deliver each line dry, then hold for the laugh you imagine. Do not fill the silence, nod, or step on it.", "items": ["Thank you. || We've been *the band*.", "We're running *slightly* late. || By festival standards, | we're *early*.", "Item four is the revised *uniform* policy. || I sense the *anticipation*.", "The next piece contains a great many *notes*. || We'll play a *selection*.", "The raffle prize is a *keyboard*. || Please take it *home*.", "That concludes the formal *proceedings*. || The informal ones have *catering*."], "hold": 2, "checks": ["Did you hold the full count without filling it?", "Did you stay still and neutral while holding?", "Did the line end on a fall rather than a lift?", "Did you resume at your normal rate after the hold?"]}, {"id": "tone-6-d4", "type": "impromptu", "title": "Open light, then commit", "how": "Open with one light line aimed at the situation or yourself, then make your single real point in a plain register with a complete fall. Stop after the point.", "items": ["Open an SCSA consultation forum that is starting forty minutes late.", "Open the festival's awards ceremony on a night of heavy rain.", "Introduce the next tune after the pianist's chart has blown off the stand.", "Begin a council meeting whose agenda runs to fourteen items.", "Open a conference presentation when the projector has failed.", "Close a retirement function for a colleague of thirty years, after a long program of speeches."], "seconds": 40, "prep": 20, "structure": "ONE", "targets": {"wpm": [115, 150], "fillers": 1}, "checks": ["Was there exactly one light line?", "Was it aimed at the situation or yourself?", "Did you shift audibly into a plain, falling register for the real point?", "Did you stop without explaining the line or adding a second one?"]}], "apply": {"source": "passage", "task": "Read the passage aloud as written. Find the one point where a single dry line would serve the room, compose it, and deliver the whole passage again with that line deadpan, payoff last and followed by a held beat. Then judge on playback whether the line earned its place, and say briefly why.", "seconds": 90, "checks": ["Did the added line target the situation or yourself?", "Did you deliver it flat, with the payoff word last and a beat after it?", "Did the rest of the passage keep its complete falls?", "Was it one line, not two?"]}}]}];
